It's a sort of hollow chocolate egg,

01:11:31.320 --> 01:11:33.455
usually comes with a
couple of bags of sweets

01:11:33.455 --> 01:11:35.291
of your choosing, in this case, M&amp;M's.

01:11:35.291 --> 01:11:36.759
They're given to kids around Easter time

01:11:36.759 --> 01:11:37.760
to celebrate the holiday.

01:11:37.760 --> 01:11:39.128
Now, for M&amp;M's, these are available

01:11:39.128 --> 01:11:41.130
as either brownie or crispy options.

01:11:41.130 --> 01:11:42.731
I actually don't know if
Americans do Easter eggs.

01:11:42.731 --> 01:11:44.066
Joe, is that a thing in the States?

01:11:44.066 --> 01:11:47.036
Easter eggs, Easter
candy, Easter chocolate,

01:11:47.036 --> 01:11:49.271
Easter baskets, Easter bunny,

01:11:49.271 --> 01:11:52.141
all that stuff is very
popular in America as well.

01:11:52.141 --> 01:11:53.742
We can also get a bunch of nonstandard

01:11:53.742 --> 01:11:55.044
M&amp;M's stuff here in the US.

01:11:55.044 --> 01:11:59.148
For instance, we have M&amp;M
cookie ice cream sandwiches.

01:11:59.148 --> 01:12:01.317
Weird, these boxes are
open. That's so weird.

01:12:01.317 --> 01:12:02.718
It says four, but
there's only two in here.

01:12:02.718 --> 01:12:03.719
I don't know what happened.

01:12:03.719 --> 01:12:07.656
You can get them in
mint, classic chocolate,

01:12:07.656 --> 01:12:10.025
and vanilla.

01:12:10.025 --> 01:12:11.827
There's also a chocolate one

01:12:11.827 --> 01:12:13.462
with ice cream that's also chocolate.

01:12:13.462 --> 01:12:15.364
That is not available in my area.

01:12:15.364 --> 01:12:17.733
See that? And then they got
the ice cream right there.

01:12:17.733 --> 01:12:19.034
We also have something called

01:12:19.034 --> 01:12:22.971
the Valentine's Day M&amp;M popcorn bucket.

01:12:22.971 --> 01:12:27.076
One, two, three packets of popcorn,

01:12:27.076 --> 01:12:28.477
two boxes,

01:12:28.477 --> 01:12:31.146
one of the milk chocolate
and one of the peanut.

01:12:31.146 --> 01:12:34.450
Also something that I got similar to this,

01:12:34.450 --> 01:12:35.884
and I think it's an exclusive,

01:12:35.884 --> 01:12:40.889
it is milk chocolate
popcorn-flavored M&amp;M's.

01:12:42.391 --> 01:12:45.661
It's weird. Tastes like
popcorn and chocolate.

01:12:45.661 --> 01:12:46.995
Now, in preparation for this episode,

01:12:46.995 --> 01:12:48.697
I did something which no true Londoner

01:12:48.697 --> 01:12:49.631
should ever have to do,

01:12:49.631 --> 01:12:51.867
which is visit M&amp;M's
World in Leicester Square.

01:12:51.867 --> 01:12:53.035
Now, according to signs in the shop,

01:12:53.035 --> 01:12:54.670
it's the largest candy shop in Europe

01:12:54.670 --> 01:12:56.739
and also has the world's
largest candy wall.

01:12:56.739 --> 01:12:58.841
I'm gonna be honest, I
absolutely hate M&amp;M's World

01:12:58.841 --> 01:13:00.209
and pretty much everything it stands for.

01:13:00.209 --> 01:13:03.011
It's kind of like walking
into the museum of capitalism.

01:13:03.011 --> 01:13:04.680
It's somehow an even bigger tourist trap

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:05.848
than, like, Buckingham Palace.

01:13:05.848 --> 01:13:07.783
I just don't understand why
people would come to London

01:13:07.783 --> 01:13:09.551
and make a beeline for the M&amp;M's store.

01:13:09.551 --> 01:13:11.453
So, I've not been to the
M&amp;M's store in London,

01:13:11.453 --> 01:13:12.921
but I've definitely been to the one

01:13:12.921 --> 01:13:14.957
in New York City, in Times Square.

01:13:14.957 --> 01:13:18.160
And I mean this as sincerely as possible,

01:13:18.160 --> 01:13:19.828
it's awesome.

01:13:19.828 --> 01:13:20.662
I'd never been in before,

01:13:20.662 --> 01:13:22.030
and I wasn't really sure what to expect.

01:13:22.030 --> 01:13:23.198
And I was disappointed, honestly.

01:13:23.198 --> 01:13:24.199
I was expecting at least

01:13:24.199 --> 01:13:26.201
some kind of innovative M&amp;M flavors,

01:13:26.201 --> 01:13:27.136
but it was just the same flavors

01:13:27.136 --> 01:13:28.404
that you can normally buy in the store.

01:13:28.404 --> 01:13:30.739
It's just kind of different
colors and different packaging.

01:13:30.739 --> 01:13:32.141
The bulk of what they
had on offer in the store

01:13:32.141 --> 01:13:33.709
is actually M&amp;M's merch,

01:13:33.709 --> 01:13:36.445
like the grotesque shirt
that I'm wearing now,

01:13:36.445 --> 01:13:38.213
ironically, for the record.

01:13:38.213 --> 01:13:39.648
I'm just so confused as to who would spend

01:13:39.648 --> 01:13:40.716
their own money on this sort of stuff.

01:13:40.716 --> 01:13:42.551
Like, why are you loving M&amp;M's so much

01:13:42.551 --> 01:13:44.153
that you're going to pay a brand

01:13:44.153 --> 01:13:46.822
to let you advertise for them?

01:13:46.822 --> 01:13:49.291
Harry, you are.

01:13:49.291 --> 01:13:51.026
You know what isn't ironic?

01:13:51.026 --> 01:13:53.462
How much they made on that shirt sale.

01:13:53.462 --> 01:13:55.164
This is what we do. We like things.

01:13:55.164 --> 01:13:58.100
We wear it to show people,
look at the thing I like.

01:13:58.100 --> 01:13:59.334
It's a capitalistic world

01:13:59.334 --> 01:14:01.703
and I'm a cog in the machine, fine.

01:14:01.703 --> 01:14:03.539
I can't escape, so I might
as well be comfortable.

01:14:03.539 --> 01:14:06.375
And that is my input on the matrix.

01:14:06.375 --> 01:14:08.644
Don't unplug me, I'm happy!

01:14:08.644 --> 01:14:10.813
All that said, I did find
one or two M&amp;M's products

01:14:10.813 --> 01:14:12.314
which I couldn't find anywhere else.

01:14:12.314 --> 01:14:14.249
Firstly, there was one
of the M&amp;M chocolate bars

01:14:14.249 --> 01:14:15.784
with a flavor I couldn't
see anywhere else,

01:14:15.784 --> 01:14:17.019
and that's hazelnut.

01:14:17.019 --> 01:14:18.787
Then there were these
two sachets of powder.

01:14:18.787 --> 01:14:20.689
Firstly, there is a cookie mix.

01:14:20.689 --> 01:14:23.058
Apparently you add some
butter and some water to this,

01:14:23.058 --> 01:14:24.927
and then you have some
ready-to-bake M&amp;M cookies.

01:14:24.927 --> 01:14:28.163
And then, finally, we have an
M&amp;M's hot chocolate powder.

01:14:28.163 --> 01:14:28.997
You know what this means:

01:14:28.997 --> 01:14:30.699
We have to summon the mug.

01:14:30.699 --> 01:14:32.801
It's the return of Buchi
and Wumi. Cheers, guys.

01:14:32.801 --> 01:14:34.603
Oh, it's got M&amp;M's in it. Whoa.

01:14:39.308 --> 01:14:40.943
No, that's fine. It's
better than the Oreo one.

01:14:40.943 --> 01:14:42.611
The bar was admittedly really low,

01:14:42.611 --> 01:14:44.079
but given the option, I would, of course,

01:14:44.079 --> 01:14:45.581
make this with milk instead of hot water,

01:14:45.581 --> 01:14:46.648
and I think if you did that,

01:14:46.648 --> 01:14:47.716
it would actually be pretty good.

01:14:47.716 --> 01:14:48.917
Carrying on from the earlier discussion

01:14:48.917 --> 01:14:49.918
about the M&amp;M's stores,

01:14:49.918 --> 01:14:51.386
I do just want to quickly call out M&amp;M's

01:14:51.386 --> 01:14:53.121
for having some really
questionable branding.

01:14:53.121 --> 01:14:54.656
A big recurring theme is that the M&amp;M's

01:14:54.656 --> 01:14:57.292
are really sexualized in all
of the marketing materials.

01:14:57.292 --> 01:14:58.827
There was that TV ad where the M&amp;M

01:14:58.827 --> 01:15:00.529
was kind of in bed with that guy's wife.

01:15:00.529 --> 01:15:02.831
I feel like the green M&amp;M
gets sexualized quite a lot.

01:15:02.831 --> 01:15:04.733
And also, if you go on the
official M&amp;M's website,

01:15:04.733 --> 01:15:06.602
they have some really questionable bios.

01:15:06.602 --> 01:15:09.371
For example, if you go on
the bio of the brown M&amp;M,

01:15:09.371 --> 01:15:12.441
you'll see that she has a
turn-on listed, which is power.

01:15:12.441 --> 01:15:15.410
Her appearance is described
as "sexy, sophisticated,"

01:15:15.410 --> 01:15:18.213
and her best attribute is
her "big, beautiful brain."

01:15:18.213 --> 01:15:20.349
As part of my M&amp;M's World
visit, I did a scan,

01:15:20.349 --> 01:15:22.317
which told me which M&amp;M
I was, and I got yellow.

01:15:22.317 --> 01:15:23.685
So let's take a look at his bio.

01:15:23.685 --> 01:15:27.289
So, apparently my likes are
pretty ladies and fluffy things.

01:15:27.289 --> 01:15:29.725
My appearance is plump,
yellow, and all smiles,

01:15:29.725 --> 01:15:31.360
which, yeah, kind of accurate.

01:15:31.360 --> 01:15:33.228
Although the bad news
is I might be an incel,

01:15:33.228 --> 01:15:36.365
because my shortcomings are
that nice guys finish last.

01:15:36.365 --> 01:15:38.467
The idea of giving
these guys personalities

01:15:38.467 --> 01:15:40.102
to begin with is like, Jesus Christ,

01:15:40.102 --> 01:15:42.104
can't I just eat my candy in peace?

01:15:42.104 --> 01:15:44.706
[rewinding]

01:15:45.841 --> 01:15:47.209
In an unbelievable turn of events,

01:15:47.209 --> 01:15:49.645
the very day after we
filmed most of this episode,

01:15:49.645 --> 01:15:51.780
M&amp;M's announced that they
would be rebranding their M&amp;M's

01:15:51.780 --> 01:15:53.749
to be more representative of the consumer.

01:15:53.749 --> 01:15:54.683
They've backtracked on a lot

01:15:54.683 --> 01:15:56.184
of the sexualization of the M&amp;M's.

01:15:56.184 --> 01:15:58.120
For example, the green M&amp;M has swapped out

01:15:58.120 --> 01:15:59.621
her high heels for sneakers,

01:15:59.621 --> 01:16:01.557
and the brown M&amp;M has had the turn-ons

01:16:01.557 --> 01:16:03.091
removed from her bio on the website.

01:16:03.091 --> 01:16:03.992
This whole thing prompted

01:16:03.992 --> 01:16:06.061
an explosion of memes on the internet.

01:16:06.061 --> 01:16:07.763
Someone went to journalism school

01:16:07.763 --> 01:16:10.566
to write some of these headlines,
and I respect them for it.

01:16:10.566 --> 01:16:11.400
I don't know why anyone ...

01:16:11.400 --> 01:16:14.403
if you had an opinion on
this, for or against it,

01:16:14.403 --> 01:16:16.572
you have entirely too
much time on your hands.

01:16:16.572 --> 01:16:18.307
It's also worth pointing
out that someone suggested

01:16:18.307 --> 01:16:20.642
the M&amp;M's rebrand could
be suspiciously timed

01:16:20.642 --> 01:16:22.244
and that they might be
trying to draw attention

01:16:22.244 --> 01:16:24.212
away from an ongoing class action lawsuit.

01:16:24.212 --> 01:16:25.647
Mars, the owner of M&amp;M's,

01:16:25.647 --> 01:16:27.449
along with several other
major chocolate producers

01:16:27.449 --> 01:16:29.518
including Hershey's, Nestlé, and more,

01:16:29.518 --> 01:16:30.986
have been accused of using cocoa

01:16:30.986 --> 01:16:33.689
from plantations which
use child slave laborers.

01:16:33.689 --> 01:16:35.757
Oh, God.

01:16:35.757 --> 01:16:37.526
Mars told Bloomberg, "We do not comment

01:16:37.526 --> 01:16:39.428
on any possible pending litigation."

01:16:39.428 --> 01:16:40.495
Oh, that's ...

01:16:40.495 --> 01:16:41.797
the fact that it wasn't a blanket,

01:16:41.797 --> 01:16:43.565
"Of course not, what?"

01:16:43.565 --> 01:16:44.866
The fact that they're like,
"Oh, we can't comment on it,"

01:16:44.866 --> 01:16:46.201
it's like, that's a yes.

01:16:46.201 --> 01:16:47.302
The lawsuit is still ongoing,

01:16:47.302 --> 01:16:49.638
so we'll have to wait and see
if any more details emerge.

01:16:49.638 --> 01:16:52.074
Was the timing of the rebrand
coincidental or cynical?

01:16:52.074 --> 01:16:53.408
We'll let you guys decide.

01:16:53.408 --> 01:16:54.710
I like how I just found out about that

01:16:54.710 --> 01:16:56.311
after I ate all these M&amp;M's.

01:16:56.311 --> 01:16:57.346
Well, if it will make
everyone feel better,

01:16:57.346 --> 01:16:59.681
I have a huge headache from
eating all this junk food.

01:17:02.084 --> 01:17:04.252
In the US, the recommended
serving size of M&amp;M's

01:17:04.252 --> 01:17:07.222
is 28 grams, which is roughly 1 ounce.

01:17:07.222 --> 01:17:08.657
So when it comes to classic M&amp;M's,

01:17:08.657 --> 01:17:10.325
the US version has more calories,

01:17:10.325 --> 01:17:13.128
saturated fat, carbs, and sodium.

01:17:13.128 --> 01:17:15.263
The UK M&amp;M's actually
contain more total fat

01:17:15.263 --> 01:17:16.431
as well as more sugar.

01:17:16.431 --> 01:17:17.899
Sugar is the worst nutritional offender

01:17:17.899 --> 01:17:19.301
as far as M&amp;M are concerned.

01:17:19.301 --> 01:17:22.070
28 grams of UK M&amp;M's
contains just over a third

01:17:22.070 --> 01:17:23.538
of your daily sugar allowance.

01:17:23.538 --> 01:17:25.307
What about the least
healthiest M&amp;M in the US?

01:17:25.307 --> 01:17:27.309
Well, for us, there's a two-way tie

01:17:27.309 --> 01:17:31.013
between the peanut butter and
the white chocolate peanut.

01:17:31.013 --> 01:17:33.749
Both of these contain
150 calories per 28 grams

01:17:33.749 --> 01:17:35.851
as well as 8 grams of fat.

01:17:35.851 --> 01:17:38.120
In the UK, our least
healthy M&amp;M is peanut.

01:17:38.120 --> 01:17:40.322
A 28-gram serving of UK peanut M&amp;M's

01:17:40.322 --> 01:17:42.324
will contain 144 calories

01:17:42.324 --> 01:17:45.761
as well as a slightly increased
7.28 grams of total fat.

01:17:46.561 --> 01:17:48.063
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

01:17:48.063 --> 01:17:49.431
we wanted to find out all the differences

01:17:49.431 --> 01:17:51.800
between Pepsi in the UK and the US.

01:17:51.800 --> 01:17:53.435
This is "Food Wars."

01:17:57.539 --> 01:17:59.775
In the UK, Pepsi comes in
a range of portion sizes,

01:17:59.775 --> 01:18:00.876
but these do vary slightly

01:18:00.876 --> 01:18:02.778
depending on which type
of Pepsi you're buying.

01:18:02.778 --> 01:18:03.912
We used to be able to get mini cans,

01:18:03.912 --> 01:18:04.913
but they've been discontinued,

01:18:04.913 --> 01:18:07.749
so now you can get classic
Pepsi in four portion sizes.

01:18:07.749 --> 01:18:10.352
As a 330-milliliter can,

01:18:10.352 --> 01:18:12.354
as a 500-milliliter bottle,

01:18:12.354 --> 01:18:14.122
as a 1.5-liter bottle,

01:18:14.122 --> 01:18:15.991
and as a 2-liter bottle.

01:18:15.991 --> 01:18:19.194
In the US, our Pepsi comes
in these following sizes:

01:18:19.194 --> 01:18:22.564
The mini can is the
smallest. 7.5 fluid ounces.

01:18:22.564 --> 01:18:25.801
Next one up, the classic
can, 12 fluid ounces.

01:18:25.801 --> 01:18:27.469
Then we move up to plastic bottles.

01:18:27.469 --> 01:18:30.005
This one is 16.9 fluid ounces.

01:18:30.005 --> 01:18:32.741
Then we got the 20-fluid-ounce bottle.

01:18:32.741 --> 01:18:33.775
And if that's not enough,

01:18:33.775 --> 01:18:37.612
we go up to 1-liter bottle. Liter of cola.

01:18:37.612 --> 01:18:41.383
Then moving up a little bit
more, 1.25-liter bottle.

01:18:41.383 --> 01:18:45.954
And then our biggest bottle
is the 2-liter bottle.

01:18:45.954 --> 01:18:47.489
We can also get glass bottles,

01:18:47.489 --> 01:18:50.225
but like the classic
can, they are 12 ounces.

01:18:53.662 --> 01:18:55.063
Where regular Pepsi is concerned,

01:18:55.063 --> 01:18:56.932
both countries cap out
at a 2-liter bottle.

01:18:56.932 --> 01:18:59.067
However, in the UK, we
can go one step further

01:18:59.067 --> 01:19:01.002
when it comes to diet and Max Pepsi.

01:19:01.002 --> 01:19:04.272
Certain retailers, such as
Iceland or B&amp;M, stock these.

01:19:06.475 --> 01:19:09.144
A 3-liter bottle of
Pepsi Max or Pepsi diet.

01:19:09.144 --> 01:19:11.413
We finally have something
bigger than America! We did it!

01:19:11.413 --> 01:19:13.115
I don't know if that's
a good or a bad thing,

01:19:13.115 --> 01:19:14.883
probably bad for the nation's health,

01:19:14.883 --> 01:19:17.152
but, you know, nice to
be different for once.

01:19:17.152 --> 01:19:18.587
There are some other
slight size differences

01:19:18.587 --> 01:19:21.022
for the sugar and sugar-free
options in the UK as well.

01:19:21.022 --> 01:19:22.190
For example, in the UK,

01:19:22.190 --> 01:19:25.660
we can get a 1.25-liter bottle
of Pepsi Max or Pepsi diet,

01:19:25.660 --> 01:19:27.729
which we can't get for the classic Pepsi.

01:19:27.729 --> 01:19:30.298
The size differences are largely
down to the UK's sugar tax.

01:19:30.298 --> 01:19:31.933
It's a tax which raised
the price of drinks

01:19:31.933 --> 01:19:34.870
with more than 5 grams of
sugar per 100 milliliters.

01:19:34.870 --> 01:19:36.404
The higher prices reduce the demand

01:19:36.404 --> 01:19:38.140
for the full-sugar
versions of soft drinks,

01:19:38.140 --> 01:19:40.442
making it less viable to sell
them in large containers,

01:19:40.442 --> 01:19:41.777
such as the 3-liter.

01:19:41.777 --> 01:19:43.979
But don't worry, because you
can still get sugary Pepsi

01:19:43.979 --> 01:19:45.180
in bulk quantities.

01:19:45.180 --> 01:19:49.117
Our largest bulk is the
36-can pack of Pepsi,

01:19:49.117 --> 01:19:51.553
and that's the 12-ounce cans.

01:19:51.553 --> 01:19:54.256
That is 432 fluid ounces of soda,

01:19:54.256 --> 01:19:57.759
or 3.375 gallons, of Pepsi.

01:19:57.759 --> 01:20:00.796
Now, that pack of cans is at
least available at Sam's Club,

01:20:00.796 --> 01:20:03.398
possibly Costco, I don't know,
but I went to Sam's Club,

01:20:03.398 --> 01:20:04.933
and unfortunately they
would not let me buy it

01:20:04.933 --> 01:20:05.867
without a membership.

01:20:05.867 --> 01:20:07.903
So, the best I could do are these pictures

01:20:07.903 --> 01:20:09.271
of me with the packs.

01:20:10.305 --> 01:20:11.940
That is much larger
than our largest option,

01:20:11.940 --> 01:20:13.875
which is this, a 24-pack of cans.

01:20:13.875 --> 01:20:14.810
Not only is our multipack

01:20:14.810 --> 01:20:16.711
only two-thirds the size
of the American one,

01:20:16.711 --> 01:20:18.580
but the cans themselves are also smaller.

01:20:18.580 --> 01:20:21.116
A can in the UK contains 330 milliliters,

01:20:21.116 --> 01:20:23.785
while a can in the US contains 355.

01:20:23.785 --> 01:20:25.520
This means that the total for our 24-pack

01:20:25.520 --> 01:20:27.589
comes to 2.09 gallons.

01:20:27.589 --> 01:20:28.890
Even if we had a 36-pack,

01:20:28.890 --> 01:20:30.659
it would still be smaller
than the American version.

01:20:30.659 --> 01:20:33.195
We need three extra cans
to make up the difference.

01:20:37.065 --> 01:20:38.867
Here are all the Pepsi
products you'll find in the UK

01:20:38.867 --> 01:20:40.669
that you won't find in the US.

01:20:40.669 --> 01:20:43.605
Here are all the Pepsi
products from the US

01:20:43.605 --> 01:20:45.540
you won't find in the UK.

01:20:45.540 --> 01:20:47.475
We'll start with the
exclusive flavors of Pepsi.

01:20:47.475 --> 01:20:49.778
In the UK, we have Pepsi Max raspberry.

01:20:53.148 --> 01:20:54.316
It's got that really comforting,

01:20:54.316 --> 01:20:57.519
extremely nostalgic artificial
blue-raspberry flavor

01:20:57.519 --> 01:20:58.720
that you're used to.

01:20:58.720 --> 01:21:01.389
On Pepsi's UK website, it
also said they do lime Pepsi,

01:21:01.389 --> 01:21:03.692
but I wasn't able to find that
at any of the stores I tried.

01:21:04.826 --> 01:21:05.660
Whoops.

01:21:05.660 --> 01:21:08.463
We have a few exclusive
Pepsi options of our own.

01:21:08.463 --> 01:21:11.166
Starting down here, we
have Pepsi real sugar,

01:21:11.166 --> 01:21:14.436
which is in fact made with real sugar,

01:21:14.436 --> 01:21:16.972
not the high-fructose-corn-syrup stuff.

01:21:20.876 --> 01:21:22.210
Not bad.

01:21:22.210 --> 01:21:25.981
We also have Pepsi mango
and Pepsi mango zero sugar.

01:21:25.981 --> 01:21:27.749
Seems like a weird fruit choice.

01:21:27.749 --> 01:21:28.683
Whoa, smells good.

01:21:30.752 --> 01:21:32.520
I'm surprised by how good this is.

01:21:32.520 --> 01:21:34.256
And they got the mango zero sugar.

01:21:35.257 --> 01:21:37.359
Ooh, already smells drastically different.

01:21:38.560 --> 01:21:40.395
It's got a real chemically aftertaste.

01:21:40.395 --> 01:21:41.429
Not as good as the regular.

01:21:41.429 --> 01:21:43.665
This one here is

01:21:43.665 --> 01:21:47.736
Pepsi soda shop cream soda.

01:21:47.736 --> 01:21:48.837
Mm.

01:21:50.505 --> 01:21:51.439
Very nice.

01:21:51.439 --> 01:21:53.975
And the last one, Pepsi vanilla.

01:21:56.611 --> 01:21:57.913
I'm gonna be honest with you, Pepsi,

01:21:57.913 --> 01:21:59.714
could use a lot more vanilla.

01:21:59.714 --> 01:22:01.483
It's, like, vanilla-kissed.

01:22:01.483 --> 01:22:02.584
[burps] There it is.

01:22:02.584 --> 01:22:04.452
All right, right now what I want to do

01:22:04.452 --> 01:22:08.290
is compare the already-tasted
real sugar Pepsi

01:22:08.290 --> 01:22:10.859
to the regular Pepsi, which
has high-fructose corn syrup.

01:22:10.859 --> 01:22:11.960
Regular Pepsi.

01:22:13.995 --> 01:22:14.829
[coughs]

01:22:14.829 --> 01:22:17.999
Pepsi's harsher than Coke.

01:22:22.437 --> 01:22:23.538
Yeah.

01:22:23.538 --> 01:22:25.407
About to say something
somewhat controversial,

01:22:25.407 --> 01:22:27.042
but bring it, I want all the smoke.

01:22:27.042 --> 01:22:30.745
Real sugar Pepsi tastes just like Coke.

01:22:31.980 --> 01:22:33.281
It's just smoother.

01:22:34.316 --> 01:22:36.584
This is like ... [burps]

01:22:36.584 --> 01:22:38.153
Yeah, this is, like, harsher.

01:22:38.153 --> 01:22:40.021
Joe was kind enough to
send me some American Pepsi

01:22:40.021 --> 01:22:41.456
as well as some real sugar Pepsi,

01:22:41.456 --> 01:22:42.991
so I'm going to do a three-way taste test

01:22:42.991 --> 01:22:44.326
and see if I can tell the difference.

01:22:47.796 --> 01:22:48.964
Pepsi No. 1.

01:22:51.733 --> 01:22:54.769
That's very, like, strong flavor,

01:22:54.769 --> 01:22:57.005
quite a syrupy texture to it almost.

01:22:57.005 --> 01:22:58.106
Pepsi No. 2.

01:23:01.543 --> 01:23:05.480
Yeah, very sweet, but almost
in a more gentle kind of way.

01:23:05.480 --> 01:23:06.548
And then No. 3.

01:23:09.317 --> 01:23:10.986
More similar in kind of, like,

01:23:10.986 --> 01:23:13.621
I'd say sweetness and
mouthfeel to the middle one.

01:23:13.621 --> 01:23:16.658
This one is the one that stood
out with the strongest taste,

01:23:16.658 --> 01:23:17.625
in my opinion.

01:23:17.625 --> 01:23:20.161
These two were very similar,
which is making me think

01:23:20.161 --> 01:23:23.298
that these are the British
and real sugar Pepsis.

01:23:23.298 --> 01:23:24.866
I'm not sure in which order,

01:23:24.866 --> 01:23:26.768
but they had a more gentle taste,

01:23:26.768 --> 01:23:29.804
whereas this one seemed
harsher and tasted very syrupy.

01:23:29.804 --> 01:23:31.773
So I'm going to guess British,

01:23:32.741 --> 01:23:34.743
American real sugar,

01:23:34.743 --> 01:23:36.144
American normal Pepsi.

01:23:36.978 --> 01:23:38.346
I'm shocked.

01:23:38.346 --> 01:23:40.648
These two tasted really similar to me,

01:23:40.648 --> 01:23:43.151
and this one stood out, like I said,

01:23:43.151 --> 01:23:44.886
and it wasn't particularly pleasant.

01:23:44.886 --> 01:23:46.788
Maybe the UK needs to take
a leaf out of America's book

01:23:46.788 --> 01:23:48.990
and just put high-fructose
corn syrup in everything.

01:23:48.990 --> 01:23:50.625
The other exclusive Pepsi item in the UK

01:23:50.625 --> 01:23:52.627
is this Pepsi SodaStream syrup.

01:23:52.627 --> 01:23:54.562
For the uninitiated, a
SodaStream is a device

01:23:54.562 --> 01:23:56.898
which lets you carbonate
your own drinks at home.

01:23:56.898 --> 01:23:58.533
Now, as a sparkling-water obsessive,

01:23:58.533 --> 01:23:59.934
I actually own one of these.

01:24:01.036 --> 01:24:02.737
So we're going to make our own Pepsi.

01:24:02.737 --> 01:24:03.705
So, what you do

01:24:03.705 --> 01:24:06.374
is you get one of your
little SodaStream bottles,

01:24:06.374 --> 01:24:09.677
fill it to the first line,
and attach it to the machine.

01:24:11.079 --> 01:24:13.214
Then you press this button,
and the magic happens.

01:24:18.353 --> 01:24:19.220
According to this bottle,

01:24:19.220 --> 01:24:21.923
you add about half a cap
of the syrup to this.

01:24:21.923 --> 01:24:23.058
That's quite cool.

01:24:27.962 --> 01:24:29.197
The first thing I get is just Pepsi.

01:24:29.197 --> 01:24:30.799
It tastes a lot like Pepsi Max.

01:24:30.799 --> 01:24:32.801
There is a slightly unpleasant aftertaste,

01:24:32.801 --> 01:24:34.269
which, I'm not sure
where that's come from.

01:24:34.269 --> 01:24:35.937
Maybe I used too much syrup. Who knows.

01:24:35.937 --> 01:24:37.972
But I think, yeah, in terms of recreating

01:24:37.972 --> 01:24:41.109
a fizzy drink at home with
not a lot of equipment,

01:24:41.109 --> 01:24:42.243
it's pretty good.

01:24:42.243 --> 01:24:43.078
And that's pretty much it

01:24:43.078 --> 01:24:45.046
as far as Pepsi exclusives are concerned,

01:24:45.046 --> 01:24:46.414
but we're not stopping there.

01:24:46.414 --> 01:24:49.284
PepsiCo owns more beverage brands,

01:24:49.284 --> 01:24:52.921
but it does differ depending
on which country you live in.

01:24:52.921 --> 01:24:55.457
Now, while in the US,
Keurig Dr Pepper owns 7Up,

01:24:55.457 --> 01:24:57.492
in the UK, it's actually owned by PepsiCo,

01:24:57.492 --> 01:24:58.326
which means that technically

01:24:58.326 --> 01:25:00.161
all of these are Pepsi exclusives.

01:25:00.161 --> 01:25:02.397
British 7Up comes in four forms.

01:25:02.397 --> 01:25:06.801
As classic 7Up, a 7Up
Free, 7Up cherry Free,

01:25:06.801 --> 01:25:08.336
and as a SodaStream syrup.

01:25:17.245 --> 01:25:18.379
That's actually not bad.

01:25:18.379 --> 01:25:19.914
It doesn't have the unpleasant aftertaste

01:25:19.914 --> 01:25:21.149
that the Pepsi one did.

01:25:21.149 --> 01:25:22.851
That to me just tastes like 7Up.

01:25:22.851 --> 01:25:24.018
One other thing that's worth pointing out

01:25:24.018 --> 01:25:26.020
is that in the UK, this
is considered lemonade.

01:25:26.020 --> 01:25:27.589
Like, if you walk into
a pub or a restaurant

01:25:27.589 --> 01:25:29.390
and ask for a glass of lemonade,
this is what you'll get.

01:25:29.390 --> 01:25:31.025
Like, a clear sparkling beverage.

01:25:31.025 --> 01:25:32.894
Is that not the case in America?

01:25:32.894 --> 01:25:34.462
That is definitely not lemonade,

01:25:34.462 --> 01:25:36.564
and it is super weird.

01:25:36.564 --> 01:25:41.002
Lemonade is lemon, not juice, right?

01:25:41.002 --> 01:25:44.539
But you squeeze lemons
in water and mix it up,

01:25:44.539 --> 01:25:47.208
and it's, like, more
refreshing and kind of tart.

01:25:47.208 --> 01:25:49.144
And it's closer to a lemon juice.

01:25:49.144 --> 01:25:50.545
It's definitely not carbonated.

01:25:50.545 --> 01:25:52.313
In front of me we have
something that's brand new,

01:25:52.313 --> 01:25:54.516
and it is called Pepsi Nitro.

01:25:54.516 --> 01:25:56.484
What is it? Wait, excuse me.

01:25:56.484 --> 01:25:58.253
Nitro Pepsi, flip 'em.

01:25:58.253 --> 01:26:02.223
Looks to be a tall boy of Pepsi

01:26:02.223 --> 01:26:05.393
that, according to the can,
is nitrogen-infused cola

01:26:05.393 --> 01:26:08.730
with smaller bubbles and a smoother taste.

01:26:08.730 --> 01:26:10.465
[drink fizzes]

01:26:10.465 --> 01:26:12.934
Did you hear that? Oh, man!

01:26:14.969 --> 01:26:16.604
That's a pretty hard pour, right?

01:26:17.972 --> 01:26:20.808
It's not going crazy like a
normal Pepsi would, right?

01:26:20.808 --> 01:26:21.709
I'm more excited about the vanilla.

01:26:21.709 --> 01:26:24.612
Let's really get really good
audio on this one now. Ready?

01:26:24.612 --> 01:26:27.182
[drink fizzes]

01:26:29.083 --> 01:26:30.118
Hard pour.

01:26:35.990 --> 01:26:37.959
Yeah, it's a little bit smoother.

01:26:37.959 --> 01:26:39.494
It still tastes just like Pepsi.

01:26:39.494 --> 01:26:42.397
Not as carbonated, which is fine.

01:26:42.397 --> 01:26:43.698
Do the vanilla.

01:26:45.233 --> 01:26:48.403
Much better. Ooh, that's nice. Yeah.

01:26:48.403 --> 01:26:49.737
The issue I'm having with it

01:26:49.737 --> 01:26:53.741
is that the only time
soda is that "smooth"

01:26:53.741 --> 01:26:55.476
is when it's flat.

01:26:55.476 --> 01:26:58.479
I'll f--- with it, but it's just OK.

01:26:58.479 --> 01:27:00.381
Pepsi has a few exclusive
brands in the US,

01:27:00.381 --> 01:27:03.685
including Mug Root Beer,
Sierra Mist, and Bubly.

01:27:03.685 --> 01:27:05.453
They're a carbonated-water brand.

01:27:08.556 --> 01:27:10.358
Mug Root Beer.

01:27:10.358 --> 01:27:12.894
I was the master at being able

01:27:12.894 --> 01:27:15.630
to tell different root beers on taste.

01:27:15.630 --> 01:27:17.198
I could tell them apart.

01:27:17.198 --> 01:27:20.001
That information is, like, in my DNA.

01:27:20.001 --> 01:27:22.437
One of these days we'll
do a root-beer lineup,

01:27:22.437 --> 01:27:23.271
a root-beer taste test.

01:27:23.271 --> 01:27:24.706
I guarantee I will ace that test.

01:27:27.075 --> 01:27:28.509
Yeah, it's taking me back.

01:27:30.211 --> 01:27:31.779
Also, they got Sierra Mist.

01:27:31.779 --> 01:27:33.281
I hate Sierra Mist.

01:27:33.281 --> 01:27:34.415
It's a lemon-lime flavor.

01:27:34.415 --> 01:27:36.517
I don't know if they're
competing with Sprite or 7Up.

01:27:36.517 --> 01:27:37.518
Always hated this.

01:27:40.088 --> 01:27:42.290
Sprite is better. 7Up is better.

01:27:42.290 --> 01:27:44.592
Sierra Mist tastes the way

01:27:44.592 --> 01:27:47.695
fruit-flavored shampoo
smells, if that makes sense.

01:27:50.665 --> 01:27:51.933
[burps] OK.

01:27:51.933 --> 01:27:55.770
This'll be my ranking
system, from best to worst.

01:27:55.770 --> 01:27:58.206
So, first we're going to go strawberry.

01:27:58.206 --> 01:27:59.474
No, I do not like that.

01:27:59.474 --> 01:28:00.308
Cherry.

01:28:01.576 --> 01:28:03.378
I think these all might be bad.

01:28:03.378 --> 01:28:05.446
That one's a little better
than the strawberry one.

01:28:06.914 --> 01:28:07.949
Pineapple.

01:28:07.949 --> 01:28:10.051
Nope, woof!

01:28:10.051 --> 01:28:11.886
Grapefruit, ugh.

01:28:11.886 --> 01:28:13.488
Low expectations on this one.

01:28:14.656 --> 01:28:16.924
Oh, my God, that smells like an armpit.

01:28:16.924 --> 01:28:18.860
Oh, man, if I went into a public restroom

01:28:18.860 --> 01:28:21.362
and it smelled like this, I would run out.

01:28:21.362 --> 01:28:22.497
No!

01:28:24.065 --> 01:28:26.100
Oh-ho-ho-ho-ho-ho-ho!

01:28:26.100 --> 01:28:27.268
Yeah, that's the worst one.

01:28:27.268 --> 01:28:30.638
Lime. Can't screw up lime, come on.

01:28:30.638 --> 01:28:32.974
Congrats, lime, you're
the least offensive.

01:28:32.974 --> 01:28:33.808
Peach.

01:28:37.512 --> 01:28:40.048
What the hell happened?
Why are these all so bad?

01:28:41.115 --> 01:28:42.183
Raspberry.

01:28:47.088 --> 01:28:49.123
Oh, man, the smell of that.

01:28:49.123 --> 01:28:50.258
It says natural flavor.

01:28:50.258 --> 01:28:52.026
It does not taste natural at all.

01:28:52.026 --> 01:28:53.928
At all. At all.

01:28:53.928 --> 01:28:55.596
Ah, blackberry.

01:28:55.596 --> 01:28:57.799
OK, that's the best one,
but it's not very good.

01:28:58.633 --> 01:29:00.001
And last and not -- mango.

01:29:00.001 --> 01:29:01.135
OK, so we had the mango,

01:29:01.135 --> 01:29:03.738
so they know how to flavor
things mango properly.

01:29:03.738 --> 01:29:06.140
The mango Pepsi's fantastic.

01:29:06.140 --> 01:29:08.109
I mean, yeah, these two are the best one.

01:29:08.109 --> 01:29:08.943
They're not very good.

01:29:08.943 --> 01:29:10.645
Bubly is not very good.

01:29:10.645 --> 01:29:12.747
How could they screw these up this bad?

01:29:12.747 --> 01:29:15.650
PepsiCo also owns Mountain
Dew in the UK and the US,

01:29:15.650 --> 01:29:17.285
but that is a story for another episode.

01:29:21.422 --> 01:29:24.792
Story time. Anyone ever
remember or heard of New Coke?

01:29:24.792 --> 01:29:26.794
Well, you can thank Pepsi for that.

01:29:26.794 --> 01:29:27.962
Once upon a time,

01:29:27.962 --> 01:29:30.131
Coca-Cola was actually
losing market share,

01:29:30.131 --> 01:29:32.834
and Pepsi put out a
very popular ad campaign

01:29:32.834 --> 01:29:35.770
where people were blind
taste-testing Coke and Pepsi

01:29:35.770 --> 01:29:36.771
and picking Pepsi.

01:29:36.771 --> 01:29:38.272
So Coke panicked,

01:29:38.272 --> 01:29:41.642
and they pushed to market
a beverage called New Coke,

01:29:41.642 --> 01:29:42.643
which is basically Coke

01:29:42.643 --> 01:29:44.379
that was supposed to
taste more like Pepsi.

01:29:44.379 --> 01:29:47.882
Guess what? Huge marketing blunder.

01:29:47.882 --> 01:29:49.951
Not only did Coca-Cola fans get annoyed

01:29:49.951 --> 01:29:51.886
that they changed the formula
of a beverage they liked,

01:29:51.886 --> 01:29:54.555
but all New Coke was doing was
giving Pepsi more attention.

01:29:54.555 --> 01:29:57.859
Either way, just 79 days
after introducing New Coke,

01:29:57.859 --> 01:29:59.394
Coke announced that
they'd be bringing back

01:29:59.394 --> 01:30:02.563
the original drink,
called Coca-Cola Classic.

01:30:02.563 --> 01:30:05.099
And you will maybe notice,
for, like, 20 years,

01:30:05.099 --> 01:30:07.301
Coca-Cola was called Coca-Cola Classic.

01:30:07.301 --> 01:30:09.404
It had "Classic" on there.
Have you ever wondered why?

01:30:09.404 --> 01:30:11.439
That's because they wanted
to make it perfectly clear,

01:30:11.439 --> 01:30:13.274
this is not that New Coke that you hated

01:30:13.274 --> 01:30:15.143
from 1988 or whatever.

01:30:15.143 --> 01:30:17.111
So, Pepsi, they had their day in the sun,

01:30:17.111 --> 01:30:18.679
but to be honest, I mean,

01:30:18.679 --> 01:30:20.481
Coke is clearly the more dominant brand.

01:30:20.481 --> 01:30:23.618
Speaking of product failures,
remember Crystal Pepsi?

01:30:23.618 --> 01:30:24.685
That was widely considered

01:30:24.685 --> 01:30:26.788
to be one of the biggest
product failures ever.

01:30:26.788 --> 01:30:29.924
Time magazine put Crystal Pepsi at No. 3

01:30:29.924 --> 01:30:32.226
on the biggest-fails list.

01:30:32.226 --> 01:30:35.096
Thrillist called it "Soda
World's Greatest Fail."

01:30:35.096 --> 01:30:36.397
But why?

01:30:36.397 --> 01:30:38.533
Soda sales were stagnant
in the early '90s,

01:30:38.533 --> 01:30:40.101
with consumers instead opting

01:30:40.101 --> 01:30:42.470
for clear and caffeine-free sodas.

01:30:42.470 --> 01:30:44.939
Former Pepsi marketing exec David Novak

01:30:44.939 --> 01:30:46.808
pitched a drink that was both,

01:30:46.808 --> 01:30:48.810
and Crystal Pepsi was born.

01:30:48.810 --> 01:30:52.013
The recipe was developed by
food scientist Surinder Kumar,

01:30:52.013 --> 01:30:53.614
and Pepsi ended up with a clear drink

01:30:53.614 --> 01:30:56.717
that maintained an almost
identical taste to Classic Pepsi.

01:30:56.717 --> 01:31:00.087
They rushed it out in time
for the 1993 Super Bowl.

01:31:00.087 --> 01:31:03.057
It was backed by a $40
million ad campaign,

01:31:03.057 --> 01:31:05.660
and they did sell about half a
billion dollars' worth of it.

01:31:05.660 --> 01:31:08.896
But by March of '93,
the hype quickly faded.

01:31:08.896 --> 01:31:10.832
Consumers did not love the new flavor,

01:31:10.832 --> 01:31:12.867
and the concept was even parodied by "SNL"

01:31:12.867 --> 01:31:14.836
in a "crystal gravy" sketch.

01:31:14.836 --> 01:31:15.837
Hilarious.

01:31:15.837 --> 01:31:17.138
Roughly one year after launch,

01:31:17.138 --> 01:31:18.539
Crystal Pepsi was discontinued.

01:31:18.539 --> 01:31:20.408
But if you never got the chance to try it,

01:31:20.408 --> 01:31:21.542
there's still hope for you.

01:31:21.542 --> 01:31:23.978
Pepsi's bringing Crystal
Pepsi back for a limited time

01:31:23.978 --> 01:31:25.847
to celebrate its 30th anniversary.

01:31:25.847 --> 01:31:28.182
You can't buy it in stores,
but you can enter a contest

01:31:28.182 --> 01:31:30.184
by sending them a picture of yourself

01:31:30.184 --> 01:31:31.953
from the '90s on Twitter.

01:31:31.953 --> 01:31:33.221
Here is my entry.

01:31:34.322 --> 01:31:35.423
I've got a story of my own.

01:31:35.423 --> 01:31:37.158
Remember that time when
Pepsi briefly became

01:31:37.158 --> 01:31:39.660
the world's sixth-most-powerful
military force?

01:31:39.660 --> 01:31:41.162
It all started in 1959

01:31:41.162 --> 01:31:43.498
at the American National
Exhibition in Moscow.

01:31:43.498 --> 01:31:44.465
When a discussion between

01:31:44.465 --> 01:31:46.000
the then vice president, Richard Nixon,

01:31:46.000 --> 01:31:47.768
and Nikita Khrushchev became heated,

01:31:47.768 --> 01:31:49.737
a Pepsi exec called
Donald Kendall stepped in

01:31:49.737 --> 01:31:52.073
to hand Khrushchev a glass
of Pepsi to cool him down.

01:31:52.073 --> 01:31:54.308
Khrushchev enjoyed Pepsi so
much that arrangements were made

01:31:54.308 --> 01:31:56.744
for it to be imported
from the US to Russia.

01:31:56.744 --> 01:31:59.180
As part of the deal, Pepsi
even negotiated a monopoly,

01:31:59.180 --> 01:32:01.048
locking Coca-Cola out of the country.

01:32:01.048 --> 01:32:02.550
The drink's popularity exploded

01:32:02.550 --> 01:32:04.051
in an otherwise uncontested market.

01:32:04.051 --> 01:32:06.053
But there was one problem: payment.

01:32:06.053 --> 01:32:07.388
The Russian ruble wasn't accepted

01:32:07.388 --> 01:32:08.756
outside of the Soviet Union,

01:32:08.756 --> 01:32:09.724
so the Russians had to come up

01:32:09.724 --> 01:32:11.192
with an alternative arrangement.

01:32:11.192 --> 01:32:13.895
They settled on a
universal currency: vodka.

01:32:13.895 --> 01:32:16.430
Russia traded thousands of
cases of Stolichnaya vodka

01:32:16.430 --> 01:32:18.933
for Pepsi syrup throughout
the '60s and the '70s.

01:32:18.933 --> 01:32:21.269
However, they did run
into problems in the '80s.

01:32:21.269 --> 01:32:23.337
Pepsi's popularity in Russia
combined with a boycott

01:32:23.337 --> 01:32:24.872
due to the Soviet-Afghan War

01:32:24.872 --> 01:32:26.841
meant that vodka just wasn't
going to cut it anymore.

01:32:26.841 --> 01:32:27.808
Here's where things get fun.

01:32:27.808 --> 01:32:29.143
In the spring of 1989,

01:32:29.143 --> 01:32:30.878
Pepsi and the Soviet Union agreed a deal

01:32:30.878 --> 01:32:33.614
whereby Pepsi would be
given 17 old submarines

01:32:33.614 --> 01:32:34.916
and three warships.

01:32:34.916 --> 01:32:37.485
They would then sell them for
scrap to pay for the Pepsi.

01:32:37.485 --> 01:32:38.886
Therefore, for a short period of time,

01:32:38.886 --> 01:32:41.589
Pepsi controlled the
sixth-most-powerful navy in the world.

01:32:41.589 --> 01:32:43.224
Now that is a food war.

01:32:46.327 --> 01:32:48.262
Here's everything in a Pepsi in the US.

01:32:48.262 --> 01:32:50.798
Carbonated water,
high-fructose corn syrup,

01:32:50.798 --> 01:32:52.233
caramel color, sugar,

01:32:52.233 --> 01:32:53.968
phosphoric acid, caffeine,

01:32:53.968 --> 01:32:56.404
citric acid, natural flavors.

01:32:56.404 --> 01:32:58.739
Here is everything in Pepsi in the UK.

01:32:58.739 --> 01:33:00.775
Carbonated water, sugar,

01:33:00.775 --> 01:33:04.011
color (caramel E150d), phosphoric acid,

01:33:04.011 --> 01:33:05.913
and flavorings including caffeine.

01:33:05.913 --> 01:33:07.682
Now, obviously Pepsi
won't go into specifics

01:33:07.682 --> 01:33:08.549
on the flavorings used,

01:33:08.549 --> 01:33:11.118
because the recipe is a
closely guarded secret.

01:33:11.118 --> 01:33:12.186
Or is it?

01:33:12.186 --> 01:33:15.122
Pepsi was invented in
North Carolina in 1893

01:33:15.122 --> 01:33:17.158
by a man named Caleb Bradham.

01:33:17.158 --> 01:33:18.793
First it was called Brad's Drink,

01:33:18.793 --> 01:33:21.495
but then they renamed it Pepsi in 1898.

01:33:21.495 --> 01:33:23.130
It comes from the fact
that it was marketed

01:33:23.130 --> 01:33:27.201
as relieving dyspepsia,
another word for indigestion.

01:33:27.201 --> 01:33:29.570
It's giving me indigestion,
so that's weird.

01:33:29.570 --> 01:33:32.306
However, in 1923, the
original Pepsi-Cola company

01:33:32.306 --> 01:33:34.408
went bankrupt due to
fluctuating sugar prices

01:33:34.408 --> 01:33:35.543
after World War I.

01:33:35.543 --> 01:33:36.777
As part of the bankruptcy filing,

01:33:36.777 --> 01:33:38.679
Bradham had to release
the recipe for Pepsi,

01:33:38.679 --> 01:33:39.780
as it was a company asset,

01:33:39.780 --> 01:33:41.983
and it's still publicly
available to this day.

01:33:41.983 --> 01:33:43.818
So you can make your own Pepsi at home

01:33:43.818 --> 01:33:47.221
simply by combining sugar,
water, caramel color,

01:33:47.221 --> 01:33:50.791
lime juice, phosphoric
acid, alcohol, lemon juice,

01:33:50.791 --> 01:33:55.096
orange juice, cinnamon oil,
nutmeg, coriander (cilantro),

01:33:55.096 --> 01:33:57.431
and petitgrain, which is an essential oil

01:33:57.431 --> 01:34:00.801
extracted from the leaves and
twigs of bitter orange trees.

01:34:00.801 --> 01:34:03.004
Yeah, just those things.

01:34:03.004 --> 01:34:04.438
The rights to Pepsi were eventually bought

01:34:04.438 --> 01:34:05.640
by a man named Charles Guth,

01:34:05.640 --> 01:34:07.708
who was then the president
of the Loft Corporation,

01:34:07.708 --> 01:34:09.410
which later merged into PepsiCo.

01:34:09.410 --> 01:34:10.244
They of course claim

01:34:10.244 --> 01:34:12.380
that they reformulated the
recipe after the takeover,

01:34:12.380 --> 01:34:14.815
but the original is still
there for all to see.

01:34:14.815 --> 01:34:15.983
Enough with the history lesson.

01:34:15.983 --> 01:34:18.386
What is the difference
between US and UK Pepsi?

01:34:18.386 --> 01:34:19.587
That is why we are here.

01:34:19.587 --> 01:34:22.023
First, let's talk about acids.

01:34:22.023 --> 01:34:24.925
Both the UK and the US acidify their Pepsi

01:34:24.925 --> 01:34:26.427
with phosphoric acid.

01:34:26.427 --> 01:34:28.863
It's a weak acid which is
primarily used as a fertilizer.

01:34:28.863 --> 01:34:30.998
When added to foods, it
provides a tangy taste,

01:34:30.998 --> 01:34:32.733
but it's also been linked
to causing kidney stones

01:34:32.733 --> 01:34:34.735
as well as osteoporosis.

01:34:34.735 --> 01:34:37.138
The US also uses citric acid in its Pepsi.

01:34:37.138 --> 01:34:38.606
Citric acid is a more common

01:34:38.606 --> 01:34:40.675
and generally safer acidifier in foods,

01:34:40.675 --> 01:34:42.677
but it's one of the things in sodas

01:34:42.677 --> 01:34:44.578
that will cause tooth enamel to erode

01:34:44.578 --> 01:34:46.380
after repeated consumption.

01:34:46.380 --> 01:34:48.315
I happen to have some of that right here.

01:34:51.585 --> 01:34:53.054
Whoo!

01:34:53.054 --> 01:34:54.221
That's sour.

01:34:55.289 --> 01:34:56.757
Another major difference in Pepsi

01:34:56.757 --> 01:34:58.359
is that in the US, we sweeten our Pepsi

01:34:58.359 --> 01:35:00.895
with high-fructose corn
syrup in addition to sugar.

01:35:00.895 --> 01:35:02.563
High-fructose corn syrup is everywhere

01:35:02.563 --> 01:35:04.398
in American sodas and candies.

01:35:04.398 --> 01:35:06.100
Government subsidies make it super-cheap

01:35:06.100 --> 01:35:07.668
for American farmers to grow corn,

01:35:07.668 --> 01:35:10.971
and food manufacturers use
corn syrup to save money

01:35:10.971 --> 01:35:12.173
instead of importing sugarcane

01:35:12.173 --> 01:35:14.542
from places like Mexico and Brazil.

01:35:14.542 --> 01:35:16.310
Corn syrup is hard to
find at all in the UK.

01:35:16.310 --> 01:35:17.845
We did manage to track
down this small bottle,

01:35:17.845 --> 01:35:19.714
but only in a specialist grocery store.

01:35:19.714 --> 01:35:21.082
Even then, it's just regular corn syrup

01:35:21.082 --> 01:35:22.316
and not high-fructose.

01:35:22.316 --> 01:35:24.051
The good news is, is
that it isn't actually

01:35:24.051 --> 01:35:25.986
any worse for you than sugar.

01:35:25.986 --> 01:35:27.288
The main impact is on the taste,

01:35:27.288 --> 01:35:30.024
which explains why we have a
Pepsi made with real sugar.

01:35:30.024 --> 01:35:32.226
Here it is, a version of the US drink

01:35:32.226 --> 01:35:35.329
which does not contain any corn syrup.

01:35:35.329 --> 01:35:37.732
This version actually has
the exact same ingredients

01:35:37.732 --> 01:35:39.400
as the UK Pepsi.

01:35:39.400 --> 01:35:40.234
Anything else to note?

01:35:40.234 --> 01:35:42.002
Well, our old friend
caramel color is back.

01:35:42.002 --> 01:35:44.572
In the UK, it's listed as caramel E150d,

01:35:44.572 --> 01:35:46.407
while the US just lists caramel color.

01:35:46.407 --> 01:35:48.275
This is a somewhat
controversial food coloring

01:35:48.275 --> 01:35:49.410
used to give things like Pepsi

01:35:49.410 --> 01:35:51.112
a distinctive golden-brown tinge.

01:35:51.112 --> 01:35:52.680
There are four classes of caramel color,

01:35:52.680 --> 01:35:55.182
classes I through IV.
Studies have suggested

01:35:55.182 --> 01:35:57.384
that the production of classes III and IV

01:35:57.384 --> 01:36:00.421
create a byproduct that
may put people at risk

01:36:00.421 --> 01:36:02.189
of developing cancer.

01:36:02.189 --> 01:36:03.557
Of course.

01:36:03.557 --> 01:36:05.793
Why can't we enjoy anything?

01:36:05.793 --> 01:36:09.029
While the FDA has stopped
short of banning caramel color,

01:36:09.029 --> 01:36:11.999
the state of California added
it to its Proposition 65

01:36:11.999 --> 01:36:14.101
list of chemicals known to cause cancer

01:36:14.101 --> 01:36:16.771
and birth defects or reproductive harm.

01:36:16.771 --> 01:36:19.073
Are these potentially harmful
caramel colors in our Pepsi?

01:36:19.073 --> 01:36:20.775
Well, in the UK, the answer is yes.

01:36:20.775 --> 01:36:22.943
We know that class IV caramel
color is used in the UK

01:36:22.943 --> 01:36:26.013
because sulfite ammonia
caramel has the E number E150d,

01:36:26.013 --> 01:36:27.114
which is on the list.

01:36:27.114 --> 01:36:28.482
What about in the US, where it just says

01:36:28.482 --> 01:36:30.351
caramel color without specifics?

01:36:30.351 --> 01:36:31.418
We reached out to Pepsi

01:36:31.418 --> 01:36:33.921
to ask which class they use over here.

01:36:33.921 --> 01:36:36.423
And their answer was: nothing.

01:36:36.423 --> 01:36:38.659
They said they would not comment
on the specific ingredients

01:36:38.659 --> 01:36:40.261
due to proprietary issues.

01:36:40.261 --> 01:36:41.362
Yeah, guys?

01:36:41.362 --> 01:36:43.430
You're afraid that
detail's gonna really help,

01:36:43.430 --> 01:36:44.265
uh,

01:36:44.265 --> 01:36:45.800
cut into your competition?

01:36:45.800 --> 01:36:48.102
So, your Pepsi may or may not

01:36:48.102 --> 01:36:49.403
be putting you at risk of cancer.

01:36:49.403 --> 01:36:50.938
So glad we cleared that up.

01:36:55.242 --> 01:36:57.645
In the US, a can of Pepsi
contains the following.

01:36:57.645 --> 01:36:59.113
150 calories,

01:36:59.113 --> 01:37:00.481
0 grams of fat,

01:37:00.481 --> 01:37:02.216
41 grams of carbohydrates,

01:37:02.216 --> 01:37:03.551
and all those are sugars,

01:37:03.551 --> 01:37:06.153
and 30 milligrams of sodium.

01:37:06.153 --> 01:37:08.556
A can of Pepsi in the UK
contains the following.

01:37:08.556 --> 01:37:10.057
135 calories,

01:37:10.057 --> 01:37:11.225
0 grams of fat,

01:37:11.225 --> 01:37:12.493
36 grams of carbs,

01:37:12.493 --> 01:37:14.295
of which 36 grams are sugars,

01:37:14.295 --> 01:37:16.130
and 4 milligrams of sodium.

01:37:16.130 --> 01:37:18.032
Obviously the alarming
stat here is the sugar.

01:37:18.032 --> 01:37:19.633
Just one can of US Pepsi

01:37:19.633 --> 01:37:22.670
contains 80% of your
daily sugar allowance.

01:37:22.670 --> 01:37:24.004
Really, one?

01:37:24.004 --> 01:37:24.839
Ugh.

01:37:24.839 --> 01:37:26.373
The UK statistic is slightly better,

01:37:26.373 --> 01:37:28.175
at just 72% of your daily allowance,

01:37:28.175 --> 01:37:29.376
but that is still a lot of sugar

01:37:29.376 --> 01:37:31.378
in a relatively small amount of liquid.

01:37:31.378 --> 01:37:33.047
There's also some caffeine in Pepsi.

01:37:33.047 --> 01:37:36.417
A 12-ounce can contains
38 milligrams of caffeine,

01:37:36.417 --> 01:37:39.553
which is about half as much
as an 8-ounce cup of coffee.

01:37:39.553 --> 01:37:40.554
I of course am drinking

01:37:40.554 --> 01:37:42.523
both Pepsi and coffee at the same time.

01:37:44.625 --> 01:37:45.793
Pepsi doesn't openly disclose

01:37:45.793 --> 01:37:47.027
its caffeine content in the UK,

01:37:47.027 --> 01:37:48.395
but according to a website I found

01:37:48.395 --> 01:37:50.030
called caffeineinformer.com,

01:37:50.030 --> 01:37:53.667
a can of UK Pepsi Max contains
42 milligrams of caffeine.

01:37:53.667 --> 01:37:56.570
The US equivalent of Pepsi
Max is Pepsi zero sugar,

01:37:56.570 --> 01:37:58.739
but a can, not pictured here,

01:37:58.739 --> 01:38:01.242
has over 69 milligrams of caffeine.

01:38:01.242 --> 01:38:03.110
N-n-n-n-nice.
[air horn goes off]

01:38:03.110 --> 01:38:05.079
Making it significantly more caffeinated

01:38:05.079 --> 01:38:06.113
than the UK version.

01:38:06.113 --> 01:38:07.715
But the good news: According to the FDA,

01:38:07.715 --> 01:38:10.184
you need to consume 1,200
milligrams of caffeine

01:38:10.184 --> 01:38:12.987
in a short period of time to
put your health in danger.

01:38:12.987 --> 01:38:16.457
That's about 18 cans of Pepsi zero sugar.

01:38:16.457 --> 01:38:18.125
I like a challenge, let's go.

01:38:20.594 --> 01:38:22.096
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

01:38:22.096 --> 01:38:23.764
we wanted to find out all
the differences between

01:38:23.764 --> 01:38:26.100
Mountain Dew in the UK and the US.

01:38:26.100 --> 01:38:27.735
This is "Food Wars."

01:38:30.070 --> 01:38:32.539
In the UK, Mountain Dew
comes in three portion sizes.

01:38:32.539 --> 01:38:35.309
Firstly, we have this 330-milliliter can.

01:38:35.309 --> 01:38:37.411
Then we have a 500-milliliter bottle.

01:38:37.411 --> 01:38:38.445
Then we have our largest size,

01:38:38.445 --> 01:38:40.080
which is a 1-liter bottle.

01:38:40.080 --> 01:38:43.584
In the US, Mountain Dew
comes in six portion sizes.

01:38:43.584 --> 01:38:46.687
The smallest is this 7.5-ounce mini can.

01:38:46.687 --> 01:38:49.390
Then we have our standard 12-ounce can,

01:38:49.390 --> 01:38:51.692
then a 16-ounce,

01:38:51.692 --> 01:38:54.495
or 1-pint, can.

01:38:54.495 --> 01:38:55.362
Then we go to bottles.

01:38:55.362 --> 01:38:58.732
We have a 16.9, or 500-milliliter, bottle.

01:38:58.732 --> 01:39:01.235
Jump up to the 20-ounce bottle,

01:39:01.235 --> 01:39:02.503
and our largest size,

01:39:02.503 --> 01:39:05.072
the 2-liter bottle.

01:39:05.072 --> 01:39:06.874
As you can see, I don't
currently have a 1-liter bottle,

01:39:06.874 --> 01:39:08.642
as it was surprisingly hard to track down.

01:39:08.642 --> 01:39:10.044
I finally thought I'd found some on Amazon

01:39:10.044 --> 01:39:11.278
and ordered a six-pack.

01:39:11.278 --> 01:39:12.379
Then this showed up.

01:39:13.781 --> 01:39:16.517
They helpfully sent me 12
500-milliliter bottles,

01:39:16.517 --> 01:39:18.919
which is the same as six times 1 liter.

01:39:18.919 --> 01:39:21.322
However, not really what we
were going for. Thanks, guys.

01:39:21.322 --> 01:39:25.192
Ha! Our largest size is
double the size of the UK's,

01:39:25.192 --> 01:39:26.660
and we don't even stop there.

01:39:26.660 --> 01:39:31.265
In the US, our largest single
Mountain Dew item is this.

01:39:33.834 --> 01:39:35.436
[cheering]

01:39:35.436 --> 01:39:38.906
24-pack of 12-ounce cans.

01:39:38.906 --> 01:39:41.809
That's 8.52 liters of Mountain Dew,

01:39:41.809 --> 01:39:44.678
or 2.25 gallons.

01:39:44.678 --> 01:39:46.447
It's a little unclear what
our largest single item is.

01:39:46.447 --> 01:39:48.682
On the UK website, there
is a multipack listed,

01:39:48.682 --> 01:39:50.150
but it doesn't say how
many cans are in one,

01:39:50.150 --> 01:39:51.785
and I also couldn't find one anywhere.

01:39:51.785 --> 01:39:53.754
So I called Britvic, who
manufacture Mountain Dew

01:39:53.754 --> 01:39:55.589
here in the UK on behalf of PepsiCo,

01:39:55.589 --> 01:39:56.857
and an employee for them told me that

01:39:56.857 --> 01:39:58.625
the multipack actually doesn't exist.

01:39:58.625 --> 01:39:59.893
The largest item that the company had

01:39:59.893 --> 01:40:01.962
on their own records that
they produce is this.

01:40:04.832 --> 01:40:06.400
A 24-pack of cans.

01:40:06.400 --> 01:40:08.469
However, this is technically
marketed at retailers

01:40:08.469 --> 01:40:10.004
rather than consumers, as you can see

01:40:10.004 --> 01:40:12.439
by the fact that they
have prices on the cans.

01:40:12.439 --> 01:40:14.141
We don't usually count
wholesale on this show,

01:40:14.141 --> 01:40:16.510
so technically a 1-liter
bottle is our largest size,

01:40:16.510 --> 01:40:17.511
if you can find one.

01:40:17.511 --> 01:40:20.247
Joe: That makes the US's largest item

01:40:20.247 --> 01:40:24.385
752% larger than the UK's.

01:40:24.385 --> 01:40:26.020
It's also worth pointing
out the difference in price

01:40:26.020 --> 01:40:27.554
between Mountain Dew in the two countries.

01:40:27.554 --> 01:40:29.690
Sugary sodas in the UK
get hit with a sugar tax,

01:40:29.690 --> 01:40:30.624
which raises their price

01:40:30.624 --> 01:40:32.493
in an effort to stop
people from drinking them.

01:40:32.493 --> 01:40:34.395
If you buy a 500-milliliter Mountain Dew

01:40:34.395 --> 01:40:36.263
from a Sainsbury's in the UK, for example,

01:40:36.263 --> 01:40:38.298
it will cost you 1 pound 25 pence,

01:40:38.298 --> 01:40:40.334
which was around $1.69

01:40:40.334 --> 01:40:41.602
at the time of recording this.

01:40:41.602 --> 01:40:42.436
Nice.

01:40:42.436 --> 01:40:44.438
The closest thing at that
price point in the US

01:40:44.438 --> 01:40:46.040
is this 2-liter.

01:40:46.040 --> 01:40:50.144
According to Target,
this is listed as $1.98.

01:40:50.144 --> 01:40:51.678
You must be joking.

01:40:51.678 --> 01:40:53.547
This isn't even $2?

01:40:53.547 --> 01:40:54.381
Look at this.

01:40:54.381 --> 01:40:56.683
That is alarmingly cheap.

01:40:56.683 --> 01:40:58.318
For every milliliter in the UK bottle,

01:40:58.318 --> 01:41:00.487
you're paying 0.338 cents.

01:41:00.487 --> 01:41:03.657
Every milliliter of this 2-liter

01:41:03.657 --> 01:41:07.961
costs 0.0995 cents.

01:41:07.961 --> 01:41:11.365
That's 70% cheaper than the UK price.

01:41:11.365 --> 01:41:12.399
Hang on.

01:41:12.399 --> 01:41:14.201
Don't you feel like when you
go to a bodega or something,

01:41:14.201 --> 01:41:15.369
this is, like, a dollar?

01:41:15.369 --> 01:41:17.237
So not only is Mountain Dew super cheap,

01:41:17.237 --> 01:41:19.173
but then when you get
it at a sandwich shop

01:41:19.173 --> 01:41:21.308
or something, you're getting gouged!

01:41:21.308 --> 01:41:22.142
Oof!

01:41:24.611 --> 01:41:26.013
Here are all the Mountain
Dew products in the UK

01:41:26.013 --> 01:41:27.614
that you won't find in the US.

01:41:27.614 --> 01:41:29.616
Here are all the Mountain Dew products

01:41:29.616 --> 01:41:32.920
you can find in the US you
will not find in the UK.

01:41:34.888 --> 01:41:36.423
[groans]

01:41:36.423 --> 01:41:38.792
Show this video to the paramedics.

01:41:38.792 --> 01:41:39.760
We can actually only get

01:41:39.760 --> 01:41:41.462
three Mountain Dew products in the UK,

01:41:41.462 --> 01:41:42.963
one of which I couldn't even find today.

01:41:42.963 --> 01:41:43.997
I guess you could sort of call

01:41:43.997 --> 01:41:45.899
our standard Mountain Dew
in the UK an exclusive,

01:41:45.899 --> 01:41:48.936
because here it's marketed
as Mountain Dew Citrus Blast,

01:41:48.936 --> 01:41:50.771
while in the US, it's just Mountain Dew.

01:41:50.771 --> 01:41:52.372
This also comes in a diet version,

01:41:52.372 --> 01:41:53.974
which is the thing that
I couldn't find today.

01:41:53.974 --> 01:41:56.276
The only other thing we
can get in the UK is this,

01:41:56.276 --> 01:41:58.612
which is a SodaStream Mountain Dew syrup.

01:41:58.612 --> 01:41:59.646
For those of you who don't know,

01:41:59.646 --> 01:42:01.215
SodaStream is a device which lets you

01:42:01.215 --> 01:42:02.683
carbonate your own drinks at home.

01:42:02.683 --> 01:42:05.119
PepsiCo owns both Mountain
Dew and SodaStream,

01:42:05.119 --> 01:42:06.353
so I guess it made sense for them to just

01:42:06.353 --> 01:42:07.521
mash their products together.

01:42:07.521 --> 01:42:08.689
Now, I love sparkling water,

01:42:08.689 --> 01:42:11.458
so I actually own my own SodaStream,

01:42:11.458 --> 01:42:13.127
and we can use it to make
our own Mountain Dew.

01:42:13.127 --> 01:42:15.329
Take one of your little
SodaStream water bottles,

01:42:15.329 --> 01:42:18.732
fill it with water, screw
it into the machine.

01:42:18.732 --> 01:42:21.235
Once it's nice and secure,
press the magic button.

01:42:22.669 --> 01:42:24.404
SodaStream, if you want to sponsor me,

01:42:24.404 --> 01:42:25.239
you know where to find me.

01:42:27.608 --> 01:42:29.810
And bang. You have fizzy water.

01:42:29.810 --> 01:42:30.644
Ta-da.

01:42:30.644 --> 01:42:32.446
Nice splash of the syrup.

01:42:33.313 --> 01:42:36.316
That is a strange color on that.

01:42:36.316 --> 01:42:39.019
I don't think that's what I
was expecting it to look like.

01:42:39.019 --> 01:42:40.287
So, I think our homemade stuff actually

01:42:40.287 --> 01:42:41.889
might look slightly more appealing.

01:42:41.889 --> 01:42:44.958
You could kind of pass this
off as more of a fruity soda

01:42:44.958 --> 01:42:46.760
rather than Mountain Dew from the bottle,

01:42:46.760 --> 01:42:49.062
which is a bit more translucent,

01:42:49.062 --> 01:42:51.231
just a bit more pallid and weak,

01:42:51.231 --> 01:42:52.599
and not really sure what's going on there.

01:42:55.269 --> 01:42:56.637
That's actually not bad.

01:42:57.671 --> 01:43:00.274
That's actually not bad.
It's quite a mild flavor.

01:43:00.274 --> 01:43:02.309
It's not actually aggressively sweet.

01:43:02.309 --> 01:43:04.111
Also not too bubbly. I
guess you have a bit more

01:43:04.111 --> 01:43:05.879
control over the bubble
level with the SodaStream.

01:43:05.879 --> 01:43:06.813
Despite the difference in the color,

01:43:06.813 --> 01:43:08.015
they actually taste really similar.

01:43:08.015 --> 01:43:09.716
That's actually a pretty
accurate recreation.

01:43:15.489 --> 01:43:16.690
[exhales]

01:43:16.690 --> 01:43:17.624
Jesus Christ.

01:43:17.624 --> 01:43:19.993
That's one of the sweetest things

01:43:19.993 --> 01:43:22.129
I've ever tasted in my entire life.

01:43:22.129 --> 01:43:22.963
God.

01:43:22.963 --> 01:43:24.731
It's like they've crammed more sugar

01:43:24.731 --> 01:43:27.467
than there is space into that. Ugh.

01:43:27.467 --> 01:43:29.503
I am guaranteeing, Yuelei,

01:43:29.503 --> 01:43:31.438
I'm going to throw up
in the middle of this.

01:43:31.438 --> 01:43:33.707
In the US, we have something
you can't get in the UK,

01:43:33.707 --> 01:43:35.876
and it's Mountain Dew zero sugar.

01:43:35.876 --> 01:43:37.945
Apparently even diet Mountain Dew

01:43:37.945 --> 01:43:39.146
still has some sugar in it.

01:43:39.146 --> 01:43:40.847
I'm not surprised at all.

01:43:40.847 --> 01:43:42.115
Let's see what we got.

01:43:44.618 --> 01:43:45.619
Regular.

01:43:48.355 --> 01:43:49.389
Zero sugar.

01:43:49.389 --> 01:43:51.225
Wow, that's, like, really close.

01:43:51.225 --> 01:43:52.559
I don't mean to do the

01:43:52.559 --> 01:43:54.194
Mountain Dew zero sugar commercial here,

01:43:54.194 --> 01:43:57.397
but it tastes exactly
like regular Mountain Dew.

01:43:57.397 --> 01:43:59.066
Mountain Dew Frost Bite.

01:43:59.066 --> 01:44:01.001
I have no clue what this is.

01:44:02.469 --> 01:44:05.305
Oh, that's terrible. I don't
even know what that is.

01:44:05.305 --> 01:44:07.774
This is an Avella classic.

01:44:07.774 --> 01:44:10.177
I'm not playing. I love
Mountain Dew Code Red.

01:44:10.177 --> 01:44:12.212
It tastes like — what's
that drink that you give kids

01:44:12.212 --> 01:44:13.480
when they want alcohol?

01:44:14.648 --> 01:44:16.283
Shirley Temple?

01:44:16.283 --> 01:44:17.117
Right? What is that?

01:44:17.117 --> 01:44:19.086
That's just Sprite and
cherry grenadine, right?

01:44:19.086 --> 01:44:20.721
That's exactly what this tastes like.

01:44:20.721 --> 01:44:22.623
Oh, man.

01:44:22.623 --> 01:44:25.259
It's back, baby. Mountain Dew Snap'd.

01:44:25.259 --> 01:44:27.894
It's the gingerbread Mountain Dew.

01:44:27.894 --> 01:44:30.797
I mean, it still even has
Christmas shit on it, so.

01:44:30.797 --> 01:44:33.000
It smells just like gingerbread cookies.

01:44:34.334 --> 01:44:35.969
It's good. It's really good.

01:44:35.969 --> 01:44:37.971
Mountain Dew Voltage.

01:44:37.971 --> 01:44:41.308
"Dew charged with raspberry,
citrus flavor, and ginseng."

01:44:41.308 --> 01:44:42.976
It says "charged," guys.

01:44:42.976 --> 01:44:45.045
Look at this. This is like Windex!

01:44:45.879 --> 01:44:46.913
Oh, no.

01:44:48.415 --> 01:44:50.517
Yeah, it has, like, a
melted-popsicle flavor.

01:44:50.517 --> 01:44:52.019
It's a really strong flavor.

01:44:52.019 --> 01:44:54.288
It's Mountain Dew Spark.

01:44:54.288 --> 01:44:58.225
"Dew with a blast of
raspberry lemonade." OK.

01:44:58.225 --> 01:44:59.359
I've already hit a wall.

01:45:03.263 --> 01:45:04.698
Oh, God.

01:45:04.698 --> 01:45:08.702
It's like if you tasked a child

01:45:08.702 --> 01:45:10.437
to make Kool-Aid.

01:45:10.437 --> 01:45:13.206
It's, I can't believe
how strong this stuff is.

01:45:13.206 --> 01:45:15.242
Mountain Dew Major Melon.

01:45:15.242 --> 01:45:16.576
Watermelon Mountain Dew.

01:45:16.576 --> 01:45:18.445
Look what's on here. Look
at what they put on here.

01:45:18.445 --> 01:45:19.513
Do you see this?

01:45:19.513 --> 01:45:21.548
Like, look at the
nightmare that's on here.

01:45:21.548 --> 01:45:23.817
Oh, it smells exactly
like a Jolly Rancher.

01:45:23.817 --> 01:45:26.153
I like Jolly Ranchers, but in liquid form?

01:45:27.721 --> 01:45:29.890
No. Oh, this is really bad.

01:45:31.558 --> 01:45:34.561
It's the most artificial-tasting
thing we've had so far.

01:45:34.561 --> 01:45:36.163
To save me from getting
too bored in this section,

01:45:36.163 --> 01:45:37.230
Joe was kind enough to send me

01:45:37.230 --> 01:45:38.565
some Mountain Dew from America

01:45:38.565 --> 01:45:40.133
as well as some Mountain Dew Code Red.

01:45:40.133 --> 01:45:41.101
Let's do a little side by side

01:45:41.101 --> 01:45:42.636
of British and American Mountain Dews.

01:45:42.636 --> 01:45:44.604
Getting a rare taste
of yellow 5 in the UK.

01:45:44.604 --> 01:45:45.472
Chin, chin.

01:45:48.742 --> 01:45:49.710
Whoa.

01:45:52.412 --> 01:45:53.513
What was that?

01:45:54.514 --> 01:45:56.216
You know in the film "Limitless"
when he takes that pill

01:45:56.216 --> 01:45:58.218
and it just completely rewires his brain?

01:45:58.218 --> 01:46:00.854
I think that's what yellow 5
now does to British people.

01:46:00.854 --> 01:46:02.322
I've had, like, two sips of this,

01:46:02.322 --> 01:46:04.124
and I feel like I could fight God.

01:46:04.124 --> 01:46:05.258
Now that I've tried the American version,

01:46:05.258 --> 01:46:07.060
the British version kind of
just tastes like sadness.

01:46:07.060 --> 01:46:09.730
There's basically no flavor
to it other than sugar,

01:46:09.730 --> 01:46:10.897
whereas at least I think with this,

01:46:10.897 --> 01:46:11.932
you get obviously a lot of sugar,

01:46:11.932 --> 01:46:13.533
but then there's a bit
more citrus as well.

01:46:13.533 --> 01:46:15.369
This has even more illegal
food colorings in it.

01:46:15.369 --> 01:46:17.104
We have red 40 and blue 1,

01:46:17.104 --> 01:46:18.739
which, again, hard to come by in the UK.

01:46:20.307 --> 01:46:21.575
OK, OK.

01:46:21.575 --> 01:46:23.076
I think I actually might just prefer

01:46:23.076 --> 01:46:24.511
classic American Mountain Dew.

01:46:24.511 --> 01:46:26.113
This one felt like a religious experience,

01:46:26.113 --> 01:46:27.547
whereas this one just felt OK.

01:46:27.547 --> 01:46:29.616
So I'll stick to the classic Dew for now.

01:46:29.616 --> 01:46:31.151
If that wasn't enough,
we also have a range of

01:46:31.151 --> 01:46:32.219
Kickstart Mountain Dews.

01:46:32.219 --> 01:46:35.455
These are cans of Dew mixed
with fruit juice and caffeine.

01:46:35.455 --> 01:46:36.623
Imagine that. Caffeine.

01:46:36.623 --> 01:46:38.225
Black cherry.

01:46:38.225 --> 01:46:39.659
Now that it said juice in there,

01:46:39.659 --> 01:46:40.660
I really taste the juice here.

01:46:40.660 --> 01:46:42.729
Ooh, and electrolytes. It's like Gatorade.

01:46:42.729 --> 01:46:45.332
Fruit punch. Love fruit punch.

01:46:46.566 --> 01:46:47.901
Oh, no. That's very bad.

01:46:47.901 --> 01:46:49.469
Full disclosure, pineapple orange mango,

01:46:49.469 --> 01:46:50.370
I had to get a case of these

01:46:50.370 --> 01:46:52.339
just to get the one for the video, so,

01:46:52.339 --> 01:46:53.874
been working on these all week.

01:46:55.742 --> 01:46:56.910
This one's pretty good.

01:46:58.445 --> 01:47:01.081
Oh, man. Caffeine meter is just exploding.

01:47:01.081 --> 01:47:02.015
Orange citrus.

01:47:02.916 --> 01:47:03.750
Yeah, it's not bad.

01:47:03.750 --> 01:47:05.051
Grape.

01:47:05.051 --> 01:47:05.952
[burps]

01:47:05.952 --> 01:47:07.954
That's the grapiest-tasting
grape thing, yeah.

01:47:07.954 --> 01:47:08.955
The flavors aren't as harsh,

01:47:08.955 --> 01:47:10.490
'cause they cut it with juice.

01:47:10.490 --> 01:47:12.426
So, yeah, I kind of like the Kickstarts.

01:47:12.426 --> 01:47:13.360
Is that everything? 'Cause I'm getting

01:47:13.360 --> 01:47:14.261
a bit bored over here.

01:47:14.261 --> 01:47:15.996
I might just go and make a cup of tea.

01:47:18.131 --> 01:47:20.667
Sorry, Harry, we have even more options.

01:47:20.667 --> 01:47:24.337
We have Mountain Dew Amp energy drink.

01:47:24.337 --> 01:47:26.039
More caffeine, I'm assuming.

01:47:26.039 --> 01:47:29.276
This is the original. You can
also get it in Cherry Blast.

01:47:32.112 --> 01:47:33.547
[Windows shutdown sound]

01:47:33.547 --> 01:47:36.550
Oh, man, that's really weird.

01:47:36.550 --> 01:47:37.617
Whoa.

01:47:39.686 --> 01:47:41.888
[inhales]

01:47:41.888 --> 01:47:44.391
[exhales]

01:47:45.692 --> 01:47:47.861
[kettle boiling]

01:47:47.861 --> 01:47:49.729
Ha ha, if that wasn't enough,

01:47:49.729 --> 01:47:51.798
we also have Mountain Dew Energy.

01:47:51.798 --> 01:47:52.732
Why?

01:47:52.732 --> 01:47:54.134
What was this?

01:47:54.134 --> 01:47:55.035
Oh, Jesus.

01:47:55.035 --> 01:47:56.436
They even broke — oh, my God, look,

01:47:56.436 --> 01:47:57.637
they even broke it down on here.

01:47:57.637 --> 01:47:59.172
Two cups of coffee.

01:47:59.172 --> 01:48:03.243
5% juice, no added sugar, zinc.

01:48:03.243 --> 01:48:04.611
Zinc?

01:48:04.611 --> 01:48:06.146
What's zinc got to do with anything?

01:48:06.146 --> 01:48:07.647
For all I know the can's made of zinc.

01:48:07.647 --> 01:48:08.482
Oh, here it goes.

01:48:08.482 --> 01:48:11.084
"Zinc helps support immune function."

01:48:11.084 --> 01:48:12.319
I mean, I feel like I'm gonna drink this

01:48:12.319 --> 01:48:13.954
and, like, try and run
through a brick wall.

01:48:13.954 --> 01:48:15.322
What's that doing for my health?

01:48:15.322 --> 01:48:17.891
"Citicoline and caffeine in combination

01:48:17.891 --> 01:48:20.560
have been shown to help
improve attention."

01:48:20.560 --> 01:48:22.195
Get —

01:48:22.195 --> 01:48:25.499
get out of my face with this on here!

01:48:25.499 --> 01:48:26.933
[groans]

01:48:28.869 --> 01:48:31.238
[echoing] Show this
video to the paramedics.

01:48:34.741 --> 01:48:36.576
[groans]

01:48:36.576 --> 01:48:38.478
Let's do this. Orange Breeze.

01:48:38.478 --> 01:48:40.146
Orange Breeze, right?

01:48:40.146 --> 01:48:42.916
A nice breeze of orange?

01:48:42.916 --> 01:48:44.718
Moments before you

01:48:44.718 --> 01:48:48.688
tackle a stranger, bite into
their neck for sustenance?

01:48:50.524 --> 01:48:52.092
Oh, that's really bad.

01:48:52.092 --> 01:48:53.059
I don't think so.

01:48:53.059 --> 01:48:54.261
They shouldn't even have it here.

01:48:54.261 --> 01:48:56.229
They should just have like a Capri Sun,

01:48:56.229 --> 01:48:57.130
like, two holes here,

01:48:57.130 --> 01:48:59.399
and you just bite into
it, like "Teen Wolf,"

01:48:59.399 --> 01:49:00.233
and drink it that way.

01:49:00.233 --> 01:49:01.735
Is that possible to do that?

01:49:01.735 --> 01:49:03.370
Pomegranate Blue Burst.

01:49:04.738 --> 01:49:07.040
This is driving me out of my mind.

01:49:07.040 --> 01:49:08.875
I've never had a pomegranate
that tastes like that,

01:49:08.875 --> 01:49:10.310
and if I did, I would spit it out.

01:49:10.310 --> 01:49:12.812
Oh, Strawberry Melon Spark.

01:49:14.247 --> 01:49:15.415
Ugh.

01:49:16.850 --> 01:49:18.084
No. Melon drinks are bad.

01:49:18.084 --> 01:49:20.086
Peach Mango Dawn.

01:49:20.086 --> 01:49:22.322
The dawn — a new dawn is here.

01:49:24.758 --> 01:49:26.159
Oh, my vision is starting to blur.

01:49:26.159 --> 01:49:28.128
Tropical Sunrise.

01:49:29.429 --> 01:49:31.464
They've somehow bottled energy.

01:49:31.464 --> 01:49:32.299
Ah!

01:49:32.299 --> 01:49:33.967
Like licking a 9-volt!

01:49:39.773 --> 01:49:42.842
The last exclusive Mountain Dew product

01:49:42.842 --> 01:49:44.010
we're gonna cover

01:49:44.010 --> 01:49:46.012
before I go totally blind

01:49:46.012 --> 01:49:48.848
is Mountain Dew Game Fuel.

01:49:48.848 --> 01:49:52.385
It's the first beverage, quote,
"designed just for gamers."

01:49:52.385 --> 01:49:53.219
For real?

01:49:53.219 --> 01:49:55.355
Hey, sitting for 12 hours at a time

01:49:55.355 --> 01:49:56.256
doing literally nothing

01:49:56.256 --> 01:49:57.991
staring at a flashing screen?

01:49:57.991 --> 01:50:01.361
Drink this. Your muscles
won't atrophy as fast?

01:50:01.361 --> 01:50:02.729
I'm not quite sure what's in here

01:50:02.729 --> 01:50:04.464
to make it better for gaming,

01:50:04.464 --> 01:50:06.633
but the flavors they have is Cherry Burst,

01:50:06.633 --> 01:50:09.002
Berry Blast, original,

01:50:09.002 --> 01:50:12.005
Tropical Strike, and Orange Storm.

01:50:12.005 --> 01:50:12.939
Let's take out the flavors

01:50:12.939 --> 01:50:14.374
and go through those words one more time.

01:50:14.374 --> 01:50:15.275
Burst!

01:50:15.275 --> 01:50:16.309
Blast.

01:50:16.309 --> 01:50:17.243
Strike.

01:50:17.243 --> 01:50:18.278
Storm.

01:50:18.278 --> 01:50:19.579
Stupid.

01:50:20.447 --> 01:50:22.749
You know, it's not as
strong as the energy drinks.

01:50:22.749 --> 01:50:24.651
The Charged Berry Blast.

01:50:24.651 --> 01:50:26.886
Charged Berry Blast.

01:50:26.886 --> 01:50:27.921
Ooh.

01:50:27.921 --> 01:50:28.788
Ah, man.

01:50:28.788 --> 01:50:31.524
Feel like I'm pulling
the tab out of a grenade.

01:50:32.425 --> 01:50:33.727
You know, I'm surprised.
These aren't as harsh

01:50:33.727 --> 01:50:34.995
as the energy drinks.

01:50:34.995 --> 01:50:38.264
I'm feeling myself getting
ready to game, Yuelei!

01:50:38.264 --> 01:50:40.433
Getting ready to do some gaming!

01:50:40.433 --> 01:50:43.403
Eh? I'm about to whoop
some Candy Crush ass!

01:50:43.403 --> 01:50:45.972
This one is the Charged Tropical Strike.

01:50:47.674 --> 01:50:48.508
This one's not that bad.

01:50:48.508 --> 01:50:50.210
Full disclosure, I thought
these were going to

01:50:50.210 --> 01:50:51.444
hands down be the worst.

01:50:51.444 --> 01:50:53.079
I still don't like them, but.

01:50:53.079 --> 01:50:55.448
Ooh! Charged Cherry Burst.

01:50:55.448 --> 01:50:57.384
Bursting with cherry.

01:50:57.384 --> 01:51:00.720
Here's the issue. Are they
going to make me a better gamer?

01:51:00.720 --> 01:51:02.188
There's only one way to find out.

01:51:02.188 --> 01:51:04.491
I'm going to play an extreme game.

01:51:04.491 --> 01:51:05.392
That's right.

01:51:05.392 --> 01:51:07.260
Let's do today's Wordle.

01:51:07.260 --> 01:51:09.829
I'm going to take a sip and
then put in a five-letter word.

01:51:09.829 --> 01:51:11.231
An extreme five-letter word.

01:51:12.265 --> 01:51:13.466
Skate.

01:51:17.003 --> 01:51:17.937
Board.

01:51:20.874 --> 01:51:21.708
Mm.

01:51:21.708 --> 01:51:22.709
Yuelei: How about blast?

01:51:22.709 --> 01:51:23.810
There you go!

01:51:23.810 --> 01:51:25.712
This Game Fuel's not helping.

01:51:25.712 --> 01:51:27.347
Taste. Oh, wait, not E though.

01:51:28.481 --> 01:51:32.052
OK, blank, A S T, blank.

01:51:32.052 --> 01:51:33.186
Let's do casts.

01:51:34.521 --> 01:51:36.156
OK. Maybe nasty?

01:51:36.156 --> 01:51:37.123
N A S T Y?

01:51:38.658 --> 01:51:40.860
All right, Wordle, here we go!

01:51:40.860 --> 01:51:42.028
Is nasty it?

01:51:43.997 --> 01:51:47.400
Oh-ho-ho!

01:51:47.400 --> 01:51:48.435
That's right.

01:51:48.435 --> 01:51:49.736
So what have we learned?

01:51:49.736 --> 01:51:51.204
Uh, me?

01:51:51.204 --> 01:51:52.138
Nothing.

01:51:52.138 --> 01:51:54.441
Carry on, don't let me interrupt.

01:51:54.441 --> 01:51:56.176
If that wasn't enough, Mountain Dew

01:51:56.176 --> 01:51:58.511
recently partnered with
the Boston Beer Company

01:51:58.511 --> 01:52:00.714
to create hard Mountain Dew,

01:52:00.714 --> 01:52:03.817
a boozy spiked-seltzer
version of Mountain Dew.

01:52:03.817 --> 01:52:05.585
And it comes in four flavors,

01:52:05.585 --> 01:52:06.820
original Mountain Dew,

01:52:06.820 --> 01:52:08.621
Baja Blast, oh, God,

01:52:08.621 --> 01:52:10.123
black cherry, and watermelon.

01:52:10.123 --> 01:52:11.691
And you're asking, "Joe, where is it?

01:52:11.691 --> 01:52:12.859
I wanna see you get drunk!"

01:52:12.859 --> 01:52:14.227
Well, I'm in California,

01:52:14.227 --> 01:52:16.396
and it currently is only
available in the United States

01:52:16.396 --> 01:52:18.932
in Tennessee, Iowa, and Florida.

01:52:18.932 --> 01:52:20.467
Of course it's in Florida.

01:52:22.335 --> 01:52:24.704
Joe, I think we're gonna need
the lab coats for this one.

01:52:27.073 --> 01:52:27.907
Let's do it.

01:52:27.907 --> 01:52:30.210
There's a fun FAQ on the
UK Mountain Dew website

01:52:30.210 --> 01:52:32.011
explaining why the American
version of the product

01:52:32.011 --> 01:52:34.514
isn't sold here, citing EU regulations.

01:52:34.514 --> 01:52:36.282
There are some pretty major
differences between the two,

01:52:36.282 --> 01:52:38.017
some of which are potentially harmful.

01:52:38.017 --> 01:52:39.552
The US sweetens its Mountain Dew

01:52:39.552 --> 01:52:41.821
with high-fructose corn
syrup instead of sugar.

01:52:41.821 --> 01:52:44.324
Well, it's not necessarily
any worse for you.

01:52:44.324 --> 01:52:46.025
It provides an even sweeter taste

01:52:46.025 --> 01:52:48.394
with a slightly more chemical flavor.

01:52:48.394 --> 01:52:50.363
The US also flavors its
Dew with orange juice,

01:52:50.363 --> 01:52:52.699
which isn't specifically
listed in the UK ingredients.

01:52:52.699 --> 01:52:55.835
One thing to look out for in
the US is sodium benzoate.

01:52:55.835 --> 01:52:57.003
This is a sodium salt

01:52:57.003 --> 01:52:59.272
which food producers
use as a preservative.

01:52:59.272 --> 01:53:00.340
On its own, it's harmless,

01:53:00.340 --> 01:53:02.575
but when it's mixed with certain acids,

01:53:02.575 --> 01:53:05.545
like erythorbic or ascorbic acid,

01:53:05.545 --> 01:53:08.248
it can form benzene.

01:53:08.248 --> 01:53:10.750
Benzene is a known carcinogen
linked to causing cancer

01:53:10.750 --> 01:53:12.719
in factory workers who've
been exposed to it.

01:53:12.719 --> 01:53:14.988
The good news is US
Mountain Dew also contains

01:53:14.988 --> 01:53:17.757
calcium disodium EDTA,

01:53:17.757 --> 01:53:20.160
aka calcium disodium ...

01:53:20.160 --> 01:53:22.929
Automated voice:
Ethylenediaminetetraacetate.

01:53:22.929 --> 01:53:25.532
This can't be spelled right. Terraacetate?

01:53:25.532 --> 01:53:28.234
Look, there's two A's next
to each other in that word!

01:53:28.234 --> 01:53:29.502
With the exception of aardvark,

01:53:29.502 --> 01:53:31.171
I've never seen two
A's next to each other.

01:53:31.171 --> 01:53:32.939
And that's how that word starts!

01:53:32.939 --> 01:53:35.008
This is a flavor enhancer,
but it actually helps

01:53:35.008 --> 01:53:37.777
mitigate the formation of benzene.

01:53:37.777 --> 01:53:40.613
Fantastic. They added
poison chemicals into it,

01:53:40.613 --> 01:53:41.981
then they just added more chemicals

01:53:41.981 --> 01:53:43.750
to stop it from being so poisonous.

01:53:43.750 --> 01:53:45.985
Studies have shown that it
chelates the metallic ions

01:53:45.985 --> 01:53:48.221
that catalyze hydroxyl radical formation.

01:53:48.221 --> 01:53:49.489
It's a complicated way of saying that

01:53:49.489 --> 01:53:51.124
it removes the fuel from the fire.

01:53:51.124 --> 01:53:51.958
Mm.

01:53:52.826 --> 01:53:54.027
Talk about teeth for a second,

01:53:54.027 --> 01:53:56.429
because Mountain Dew is
pretty bad for your teeth.

01:53:57.797 --> 01:54:00.600
When a man sued PepsiCo in 2009,

01:54:00.600 --> 01:54:02.735
he alleged that he found a mouse

01:54:02.735 --> 01:54:04.270
in his can of Mountain Dew.

01:54:04.270 --> 01:54:07.207
But experts called in by PepsiCo said

01:54:07.207 --> 01:54:08.675
that couldn't have been true

01:54:08.675 --> 01:54:11.911
because Mountain Dew would
have dissolved the mouse

01:54:11.911 --> 01:54:14.247
into a jellylike substance.

01:54:14.247 --> 01:54:16.482
Oh, my God.

01:54:16.482 --> 01:54:20.119
If it is strong enough to
dissolve an entire mouse,

01:54:20.119 --> 01:54:21.888
you can imagine the
effect Mountain Dew has

01:54:21.888 --> 01:54:23.289
on the enamel of your teeth.

01:54:23.289 --> 01:54:25.391
But why is this happening?

01:54:25.391 --> 01:54:26.559
It comes down to two things.

01:54:26.559 --> 01:54:27.861
Sugar and acid.

01:54:27.861 --> 01:54:29.729
Tooth enamel will start to erode

01:54:29.729 --> 01:54:32.765
if your mouth's pH level drops below 5.5.

01:54:32.765 --> 01:54:36.135
Mountain Dew has a pH of
around 3.3, which is acidic,

01:54:36.135 --> 01:54:38.137
but actually on the
better end of the spectrum

01:54:38.137 --> 01:54:39.939
as far as sodas are concerned.

01:54:39.939 --> 01:54:41.474
Sugar itself has a neutral pH,

01:54:41.474 --> 01:54:43.009
so why does it aid tooth decay?

01:54:43.009 --> 01:54:44.844
Our mouths play host
to a range of bacteria.

01:54:44.844 --> 01:54:46.579
They're usually harmless
and can even be helpful

01:54:46.579 --> 01:54:48.648
by fighting things like gingivitis.

01:54:48.648 --> 01:54:49.916
However, they also love sugar,

01:54:49.916 --> 01:54:51.651
and when they eat it, they produce acid.

01:54:51.651 --> 01:54:53.319
Bacteria like Streptococcus mutans

01:54:53.319 --> 01:54:55.488
will metabolize sugar into lactic acid,

01:54:55.488 --> 01:54:56.923
which will then erode your teeth.

01:54:56.923 --> 01:54:59.259
The more sugar in a soda, the
more food these bacteria have

01:54:59.259 --> 01:55:00.593
and the more acid they'll produce,

01:55:00.593 --> 01:55:02.395
so sugar isn't causing it per se,

01:55:02.395 --> 01:55:03.997
but it is aiding tooth decay.

01:55:03.997 --> 01:55:05.465
Acids commonly used in soda

01:55:05.465 --> 01:55:07.667
include citric acid and phosphoric acid.

01:55:07.667 --> 01:55:10.603
Firstly, these acids will
lower the pH of your soda

01:55:10.603 --> 01:55:12.605
and contribute to regular erosion.

01:55:12.605 --> 01:55:14.874
But they're also examples
of polybasic acids.

01:55:14.874 --> 01:55:16.442
These bind to the calcium in our teeth

01:55:16.442 --> 01:55:18.711
and cause us to lose
enamel through dissolution.

01:55:18.711 --> 01:55:20.346
It's kind of like how you can use acid

01:55:20.346 --> 01:55:22.248
to dissolve rust on a metallic object.

01:55:22.248 --> 01:55:23.683
All in all, while Mountain
Dew might not have

01:55:23.683 --> 01:55:25.985
one of the lowest pHs when
compared to other sodas,

01:55:25.985 --> 01:55:27.954
its sugar content combined
with its other ingredients

01:55:27.954 --> 01:55:29.822
means that it is one of
the worst for your teeth.

01:55:29.822 --> 01:55:33.660
A 2004 study place it as
the second-worst offender

01:55:33.660 --> 01:55:36.462
for the dissolution of tooth enamel,

01:55:36.462 --> 01:55:37.864
second only to Sprite.

01:55:37.864 --> 01:55:39.832
Really? Sprite's worse? Hm.

01:55:39.832 --> 01:55:42.568
And the bad news is the
sugar-free versions of sodas

01:55:42.568 --> 01:55:44.604
don't seem to be any less harmful.

01:55:44.604 --> 01:55:46.673
The replacement sweeteners
have the same effect

01:55:46.673 --> 01:55:48.875
on the bacteria in your teeth.

01:55:48.875 --> 01:55:49.876
If you're worried about your teeth,

01:55:49.876 --> 01:55:51.210
it's better to drink a soda quickly

01:55:51.210 --> 01:55:52.578
than it is to continuously sip it

01:55:52.578 --> 01:55:53.746
and prolong the exposure.

01:55:53.746 --> 01:55:55.581
Or, you know, you could
just stop drinking soda.

01:55:57.650 --> 01:55:58.985
Interestingly, the UK version

01:55:58.985 --> 01:56:00.420
actually has 1 more gram of sugar

01:56:00.420 --> 01:56:02.055
when compared to the American version.

01:56:02.055 --> 01:56:04.390
But our Mountain Dew is
slightly more calorific

01:56:04.390 --> 01:56:06.826
and has double the
sodium of the UK version.

01:56:06.826 --> 01:56:09.696
The bottles contain 132% and 130%

01:56:09.696 --> 01:56:11.698
of your daily sugar
allowance, respectively.

01:56:11.698 --> 01:56:12.932
Let's find out what that looks like.

01:56:15.768 --> 01:56:17.971
Wait, this is for how big of a thing?

01:56:17.971 --> 01:56:19.739
No, way. Yeah!

01:56:19.739 --> 01:56:21.207
Oh, my God.

01:56:22.175 --> 01:56:23.710
That's 50. We're not done yet.

01:56:26.379 --> 01:56:29.082
That's an appalling amount of sugar.

01:56:29.082 --> 01:56:30.550
I'm still gonna drink it.

01:56:30.550 --> 01:56:31.751
Another thing worth pointing out

01:56:31.751 --> 01:56:34.387
is the caffeine content
of your Mountain Dew.

01:56:34.387 --> 01:56:36.122
In the UK, many supermarkets will actually

01:56:36.122 --> 01:56:38.358
list Mountain Dew as Mountain Dew Energy.

01:56:38.358 --> 01:56:39.993
This is because we have
stricter rules in the UK

01:56:39.993 --> 01:56:42.161
about what does and doesn't
count as an energy drink.

01:56:42.161 --> 01:56:43.896
In the UK, sugar is a major factor

01:56:43.896 --> 01:56:45.698
in deciding whether or not
something is an energy drink,

01:56:45.698 --> 01:56:47.066
as opposed to just caffeine.

01:56:47.066 --> 01:56:48.001
Mountain Dew doesn't actually

01:56:48.001 --> 01:56:50.003
disclose its caffeine content in the UK.

01:56:50.003 --> 01:56:52.472
All I could find was this
Twitter reply from 2018

01:56:52.472 --> 01:56:54.207
where they said it used
to be an energy drink,

01:56:54.207 --> 01:56:55.308
but then they reformulated it

01:56:55.308 --> 01:56:57.577
and now it has a similar
amount of caffeine to Pepsi.

01:56:57.577 --> 01:56:58.878
They claim that this is less than half

01:56:58.878 --> 01:57:00.713
the caffeine content of a cup of coffee.

01:57:00.713 --> 01:57:04.450
Regular Mountain Dew contains
72 milligrams of caffeine

01:57:04.450 --> 01:57:06.219
per 16 ounces.

01:57:06.219 --> 01:57:09.756
There's around 92 milligrams of caffeine

01:57:09.756 --> 01:57:11.491
in the same amount of Kickstart,

01:57:11.491 --> 01:57:14.293
almost one and a half
espressos' worth of caffeine.

01:57:14.293 --> 01:57:17.497
Moving up, Mountain Dew
Amp, original and cherry,

01:57:17.497 --> 01:57:21.367
contain 142 and 160 milligrams of caffeine

01:57:21.367 --> 01:57:22.602
per 16 ounces,

01:57:22.602 --> 01:57:25.171
more than a double espresso.

01:57:25.171 --> 01:57:27.907
The worst offender is Mountain Dew Energy.

01:57:27.907 --> 01:57:31.377
One of these cans contains
180 milligrams of caffeine

01:57:31.377 --> 01:57:34.747
per 16 ounces, which is
basically a triple espresso.

01:57:34.747 --> 01:57:36.149
For reference, the FDA recommends that

01:57:36.149 --> 01:57:37.817
the average healthy
adult shouldn't consume

01:57:37.817 --> 01:57:40.153
more than 400 milligrams
of caffeine per day.

01:57:40.153 --> 01:57:42.221
That's just over two of
those Mountain Dew energies.

01:57:42.221 --> 01:57:44.190
It's around the 1,200-milligram mark

01:57:44.190 --> 01:57:46.325
where you're actually
putting yourself in danger.

01:57:46.325 --> 01:57:47.226
According to the FDA,

01:57:47.226 --> 01:57:49.695
ingesting 1,200 milligrams of caffeine,

01:57:49.695 --> 01:57:51.964
and you may experience seizures

01:57:51.964 --> 01:57:55.568
or put yourself at risk
of cardiac arrhythmia.

01:57:55.568 --> 01:57:59.205
So please do not drink more
than six of these at one time.

01:57:59.205 --> 01:58:01.741
The "good" news is that
the lethal dose of caffeine

01:58:01.741 --> 01:58:03.342
is around 5 grams,

01:58:03.342 --> 01:58:05.044
so to actually overdose on Mountain Dew,

01:58:05.044 --> 01:58:07.413
you'd need to chug 28 of
the Mountain Dew Energies

01:58:07.413 --> 01:58:08.681
in pretty quick succession.

01:58:08.681 --> 01:58:09.615
What a way to go.

01:58:09.982 --> 01:58:11.384
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

01:58:11.384 --> 01:58:12.785
we wanted to find out all the differences

01:58:12.785 --> 01:58:15.288
between Monster Energy
in the UK and the US.

01:58:15.288 --> 01:58:16.856
This is "Food Wars."

01:58:20.726 --> 01:58:22.295
Officially speaking,
classic Monster Energy

01:58:22.295 --> 01:58:24.297
only comes in one portion size in the UK,

01:58:24.297 --> 01:58:25.731
a 500-milliliter can.

01:58:25.731 --> 01:58:27.166
I looked around for other sizes online,

01:58:27.166 --> 01:58:30.470
and I thought I'd found
some 440-milliliter cans,

01:58:30.470 --> 01:58:31.938
but unfortunately the seller just sent me

01:58:31.938 --> 01:58:33.506
a bunch more regular cans.

01:58:35.208 --> 01:58:36.476
Only on "Food Wars" are we annoyed

01:58:36.476 --> 01:58:38.244
when they send you more than you paid for.

01:58:38.244 --> 01:58:41.447
Classic US Monster Energy
comes in two sizes:

01:58:41.447 --> 01:58:43.349
the standard 16-ounce cans

01:58:43.349 --> 01:58:46.786
or the mega Monster Energy
can, which is 24 ounces.

01:58:46.786 --> 01:58:48.287
Good Lord.

01:58:48.287 --> 01:58:50.223
That's 710 milliliters,

01:58:50.223 --> 01:58:54.127
a 42% increase of the largest UK can.

01:58:54.127 --> 01:58:55.194
This is too big.

01:58:55.194 --> 01:58:57.096
This looks like the canister of ooze

01:58:57.096 --> 01:58:59.165
that fell in the sewer and
made the Ninja Turtles.

01:58:59.165 --> 01:59:01.067
When you buy other Monster
products in the UK, however,

01:59:01.067 --> 01:59:03.369
some of them do come in different
sizes to the classic can.

01:59:03.369 --> 01:59:05.471
For example, an Espresso
Monster can in the UK

01:59:05.471 --> 01:59:07.473
is just 250 milliliters.

01:59:07.473 --> 01:59:09.308
Certain Monster Ultra flavors come in a

01:59:09.308 --> 01:59:11.777
553-milliliter mega can.

01:59:11.777 --> 01:59:13.980
And if you get a bottle
of Monster HydroSport,

01:59:13.980 --> 01:59:16.883
it comes in a 650-milliliter bottle.

01:59:16.883 --> 01:59:18.518
The type of Monster Energy you drink

01:59:18.518 --> 01:59:20.586
also affects the can size in the US.

01:59:20.586 --> 01:59:23.723
Java Monster cans come in 15 fluid ounces,

01:59:23.723 --> 01:59:26.192
and the Rehab Monsters come in cans

01:59:26.192 --> 01:59:28.094
of 15.5 fluid ounces.

01:59:28.094 --> 01:59:32.098
16's too much. 15.5? Print it!

01:59:32.098 --> 01:59:34.100
What about the largest
multipack item by volume?

01:59:34.100 --> 01:59:37.537
Well, in the UK, it's a
12-pack of Monster HydroSport,

01:59:38.938 --> 01:59:41.474
which totals 9.75 liters of Monster.

01:59:41.474 --> 01:59:42.575
That's quite a lot.

01:59:42.575 --> 01:59:46.712
That pales in comparison to
the US's largest multipack.

01:59:46.712 --> 01:59:48.915
That's 24-pack

01:59:48.915 --> 01:59:50.583
of 16-ounce cans.

01:59:50.583 --> 01:59:54.453
It's a total of 11.365 liters of Monster

01:59:54.453 --> 01:59:57.957
and a 16.5% increase of the UK multipack.

01:59:57.957 --> 01:59:59.625
I didn't get it, but it is at Target,

01:59:59.625 --> 02:00:01.661
so here's a video of me holding it up.

02:00:01.661 --> 02:00:02.929
I thought I was taking a picture.

02:00:02.929 --> 02:00:05.198
That's me realizing that,
oops, it's on video.

02:00:07.466 --> 02:00:09.035
Here are all the Monster
Energy products you'll find

02:00:09.035 --> 02:00:11.771
in the UK, including those
that you won't find in the US.

02:00:11.771 --> 02:00:14.574
Here are all the Monster
Energy products in the US,

02:00:14.574 --> 02:00:17.009
including a bunch you
won't find in the UK.

02:00:17.009 --> 02:00:18.911
Not only are we going to
talk through all these,

02:00:18.911 --> 02:00:20.513
but we're also going to taste them

02:00:20.513 --> 02:00:22.548
and provide some thoughts.

02:00:22.548 --> 02:00:24.250
Oh, I'm dead.

02:00:24.250 --> 02:00:26.018
I'm dead!

02:00:26.018 --> 02:00:27.486
Ugh. I mean, I barely survived

02:00:27.486 --> 02:00:29.355
the Mountain Dew episode, so.

02:00:29.355 --> 02:00:30.489
[laughs]

02:00:30.489 --> 02:00:32.225
If you would've thought
by now on "Food Wars"

02:00:32.225 --> 02:00:33.793
I would've learned my lesson,

02:00:33.793 --> 02:00:35.027
you would think wrong.

02:00:35.027 --> 02:00:36.896
Let's start with just the
classic Monster Energy drinks.

02:00:36.896 --> 02:00:38.197
In the UK, we have two flavors

02:00:38.197 --> 02:00:39.432
that you won't find in the US.

02:00:39.432 --> 02:00:41.367
We could only get one of them
today, and it's this one:

02:00:41.367 --> 02:00:43.836
The Lewis Hamilton 44 Monster Energy.

02:00:43.836 --> 02:00:45.538
Lewis Hamilton is a Formula 1 driver.

02:00:45.538 --> 02:00:46.906
He's one of the best ones of all time.

02:00:46.906 --> 02:00:48.708
He's got, I think, seven
world championships,

02:00:48.708 --> 02:00:51.444
and when he races, he wears
his lucky number, which is 44.

02:00:51.444 --> 02:00:53.079
So they partnered with
Lewis to create this drink.

02:00:53.079 --> 02:00:54.113
Let's give it a try.

02:00:58.017 --> 02:01:00.253
It just smells like Red
Bull, I'll be honest.

02:01:00.253 --> 02:01:01.554
It's probably a good time now to say that

02:01:01.554 --> 02:01:03.089
I don't actually drink energy drinks.

02:01:03.089 --> 02:01:05.191
I barely even drink coffee.
I'm very loyal to tea.

02:01:05.191 --> 02:01:07.126
So this is gonna be fun.

02:01:07.126 --> 02:01:08.127
Cheers.

02:01:12.265 --> 02:01:13.933
It's just quite a standard
energy-drink flavor.

02:01:13.933 --> 02:01:15.301
But yeah, goes down pretty smooth.

02:01:15.301 --> 02:01:17.536
I wonder if this would make
me faster if I drank it.

02:01:17.536 --> 02:01:20.239
The other exclusive UK Monster
Energy flavor is VR/46,

02:01:20.239 --> 02:01:22.508
which unfortunately I wasn't
able to track down today.

02:01:22.508 --> 02:01:24.043
The VR/46 can is designed

02:01:24.043 --> 02:01:25.811
in collaboration with Valentino Rossi,

02:01:25.811 --> 02:01:27.647
who's a very famous MotoGP driver.

02:01:27.647 --> 02:01:28.681
I'm sad I couldn't get the can today,

02:01:28.681 --> 02:01:31.217
because it gave me enormous
"Mario Sunshine" vibes.

02:01:31.217 --> 02:01:32.551
Also, I need to stop drinking this,

02:01:32.551 --> 02:01:35.221
because I've got a lot
more Monsters to try.

02:01:35.221 --> 02:01:37.790
We have a few exclusive Monster
Energy drinks in the US.

02:01:37.790 --> 02:01:40.259
Got the Monster Energy low-carb.

02:01:41.160 --> 02:01:42.895
Oh, I hate how that smells.

02:01:42.895 --> 02:01:44.463
Like, I instantly have a headache.

02:01:45.865 --> 02:01:48.034
This has to be the first time in history

02:01:48.034 --> 02:01:51.771
someone has poured Monster
Energy drink into a cup

02:01:51.771 --> 02:01:53.906
and not drank it straight
from the can, right?

02:01:53.906 --> 02:01:55.741
Who's ever done this before?

02:01:58.611 --> 02:02:00.713
It tastes like electricity.

02:02:00.713 --> 02:02:04.483
Next up, we have the
Monster Reserve watermelon

02:02:04.483 --> 02:02:06.619
and the Reserve white pineapple.

02:02:06.619 --> 02:02:07.453
Oh!

02:02:08.888 --> 02:02:11.957
It tastes like sparkling juice, I guess.

02:02:11.957 --> 02:02:15.428
There's this flavor of, like, static.

02:02:15.428 --> 02:02:17.897
We got this one, this is the
Monster Import super premium.

02:02:17.897 --> 02:02:20.633
What is the -- it's 18.6 fluid ounces.

02:02:20.633 --> 02:02:21.934
But look at the top on this one.

02:02:21.934 --> 02:02:24.136
Do you understand how to open this?

02:02:24.136 --> 02:02:25.971
Oh, here we go.

02:02:25.971 --> 02:02:27.206
This to me is like,

02:02:27.206 --> 02:02:29.542
maybe don't drink this all in one sitting.

02:02:29.542 --> 02:02:31.610
"While in Europe at the Spanish MotoGP,

02:02:31.610 --> 02:02:33.379
we saw a cool resealable can

02:02:33.379 --> 02:02:35.081
and knew it'd be perfect for Monster.

02:02:35.081 --> 02:02:37.383
The can company said,
'only available in Europe.'

02:02:37.383 --> 02:02:39.352
We said, 'BS, we gotta have it!'"

02:02:39.352 --> 02:02:41.354
Whoa, Monster with the attitude!

02:02:41.354 --> 02:02:43.889
"If you can open and close
the can with one hand,

02:02:43.889 --> 02:02:46.692
you probably dated a lot
when you were younger."

02:02:46.692 --> 02:02:48.828
I mean, everyone who's
drinking this is 12.

02:02:48.828 --> 02:02:50.396
[grunts quietly]

02:02:50.396 --> 02:02:51.530
How European.

02:02:51.530 --> 02:02:53.332
The next category is Juiced Monster.

02:02:53.332 --> 02:02:54.400
Well, in the UK, it's "Juiced,"

02:02:54.400 --> 02:02:56.402
whereas in the US, it
seems to just be "Juice."

02:02:56.402 --> 02:02:57.670
We do share most of our flavors

02:02:57.670 --> 02:02:58.771
with the US in this category,

02:02:58.771 --> 02:03:00.239
but we do have one or
two which are different.

02:03:00.239 --> 02:03:02.274
We'll start over here, with Ripper.

02:03:02.274 --> 02:03:03.476
When I first saw the can and the name,

02:03:03.476 --> 02:03:04.910
I thought it was, like, based on Australia

02:03:04.910 --> 02:03:07.947
because "you little ripper" is
a kind of Australian saying,

02:03:07.947 --> 02:03:09.115
but then I read the can, and it turns out

02:03:09.115 --> 02:03:11.817
it's actually based on
Hawaii and surf culture.

02:03:11.817 --> 02:03:14.387
Sometimes you gotta just
throw a shrimp on the barbie

02:03:14.387 --> 02:03:16.255
and say "you little ripper."

02:03:18.424 --> 02:03:20.693
Ooh. This one smells nice.

02:03:23.095 --> 02:03:24.630
I've sipped, like, two of these,

02:03:24.630 --> 02:03:25.965
and I have a headache already,

02:03:25.965 --> 02:03:27.166
so this is going well.

02:03:27.166 --> 02:03:29.268
Then we have one which seems
to be a linguistic difference.

02:03:29.268 --> 02:03:31.570
In the UK, you can find Juiced Monarch.

02:03:31.570 --> 02:03:33.572
The can is adorned with
loads of butterflies,

02:03:33.572 --> 02:03:35.641
and monarch is, of course,
a type of butterfly.

02:03:35.641 --> 02:03:37.743
Whereas in the US, you guys have Papillon,

02:03:37.743 --> 02:03:39.078
which is French for butterfly,

02:03:39.078 --> 02:03:40.846
so I'm not really sure what
they've gone for there.

02:03:40.846 --> 02:03:43.249
Obviously the UK has a monarch,
so maybe we've just gone

02:03:43.249 --> 02:03:45.117
for the kind of royal theme
on this one. Who knows?

02:03:48.154 --> 02:03:50.122
Ooh, I actually like that one.

02:03:50.122 --> 02:03:51.257
Oh, no, this is bad.

02:03:51.257 --> 02:03:52.391
I can't like these.

02:03:55.528 --> 02:03:57.229
I'm not ready for an energy-drink era.

02:03:57.229 --> 02:04:00.199
Our Juice Monster drinks
come in two exclusives.

02:04:00.199 --> 02:04:01.834
The Juice Monster Papillon,

02:04:01.834 --> 02:04:04.103
butterfly-themed, like UK Monarch.

02:04:07.940 --> 02:04:10.409
I mean, I don't want
to keep drinking this,

02:04:10.409 --> 02:04:13.145
but if I had to, I wouldn't be as upset.

02:04:13.145 --> 02:04:15.948
Aussie lemonade style.

02:04:15.948 --> 02:04:18.350
It's like, I'm surprised these
don't have alcohol in them,

02:04:18.350 --> 02:04:20.686
because it smells like
an alcoholic beverage.

02:04:20.686 --> 02:04:21.520
Oof.

02:04:23.389 --> 02:04:24.723
Actually, I do like this.

02:04:24.723 --> 02:04:26.625
It's not bad. Because it
tastes just like lemonade.

02:04:29.028 --> 02:04:31.263
Like, I'm drinking it, it
doesn't feel like I'm also, like,

02:04:31.263 --> 02:04:32.998
getting energized to fight.

02:04:32.998 --> 02:04:35.501
Next up, we have some
coffee-and-Monster hybrid beverages.

02:04:35.501 --> 02:04:38.037
In the UK on the website it
shows us Espresso Monster,

02:04:38.037 --> 02:04:40.072
and these include
espresso and milk Monster,

02:04:40.072 --> 02:04:41.841
vanilla Espresso Monster,

02:04:41.841 --> 02:04:43.742
and salted caramel Espresso Monster.

02:04:43.742 --> 02:04:45.444
However, if you click the buy-now link,

02:04:45.444 --> 02:04:46.846
it takes you to a page
where it doesn't actually

02:04:46.846 --> 02:04:48.347
seem to contain any Espresso Monster,

02:04:48.347 --> 02:04:50.382
so I'm not sure if they've
been discontinued or not.

02:04:50.382 --> 02:04:53.185
I was able to track down the
espresso and milk Monster

02:04:53.185 --> 02:04:54.019
on the internet.

02:04:55.955 --> 02:04:57.890
To be fair, that just
tastes like a coffee.

02:04:57.890 --> 02:04:59.725
I don't get any kind of
Monstery elements from that,

02:04:59.725 --> 02:05:01.227
which I think is a good thing.

02:05:01.227 --> 02:05:02.628
The pace at which I talk is just going to

02:05:02.628 --> 02:05:04.763
increase and increase
throughout this episode.

02:05:04.763 --> 02:05:07.967
We have the coffee Monster in the US too.

02:05:07.967 --> 02:05:11.070
However, over here we
call it the Java Monster,

02:05:11.070 --> 02:05:12.571
and there's so many flavors.

02:05:12.571 --> 02:05:16.742
Got the Java Monster
Mean Bean, the Loca Moca,

02:05:16.742 --> 02:05:19.111
Kona blend, the Irish blend,

02:05:19.111 --> 02:05:21.280
the low-cal vanilla light,

02:05:21.280 --> 02:05:23.849
the salted caramel, the Swiss chocolate,

02:05:23.849 --> 02:05:27.019
the cold brew latte, the
cold brew sweet black,

02:05:27.019 --> 02:05:29.755
the farmer's oats vegan coffee,

02:05:29.755 --> 02:05:31.924
and the 300 triple-shot mocha

02:05:31.924 --> 02:05:34.026
and 300 triple-shot French vanilla.

02:05:34.026 --> 02:05:37.363
I, of course, got the 300 triple-shot ...

02:05:37.363 --> 02:05:38.464
mocha, aha.

02:05:38.464 --> 02:05:40.533
"300 milligrams of natural caffeine."

02:05:40.533 --> 02:05:41.700
Oh, go to hell.

02:05:41.700 --> 02:05:43.402
"Robust coffee plus cream."

02:05:43.402 --> 02:05:44.637
Oh, there's cream in this?

02:05:44.637 --> 02:05:46.105
Says "SHAKE IT UP."

02:05:46.105 --> 02:05:47.740
So I'm shaking it.

02:05:49.041 --> 02:05:50.442
[can fizzing]

02:05:50.442 --> 02:05:52.311
Why did it have me shake it up?

02:05:52.311 --> 02:05:53.345
Why did it have me shake it up?

02:06:01.186 --> 02:06:02.555
If you're at work and you see

02:06:02.555 --> 02:06:04.723
any one of your fellow
employees walking in

02:06:04.723 --> 02:06:06.859
in the morning drinking one of these,

02:06:06.859 --> 02:06:08.260
like, it's time to find a new job,

02:06:08.260 --> 02:06:10.963
'cause that guy is going to flip out.

02:06:10.963 --> 02:06:13.332
Bad. Is there a napkin somewhere?

02:06:13.332 --> 02:06:15.901
Next up, we've got Monster HydroSport.

02:06:15.901 --> 02:06:18.103
Now, these are a range of
noncarbonated energy drinks

02:06:18.103 --> 02:06:19.638
which are kind of targeted at athletes,

02:06:19.638 --> 02:06:22.408
similarly to a Powerade-
or Gatorade-style beverage.

02:06:22.408 --> 02:06:23.976
In the UK, they come in three flavors,

02:06:23.976 --> 02:06:26.078
Charge, Striker, and Hang Time.

02:06:26.078 --> 02:06:27.379
I've only got the Charge flavor today.

02:06:27.379 --> 02:06:28.714
You can find the other
ones on the internet,

02:06:28.714 --> 02:06:30.282
but we're going to give this one a try.

02:06:31.383 --> 02:06:34.186
It's got a really strong,
slightly chemical smell to it.

02:06:36.288 --> 02:06:38.123
But the taste actually isn't that bad.

02:06:38.123 --> 02:06:39.758
In the UK, we drink a lot of squash,

02:06:39.758 --> 02:06:42.394
which is kind of like a
concentrated-juice thing

02:06:42.394 --> 02:06:43.729
that then you dilute with water.

02:06:43.729 --> 02:06:46.432
It just tastes like a
kind of strong squash.

02:06:46.432 --> 02:06:47.967
We're making a mess.

02:06:47.967 --> 02:06:49.134
All right.

02:06:49.134 --> 02:06:54.139
"Monster HydroSport. Energy
water for advanced hydration."

02:06:54.139 --> 02:06:56.375
OK. They have a bunch of these.

02:06:56.375 --> 02:06:58.143
This is the Tropical Thunder.

02:06:59.378 --> 02:07:00.646
OK. Oof. Oof!

02:07:01.847 --> 02:07:03.048
That looks exactly like Gatorade.

02:07:03.048 --> 02:07:04.149
Ugh!

02:07:07.186 --> 02:07:08.520
Oh, this is bad.

02:07:09.655 --> 02:07:10.723
Blech.

02:07:10.723 --> 02:07:11.957
Purple Passion.

02:07:15.894 --> 02:07:16.996
Mm!

02:07:18.697 --> 02:07:20.199
This one's, like, really bad.

02:07:20.199 --> 02:07:21.433
But the second it touched my lips,

02:07:21.433 --> 02:07:22.735
I was like, I've made a mistake.

02:07:22.735 --> 02:07:26.505
Other hydro flavors include
super sport Killer Kiwi,

02:07:26.505 --> 02:07:31.076
super sport Macho Mango,
super sport Red Dawn,

02:07:31.076 --> 02:07:33.078
super sport Blue Streak,

02:07:33.078 --> 02:07:34.947
Blue Ice, watermelon,

02:07:34.947 --> 02:07:39.151
and hydro zero sugar, aka hydro white.

02:07:39.151 --> 02:07:41.153
We have a couple of Monster product ranges

02:07:41.153 --> 02:07:42.121
you won't find in the UK,

02:07:42.121 --> 02:07:44.690
starting with the Rehab Monsters.

02:07:44.690 --> 02:07:46.225
These claim to help you, quote,

02:07:46.225 --> 02:07:48.527
"refresh, recover, and revive," end quote,

02:07:48.527 --> 02:07:50.029
after a "heavy night."

02:07:50.029 --> 02:07:51.196
Yeah. Right.

02:07:51.196 --> 02:07:53.332
The two that I was able to get my hands on

02:07:53.332 --> 02:07:56.235
are strawberry lemonade and tea lemonade.

02:07:56.235 --> 02:07:57.569
The lemonades.

02:08:01.106 --> 02:08:02.174
Yeah, no.

02:08:04.276 --> 02:08:07.613
Yep. Looks exactly like the
first piss in the morning.

02:08:10.182 --> 02:08:11.150
Maybe the worst one.

02:08:11.150 --> 02:08:13.719
OK. So we have Rehab Monster watermelon.

02:08:13.719 --> 02:08:16.055
You can also get a
Rehab Monster peach tea,

02:08:16.055 --> 02:08:19.258
an orangeade, and a raspberry tea.

02:08:19.258 --> 02:08:20.492
I was actually able to
track one of these down

02:08:20.492 --> 02:08:21.827
on the UK Amazon website.

02:08:21.827 --> 02:08:24.029
It seems to have been imported
from somewhere in Europe.

02:08:24.029 --> 02:08:25.264
I think maybe Italy.

02:08:29.468 --> 02:08:30.469
Quite bad.

02:08:30.469 --> 02:08:32.938
I'm at that age now where
I'm always hungover.

02:08:32.938 --> 02:08:34.306
What about you, Harry?

02:08:34.306 --> 02:08:35.607
I'll be honest. If I'm hungover,

02:08:35.607 --> 02:08:37.576
I'm going to go for a giant
full English breakfast,

02:08:37.576 --> 02:08:38.977
loads of salt-and-vinegar crisps,

02:08:38.977 --> 02:08:40.813
and then about 2 liters of fizzy water

02:08:40.813 --> 02:08:42.081
instead of one of these.

02:08:42.081 --> 02:08:45.884
Then our final exclusive
range is Muscle Monster.

02:08:45.884 --> 02:08:49.321
These are exclusive shakes
with 27 grams of protein

02:08:49.321 --> 02:08:51.323
and 157 milligrams of caffeine.

02:08:51.323 --> 02:08:52.324
You can get them in

02:08:52.324 --> 02:08:54.393
vanilla Muscle Monster energy drink shake

02:08:54.393 --> 02:08:57.162
and chocolate Muscle Monster energy shake.

02:08:57.162 --> 02:08:57.996
I don't have them.

02:08:57.996 --> 02:08:59.665
It's probably a good thing.

02:08:59.665 --> 02:09:01.066
Now, we can't get Muscle
Monster in the UK,

02:09:01.066 --> 02:09:03.702
but I'm pretty sure I can just
combine a standard Monster

02:09:03.702 --> 02:09:04.770
with some protein powder

02:09:04.770 --> 02:09:06.605
and it'll have the same effect, right?

02:09:06.605 --> 02:09:09.775
Of many bad ideas that
I've had on this show,

02:09:09.775 --> 02:09:10.976
this might be the worst.

02:09:12.077 --> 02:09:14.880
I'm going to go with 2
teaspoons of protein powder.

02:09:17.249 --> 02:09:18.217
[laughs]

02:09:18.217 --> 02:09:19.618
Charlie: That's the good stuff.

02:09:19.618 --> 02:09:22.688
Harry: What on earth am
I doing with my life?

02:09:22.688 --> 02:09:25.023
It's just currently kind
of formed a big layer.

02:09:26.358 --> 02:09:28.060
There we go.

02:09:28.060 --> 02:09:30.062
I hope you can see how
badly this has gone.

02:09:30.062 --> 02:09:31.764
This is not mixed at all.

02:09:31.764 --> 02:09:33.365
Ah, dear God.

02:09:42.441 --> 02:09:43.709
Oh, God.

02:09:43.709 --> 02:09:45.944
I think the texture's more
off-putting than the taste.

02:09:45.944 --> 02:09:48.714
It's just like drinking a
really grainy Monster Energy.

02:09:48.714 --> 02:09:51.016
The things we do for
gains! Am I right, guys?

02:09:53.852 --> 02:09:56.288
Monster Energy products
have some ingredients

02:09:56.288 --> 02:09:58.457
that you might want to watch out for.

02:09:58.457 --> 02:09:59.658
But there are also lessons to be learned

02:09:59.658 --> 02:10:00.993
about ingredients which sound scary

02:10:00.993 --> 02:10:02.628
but are actually completely fine.

02:10:02.628 --> 02:10:03.829
Here's the ingredients list

02:10:03.829 --> 02:10:06.698
as it appears on a can of
classic Monster Energy drink.

02:10:06.698 --> 02:10:08.200
I haven't even opened this yet.

02:10:08.200 --> 02:10:11.103
Cheers, guys! I mean, it's
classic Monster Energy.

02:10:12.371 --> 02:10:13.338
Oh, it's poison.

02:10:13.338 --> 02:10:14.206
Here's the ingredients list

02:10:14.206 --> 02:10:17.643
as it appears on a can of
classic Monster Energy in the UK.

02:10:17.643 --> 02:10:18.644
Let's start with the good news.

02:10:18.644 --> 02:10:19.845
If you take a look at the US list,

02:10:19.845 --> 02:10:21.480
you might see some
scary-sounding chemicals,

02:10:21.480 --> 02:10:24.116
like pyridoxine
hydrochloride or niacinamide,

02:10:24.116 --> 02:10:26.652
which seemingly aren't
present in the UK drink.

02:10:26.652 --> 02:10:28.387
However, these are
actually just full names

02:10:28.387 --> 02:10:30.989
for vitamins B6 and B3, respectively.

02:10:30.989 --> 02:10:33.525
Same goes for riboflavin and cyn --

02:10:33.525 --> 02:10:37.062
cyanocobalamin in the US list,

02:10:37.062 --> 02:10:40.399
which is just vitamin B2 and B12.

02:10:40.399 --> 02:10:42.234
In fact, when you go through
the list and edit a couple

02:10:42.234 --> 02:10:44.269
of other linguistic differences,
you'll see that the drinks

02:10:44.269 --> 02:10:46.238
are actually identical
in the two countries.

02:10:46.238 --> 02:10:47.906
That's not the only thing we can demystify

02:10:47.906 --> 02:10:49.174
about Monster, either.

02:10:49.174 --> 02:10:51.844
Oh, God. I'm, like, seething
from all this energy drink. Ah!

02:10:51.844 --> 02:10:53.912
Yu may notice that
Monster contains taurine.

02:10:53.912 --> 02:10:55.647
For years, taurine has been the subject of

02:10:55.647 --> 02:10:57.850
some inaccurate rumors on social media.

02:10:57.850 --> 02:11:01.119
Inaccurate rumors on social media? Huh?

02:11:01.119 --> 02:11:02.788
Taurine has been associated with bulls,

02:11:02.788 --> 02:11:05.123
drawing its name from the Latin "taurus."

02:11:05.123 --> 02:11:07.426
Some people ran with
this and falsely claimed

02:11:07.426 --> 02:11:09.261
that taurine is code for bull semen.

02:11:09.261 --> 02:11:11.597
You have to be an absolute idiot

02:11:11.597 --> 02:11:13.832
to think that taurine
is bull semen. Period.

02:11:13.832 --> 02:11:15.100
The name taurine comes from the fact

02:11:15.100 --> 02:11:17.603
that it was actually first
isolated from ox bile.

02:11:17.603 --> 02:11:19.538
Even more good news is
that there's no ox spit

02:11:19.538 --> 02:11:21.607
in Monster energy either,
as most food producers

02:11:21.607 --> 02:11:23.275
will now use a synthetic
version of taurine

02:11:23.275 --> 02:11:24.643
that isn't derived from animals.

02:11:24.643 --> 02:11:27.079
This also means that Monster
is suitable for vegans.

02:11:27.079 --> 02:11:29.147
OK. Enough defending Monster.

02:11:29.147 --> 02:11:31.250
What about the more
questionable ingredients?

02:11:31.250 --> 02:11:32.918
Some of the flavors of
Monster Energy drink

02:11:32.918 --> 02:11:35.187
contain controversial food colors.

02:11:35.187 --> 02:11:38.991
For instance, all these four
bad boys have red 40 in them.

02:11:38.991 --> 02:11:41.660
This is the visual representation

02:11:41.660 --> 02:11:44.029
of children bouncing off the walls.

02:11:44.029 --> 02:11:45.998
All these got the red 40 in it.

02:11:45.998 --> 02:11:50.102
Let's do a power
controversial-chemical taste test.

02:11:53.138 --> 02:11:53.972
Bad.

02:11:55.374 --> 02:11:56.275
Mm-mm.

02:11:56.275 --> 02:11:57.442
Somehow worse.

02:11:59.211 --> 02:12:00.879
I don't know what flowers taste like.

02:12:00.879 --> 02:12:01.713
Definitely not that.

02:12:05.984 --> 02:12:07.019
That's kind of nice, actually.

02:12:07.019 --> 02:12:08.320
This other one that's sitting over here,

02:12:08.320 --> 02:12:09.621
if you're wondering why it's over here --

02:12:09.621 --> 02:12:11.123
sorry I'm talking, like,
superfast all of a sudden.

02:12:11.123 --> 02:12:12.057
This s--- is really kicking in.

02:12:12.057 --> 02:12:14.760
So, this section, the US portion of this,

02:12:14.760 --> 02:12:17.462
is completely disorganized.
It's 100% my fault.

02:12:17.462 --> 02:12:20.065
This, I just, what? I, what time is it?

02:12:20.065 --> 02:12:22.000
These are food colorings
derived from petroleum.

02:12:22.000 --> 02:12:23.468
They've received a lot
of attention in the past

02:12:23.468 --> 02:12:25.137
due to concerns about their safety.

02:12:25.137 --> 02:12:28.140
In 2007, a study from UK
academics linked red 40,

02:12:28.140 --> 02:12:30.275
yellow 5, and yellow 6 to hyperactivity

02:12:30.275 --> 02:12:31.944
in children, possibly adults,

02:12:31.944 --> 02:12:35.580
and yellow No. 5 to also being
linked to causing cancer.

02:12:35.580 --> 02:12:39.551
Gimme a dancing rat,
sometime now. Thank you.

02:12:39.551 --> 02:12:41.153
While these dyes aren't illegal in the UK,

02:12:41.153 --> 02:12:43.055
most manufacturers will
still avoid using them

02:12:43.055 --> 02:12:44.456
because if they are added to food,

02:12:44.456 --> 02:12:45.557
they must bear a warning label

02:12:45.557 --> 02:12:47.292
which says that they "may
have an adverse effect

02:12:47.292 --> 02:12:49.328
on activity and attention in children."

02:12:49.328 --> 02:12:51.029
However, as Monster
Energy is kind of designed

02:12:51.029 --> 02:12:51.930
to make you hyperactive,

02:12:51.930 --> 02:12:54.466
it's one of the UK products
which still uses one of them.

02:12:54.466 --> 02:12:56.702
Our Pipeline Punch and
Ultra Red Monster Energies

02:12:56.702 --> 02:13:00.172
contain red 40, listed on
the ingredients as E129,

02:13:00.172 --> 02:13:01.540
which means that they also have to have

02:13:01.540 --> 02:13:03.108
the hyperactivity warning.

02:13:03.108 --> 02:13:04.843
The good news for the UK
is that we stopped short

02:13:04.843 --> 02:13:07.145
of using yellow 5 and
yellow 6 in our Monster,

02:13:07.145 --> 02:13:08.647
instead opting to use carotenes

02:13:08.647 --> 02:13:10.816
to color our yellow
drinks, like Mango Loco.

02:13:10.816 --> 02:13:12.517
There was one other
Monster-based controversy

02:13:12.517 --> 02:13:14.019
that came out of the British Isles.

02:13:14.019 --> 02:13:16.188
The Republic of Ireland pulled
four varieties of Monster

02:13:16.188 --> 02:13:18.924
from its shelves, including Juiced Ripper

02:13:18.924 --> 02:13:21.727
and Lewis Hamilton 44, after
they were found to contain

02:13:21.727 --> 02:13:23.829
excessive levels of propylene glycol.

02:13:23.829 --> 02:13:25.597
Propylene gly-sol --

02:13:25.597 --> 02:13:26.898
no idea if that's how it's pronounced --

02:13:26.898 --> 02:13:31.603
is a chemical which has GRAS,
Generally Recognized as Safe,

02:13:31.603 --> 02:13:33.271
status from the FDA.

02:13:33.271 --> 02:13:35.841
It's mostly used in food as a humectant,

02:13:35.841 --> 02:13:37.275
which is something which absorbs water

02:13:37.275 --> 02:13:39.778
and provides additional nutritional value.

02:13:39.778 --> 02:13:41.747
It has been the subject of
some hysteria in the past.

02:13:41.747 --> 02:13:43.448
It's one of the main
ingredients of antifreeze,

02:13:43.448 --> 02:13:45.550
as it can lower the
freezing points of liquids.

02:13:45.550 --> 02:13:46.418
However, this level of it

02:13:46.418 --> 02:13:48.253
would never be found in food or drink.

02:13:48.253 --> 02:13:49.855
Even the Food Safety Authority of Ireland

02:13:49.855 --> 02:13:50.956
said that the products it recalled

02:13:50.956 --> 02:13:53.091
were unlikely to cause
any harmful effects.

02:13:55.961 --> 02:13:57.496
Let's talk about caffeine for a second.

02:13:57.496 --> 02:13:59.164
Regular Monster Energy in the UK contains

02:13:59.164 --> 02:14:01.700
32 milligrams of caffeine
per 100 milliliters,

02:14:01.700 --> 02:14:05.103
which equates to 160 milligrams
in a 500-milliliter can.

02:14:05.103 --> 02:14:07.639
A regular can of Monster Energy in the US

02:14:07.639 --> 02:14:10.609
also contains 160 milligrams of caffeine,

02:14:10.609 --> 02:14:13.211
with a slightly higher
figure per milliliter

02:14:13.211 --> 02:14:15.747
than the UK, as our cans
are slightly smaller.

02:14:15.747 --> 02:14:17.949
That's roughly equivalent
to two shots of espresso,

02:14:17.949 --> 02:14:19.584
which usually contain around 80 milligrams

02:14:19.584 --> 02:14:20.819
of caffeine per shot.

02:14:20.819 --> 02:14:22.087
If we're talking per milliliter,

02:14:22.087 --> 02:14:23.655
the UK's most caffeinated Monster

02:14:23.655 --> 02:14:25.390
would be the Espresso
Monster, which contains

02:14:25.390 --> 02:14:27.993
around 60 milligrams of
caffeine per 100 milliliters,

02:14:27.993 --> 02:14:29.928
almost double the amount
of the normal can.

02:14:29.928 --> 02:14:33.098
However, the UK espresso cans
are only 250 milliliters.

02:14:33.098 --> 02:14:35.233
That means that a whole can
of that contains less caffeine

02:14:35.233 --> 02:14:37.069
than a whole can of the classic version.

02:14:37.069 --> 02:14:39.871
The most caffeinated US Monster drink

02:14:39.871 --> 02:14:43.875
is the Monster Java 300 line,
which, as the name suggests,

02:14:43.875 --> 02:14:47.946
contains 300 milligrams of
caffeine per 15 fluid ounces.

02:14:47.946 --> 02:14:49.548
Good God.

02:14:49.548 --> 02:14:51.550
At what point does caffeine
intake become a problem?

02:14:51.550 --> 02:14:54.286
The FDA says that 400
milligrams per day is generally

02:14:54.286 --> 02:14:56.755
not associated with
dangerous negative effects.

02:14:56.755 --> 02:14:58.723
But if you consume 1,200 milligrams

02:14:58.723 --> 02:15:00.192
in a relatively short amount of time,

02:15:00.192 --> 02:15:01.693
that's when the toxic effects

02:15:01.693 --> 02:15:03.795
like seizures can start to be observed.

02:15:03.795 --> 02:15:06.498
According to the FDA,
the life-threatening dose

02:15:06.498 --> 02:15:09.601
of caffeine is 10 grams of caffeine.

02:15:09.601 --> 02:15:12.237
It's surprisingly easy to get
ahold of pure caffeine online.

02:15:12.237 --> 02:15:13.538
Without any age verification,

02:15:13.538 --> 02:15:16.074
I was able to pay £23
for 100 grams of this,

02:15:16.074 --> 02:15:18.310
which is theoretically
enough to kill 10 people.

02:15:18.310 --> 02:15:20.312
We're going to weigh out
a 10-gram lethal dosage

02:15:20.312 --> 02:15:22.280
just to show you how
little that actually is.

02:15:22.280 --> 02:15:24.783
So that is roughly what's in there.

02:15:24.783 --> 02:15:26.351
That right there is more
than enough caffeine

02:15:26.351 --> 02:15:28.053
to kill a normal human being.

02:15:28.053 --> 02:15:29.821
That was about 4 teaspoons' worth.

02:15:29.821 --> 02:15:31.556
What's this like in terms
of Monster Energies?

02:15:31.556 --> 02:15:33.592
Well, in the UK, you'd
need to drink 7.5 of them

02:15:33.592 --> 02:15:35.727
to hit the 1,200-milligram mark,

02:15:35.727 --> 02:15:37.629
but you'd need to drink 62.5 of them

02:15:37.629 --> 02:15:38.964
to hit the 10-gram mark.

02:15:38.964 --> 02:15:40.365
This means you'd be unlikely to die

02:15:40.365 --> 02:15:41.500
just from the caffeine on its own,

02:15:41.500 --> 02:15:43.235
but it's not unrealistic
that you may experience

02:15:43.235 --> 02:15:45.670
some negative side effects
if you consume a lot of it.

02:15:45.670 --> 02:15:47.372
It's a little more risky here in the US.

02:15:47.372 --> 02:15:49.808
Just four of these Java 300 cans

02:15:49.808 --> 02:15:51.376
can put you at risk of seizures,

02:15:51.376 --> 02:15:54.446
although it'd probably take
33.3 of them to kill you.

02:15:54.446 --> 02:15:55.981
Yeah. And you wouldn't die from caffeine.

02:15:55.981 --> 02:15:58.283
You'd die from, like,
your bladder exploding

02:15:58.283 --> 02:15:59.618
from having 33 of these.

02:15:59.618 --> 02:16:01.186
How many fluid ounces is that?

02:16:01.186 --> 02:16:03.188
What's 15 times 33?

02:16:03.188 --> 02:16:05.056
It's worth mentioning that
in some stores across the UK,

02:16:05.056 --> 02:16:06.424
they will ask for proof of age

02:16:06.424 --> 02:16:08.026
in order to let you buy Monster Energy.

02:16:08.026 --> 02:16:09.895
I got ID'd in a co-op trying
to buy some of the drinks

02:16:09.895 --> 02:16:11.763
for this video. There's
no legal restriction

02:16:11.763 --> 02:16:13.198
on the sale of energy drinks in the UK,

02:16:13.198 --> 02:16:15.000
and they can technically
be sold to anyone,

02:16:15.000 --> 02:16:16.735
but some retailers will
voluntarily cap them

02:16:16.735 --> 02:16:18.303
to people over the age of 16.

02:16:18.303 --> 02:16:19.571
This is a fantastic idea.

02:16:19.571 --> 02:16:21.006
I'm not kidding. We should be doing that.

02:16:21.006 --> 02:16:23.942
The age restriction is, no
one over 25 drinks this crap.

02:16:23.942 --> 02:16:26.478
You stop drinking this when you get, like,

02:16:26.478 --> 02:16:28.813
I don't know, responsibilities.

02:16:28.813 --> 02:16:31.016
There's also a large amount
of sugar in Monster Energy.

02:16:31.016 --> 02:16:34.686
In the UK, a 500-milliliter
can contains 55 grams of sugar.

02:16:34.686 --> 02:16:36.888
That's 110% of your daily allowance.

02:16:36.888 --> 02:16:39.024
A 16-ounce can in the US

02:16:39.024 --> 02:16:40.959
contains 54 grams of sugar,

02:16:40.959 --> 02:16:43.428
which is 108% your daily allowance.

02:16:43.428 --> 02:16:45.931
Now remember, though, the US cans contains

02:16:45.931 --> 02:16:48.967
around 30 milliliters
less than the UK can,

02:16:48.967 --> 02:16:51.236
so proportionately speaking,
they're basically the same.

02:16:51.870 --> 02:16:53.471
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

02:16:53.471 --> 02:16:54.940
we wanted to find out
all of the differences

02:16:54.940 --> 02:16:57.175
between Skittles in the UK and the US.

02:16:57.175 --> 02:16:58.476
This is "Food Wars."

02:17:01.046 --> 02:17:03.114
In the UK, Skittles come
in the following sizes.

02:17:03.114 --> 02:17:05.750
Firstly, we have these
18-gram fun-size pouches.

02:17:05.750 --> 02:17:08.119
Then we have these 45-gram bags.

02:17:09.020 --> 02:17:11.890
Then we have a 125-gram bag of Skittles.

02:17:11.890 --> 02:17:13.658
Next up is what I think
is the most common size,

02:17:13.658 --> 02:17:15.827
a 152-gram bag.

02:17:15.827 --> 02:17:19.097
Then we have this one, a
350-gram More to Share bag.

02:17:19.097 --> 02:17:21.466
Then we have our largest
size, which is this one,

02:17:21.466 --> 02:17:24.869
a 1-kilogram big-size share bag.

02:17:24.869 --> 02:17:28.273
And in the US, our Skittles
come in these sizes.

02:17:28.273 --> 02:17:31.309
First one, the fun size, 10 grams.

02:17:31.309 --> 02:17:34.512
We got the 61.5-gram bag.

02:17:34.512 --> 02:17:37.315
Next size, 99-gram box.

02:17:37.315 --> 02:17:39.150
Yeah, the movie-theater pack.

02:17:39.150 --> 02:17:43.154
The share size. 113.4 grams.

02:17:43.154 --> 02:17:46.725
First of the resealable bags, 204.1 grams.

02:17:46.725 --> 02:17:51.363
This is the Grab n Go. 255.1 grams.

02:17:51.363 --> 02:17:56.368
Next bag up, the sharing
size. 442.3 grams.

02:17:56.801 --> 02:17:58.303
The family size.

02:17:58.303 --> 02:18:00.939
779.6 grams.

02:18:02.774 --> 02:18:06.077
Hey, good enough for the
Egyptians, good enough for us.

02:18:06.077 --> 02:18:08.146
Pyramids. Check 'em out.

02:18:08.146 --> 02:18:10.148
This is the party pouch.

02:18:10.148 --> 02:18:12.083
1.4 kilograms.

02:18:12.083 --> 02:18:14.085
Have you noticed how good I
am at pouring these Skittles?

02:18:14.085 --> 02:18:15.520
Can this be a job?

02:18:15.520 --> 02:18:17.789
Forgive me for getting cocky,
but I haven't spilled one.

02:18:22.527 --> 02:18:24.663
The biggest size, the jar.

02:18:24.663 --> 02:18:27.399
1.53 kilograms.

02:18:31.569 --> 02:18:33.538
Now, for "Food Wars" and
general-consumer purposes,

02:18:33.538 --> 02:18:35.240
the 1-kilogram bag is our largest size.

02:18:35.240 --> 02:18:38.176
However, I did want to point out this one.

02:18:38.176 --> 02:18:40.178
A 1.6-kilogram bulk bag.

02:18:40.178 --> 02:18:41.513
I was able to find one of these online

02:18:41.513 --> 02:18:42.681
without too much hassle.

02:18:42.681 --> 02:18:43.915
I think they are more aimed at, like,

02:18:43.915 --> 02:18:46.284
vending-machine refills
rather than consumers,

02:18:46.284 --> 02:18:48.987
but yeah, you are able to
get 1.6 kilograms of Skittles

02:18:48.987 --> 02:18:51.456
if you are a massive
Skittles fan in the UK.

02:18:51.456 --> 02:18:52.924
We also wanted to check how many Skittles

02:18:52.924 --> 02:18:53.758
you're getting in each country

02:18:53.758 --> 02:18:55.927
and also if there is
even color distribution.

02:18:55.927 --> 02:18:58.096
So Joe has asked me to
count all of the Skittles

02:18:58.096 --> 02:18:59.631
in a 1-kilogram pouch,

02:18:59.631 --> 02:19:00.932
and we're also going to
sort them into colors

02:19:00.932 --> 02:19:01.833
to see how many you get.

02:19:01.833 --> 02:19:04.069
What color order makes the
most sense in your head?

02:19:04.069 --> 02:19:05.904
The rainbow actually
has a order of colors?

02:19:10.008 --> 02:19:12.911
To me, it's a bit of a ROYGBIV vibe.

02:19:12.911 --> 02:19:14.946
I'm more of a CMYK guy.

02:19:14.946 --> 02:19:18.616
Harry sorts Skittles and
asks you about your day ASMR.

02:19:19.484 --> 02:19:21.353
Ah! Whoops. Whoo, God!

02:19:21.353 --> 02:19:23.555
They're all running around
like they have personalities.

02:19:23.555 --> 02:19:26.725
Just keep seeing the
yellow-and-purple color combination

02:19:26.725 --> 02:19:28.493
and thinking of Waluigi.

02:19:29.527 --> 02:19:31.563
Wah!

02:19:31.563 --> 02:19:34.099
Eh and eh. All right!

02:19:35.333 --> 02:19:36.701
OK, I have now sorted them by color.

02:19:36.701 --> 02:19:38.536
It was satisfying, but it did take ages.

02:19:38.536 --> 02:19:40.105
Now we have to count them all.

02:19:41.373 --> 02:19:42.707
So now we gotta count these.

02:19:42.707 --> 02:19:47.212
And I am not spending all
day counting these. So ...

02:19:47.212 --> 02:19:48.513
Two ... four ...

02:19:48.513 --> 02:19:51.683
I figure out how much 10 Skittles weigh.

02:19:51.683 --> 02:19:54.719
Jot that down. Step two, weigh each cup,

02:19:54.719 --> 02:19:56.454
obviously subtracting
the weight of the cup.

02:19:56.454 --> 02:19:59.657
And then take the weight
of each cup, divide by 10.

02:19:59.657 --> 02:20:01.226
Six ... eight ...

02:20:01.226 --> 02:20:02.193
Three, four ...

02:20:02.193 --> 02:20:03.294
Um ...

02:20:03.294 --> 02:20:04.362
Is ...

02:20:05.263 --> 02:20:07.465
After far too long spent
sorting and counting these,

02:20:07.465 --> 02:20:08.566
we have our final totals.

02:20:08.566 --> 02:20:09.934
I feel like we learned something,

02:20:09.934 --> 02:20:11.770
but I don't know exactly what.

02:20:11.770 --> 02:20:12.771
What have we learned from that?

02:20:12.771 --> 02:20:15.974
Uh, do not do that at home.
It's an enormous waste of time.

02:20:15.974 --> 02:20:17.509
We did some science today,

02:20:17.509 --> 02:20:19.744
and that is the most important thing.

02:20:21.179 --> 02:20:23.448
Now, while Skittles look the
same in both the US and the UK,

02:20:23.448 --> 02:20:24.916
the flavors are actually different.

02:20:24.916 --> 02:20:27.252
For example, in the UK, purple Skittles

02:20:27.252 --> 02:20:28.820
are flavored with black currant.

02:20:28.820 --> 02:20:32.657
Whereas in the US, our purple
Skittle is grape-flavored.

02:20:32.657 --> 02:20:34.426
Back in the early 20th century,

02:20:34.426 --> 02:20:37.095
the US government made it
illegal to grow black currants

02:20:37.095 --> 02:20:39.964
because it was spreading
a wood-destroying disease.

02:20:39.964 --> 02:20:42.200
Now, the ban has since been lifted,

02:20:42.200 --> 02:20:44.369
but black currants never
really became popular

02:20:44.369 --> 02:20:47.439
as a flavor here. Certainly
not as popular as grapes.

02:20:47.439 --> 02:20:49.140
When it comes to tropical
Skittles in the UK,

02:20:49.140 --> 02:20:51.109
the colors and flavors are as follows:

02:20:51.109 --> 02:20:54.913
pineapple, orange, fruit punch,

02:20:54.913 --> 02:20:56.948
watermelon, and passion fruit.

02:20:56.948 --> 02:20:59.751
In the US, our tropical
Skittles flavors are

02:20:59.751 --> 02:21:01.486
strawberry starfruit, which is pink;

02:21:01.486 --> 02:21:04.155
mango tangelo, which is orange;

02:21:04.155 --> 02:21:05.890
yellow is banana berry;

02:21:05.890 --> 02:21:06.991
we don't have a purple one,

02:21:06.991 --> 02:21:09.227
so we have a green one,
and it's kiwi lime;

02:21:09.227 --> 02:21:12.564
and last here is pineapple
passion fruit, which is blue.

02:21:12.564 --> 02:21:14.232
The Skittle-flavor controversies continue

02:21:14.232 --> 02:21:15.800
with our wild berry Skittles in the UK.

02:21:15.800 --> 02:21:17.302
A, because they contain cherry,

02:21:17.302 --> 02:21:18.770
which is very much not a berry,

02:21:18.770 --> 02:21:21.306
and also, B, because the
colors are all over the place.

02:21:21.306 --> 02:21:23.107
We have cherry in the purple one.

02:21:23.107 --> 02:21:25.577
Apple and blackberry with the green one.

02:21:25.577 --> 02:21:27.312
Blueberry is the light blue one.

02:21:27.312 --> 02:21:28.680
Raspberry is the pink one.

02:21:28.680 --> 02:21:30.915
And then strawberry and kiwi for the red.

02:21:30.915 --> 02:21:32.884
Our US wild berry
flavors are kind of close

02:21:32.884 --> 02:21:36.020
to that of the UK's, and we
too have a non-berry cherry.

02:21:36.020 --> 02:21:38.790
They even added "wild" onto the title,

02:21:38.790 --> 02:21:39.891
but I'm not buying it.

02:21:39.891 --> 02:21:40.859
Berry punch;

02:21:40.859 --> 02:21:42.861
the raspberry, which is
blue, I don't know why;

02:21:42.861 --> 02:21:45.363
wild cherry, which is not a berry;

02:21:45.363 --> 02:21:47.499
green guy is melon berry;

02:21:47.499 --> 02:21:50.101
and the pink one at the
end here is strawberry.

02:21:50.101 --> 02:21:51.469
Now, if I had anything to say about this,

02:21:51.469 --> 02:21:52.904
I think I'd mix the colors up as well.

02:21:52.904 --> 02:21:55.940
I think in my mind, blue
raspberry would make more sense

02:21:55.940 --> 02:21:57.876
than the current blue, which is blueberry.

02:21:57.876 --> 02:22:00.078
I think blueberry would make
more sense as the purple one,

02:22:00.078 --> 02:22:02.313
because as we all know, blueberry
isn't actually that blue.

02:22:02.313 --> 02:22:03.815
It is more of a purple color.

02:22:03.815 --> 02:22:07.218
And then I'd probably put
pink as the cherry one.

02:22:07.218 --> 02:22:10.021
Full-flavor lineup. Ah!

02:22:10.021 --> 02:22:12.357
And, of course, we have
Skittles Crazy Sours.

02:22:12.357 --> 02:22:13.591
In the UK, our sour Skittles

02:22:13.591 --> 02:22:15.193
are flavored and colored as follows:

02:22:15.193 --> 02:22:16.861
we have sour cherry as the purple one;

02:22:16.861 --> 02:22:18.863
we have sour green apple as the green one;

02:22:18.863 --> 02:22:20.732
sour pineapple is the yellow one;

02:22:20.732 --> 02:22:22.534
sour raspberry is the pink one;

02:22:22.534 --> 02:22:24.536
and sour mandarin is the orange one.

02:22:24.536 --> 02:22:27.572
In the US, Skittles sour
flavors are also different.

02:22:27.572 --> 02:22:29.173
So, purple is grape.

02:22:29.173 --> 02:22:30.742
This one says "the original lime-up,"

02:22:30.742 --> 02:22:32.143
so I'm assuming green is now lime.

02:22:32.143 --> 02:22:33.745
Yellow is lemon.

02:22:33.745 --> 02:22:34.946
Red, strawberry.

02:22:34.946 --> 02:22:35.847
And orange.

02:22:35.847 --> 02:22:37.649
Do you think they're as crazy-sour

02:22:37.649 --> 02:22:39.117
as the packaging suggests?

02:22:41.119 --> 02:22:41.953
Let's go.

02:22:42.887 --> 02:22:44.255
Oh, it's sour. Bleh!

02:22:44.255 --> 02:22:46.824
A very sane level of sour, unfortunately.

02:22:46.824 --> 02:22:48.293
We reached out to Skittles to ask them

02:22:48.293 --> 02:22:50.261
about their flavor choices,

02:22:50.261 --> 02:22:51.896
and they mentioned they had a variety

02:22:51.896 --> 02:22:53.331
of methods of picking flavors,

02:22:53.331 --> 02:22:55.333
one of which is something
called "social listening,"

02:22:55.333 --> 02:22:57.602
which I assume means
checking social media.

02:22:57.602 --> 02:23:00.138
And due to popular
complaints on social media,

02:23:00.138 --> 02:23:02.040
the green Skittle was changed back to lime

02:23:02.040 --> 02:23:04.609
after 10-ish years of being green apple.

02:23:04.609 --> 02:23:05.677
With all this talk about flavors,

02:23:05.677 --> 02:23:07.445
it's worth pointing out
that some people claim that

02:23:07.445 --> 02:23:09.881
all Skittles might
actually taste the same.

02:23:09.881 --> 02:23:12.917
In an interview with NPR,
neuropsychologist Don Katz,

02:23:12.917 --> 02:23:15.353
who specializes in taste, said, quote,

02:23:15.353 --> 02:23:17.555
"it is cheaper to make things
smell and look different

02:23:17.555 --> 02:23:19.724
than it is to make them
actually taste different."

02:23:19.724 --> 02:23:20.725
Dot dot dot.

02:23:20.725 --> 02:23:24.429
So, Skittles have different
fragrances and different colors,

02:23:24.429 --> 02:23:26.898
but they taste exactly the same.

02:23:26.898 --> 02:23:28.466
This shouldn't come as too
much of a surprise to you,

02:23:28.466 --> 02:23:30.068
because if you've been
stuffed up with a cold

02:23:30.068 --> 02:23:31.469
or with allergies, you might know that

02:23:31.469 --> 02:23:34.505
without your sense of smell,
food can seem rather tasteless.

02:23:34.505 --> 02:23:36.507
That's because our sense
of smell is responsible

02:23:36.507 --> 02:23:38.743
for a large percentage of what we taste.

02:23:38.743 --> 02:23:41.312
General flavors like
salty, bitter, sweet, sour,

02:23:41.312 --> 02:23:42.880
those can be detected without smell.

02:23:42.880 --> 02:23:44.782
However, more complex flavors,

02:23:44.782 --> 02:23:45.950
such as fruit,

02:23:45.950 --> 02:23:48.853
require both taste and
smell to be recognized.

02:23:48.853 --> 02:23:49.887
OK, Joe, I'm curious.

02:23:49.887 --> 02:23:51.422
Let me see how many of
these original Skittles

02:23:51.422 --> 02:23:54.392
I can identify correctly without
my sense of sight or smell.

02:23:54.392 --> 02:23:58.062
So now, with the assistance
of Yuelei or Joe,

02:23:58.062 --> 02:23:59.797
so I'm going to go ahead and do this,

02:23:59.797 --> 02:24:03.701
and can someone please place
five Skittles in front of me,

02:24:03.701 --> 02:24:05.403
and I will see if I can determine,

02:24:05.403 --> 02:24:07.138
mask goes over the nose,

02:24:07.138 --> 02:24:08.640
see if I can determine the flavor. Ready?

02:24:10.475 --> 02:24:12.310
No. I mean, it's my mouth and my face.

02:24:15.179 --> 02:24:18.483
Yuelei! What is going on
with my breath and my mouth

02:24:18.483 --> 02:24:19.817
that I'm getting pink eye?

02:24:21.486 --> 02:24:23.121
Harry: My God.

02:24:23.121 --> 02:24:24.288
You sicko.

02:24:24.288 --> 02:24:25.289
Joe: Hey, Joe, are you
going to try and trick me

02:24:25.289 --> 02:24:26.424
by repeating flavors?

02:24:28.226 --> 02:24:29.527
So, I've blindfolded myself.

02:24:29.527 --> 02:24:31.929
Charlie has set five
Skittles in front of me.

02:24:31.929 --> 02:24:33.197
I don't know which colors they are.

02:24:33.197 --> 02:24:35.233
I'm going to hold my
nose as well, eat them,

02:24:35.233 --> 02:24:36.401
and try and identify them.

02:24:36.401 --> 02:24:37.602
First one.

02:24:40.171 --> 02:24:41.606
Orange?

02:24:41.606 --> 02:24:44.409
All right, wish me luck.

02:24:44.409 --> 02:24:46.611
I really can't taste much.

02:24:46.611 --> 02:24:48.046
That's tough! Wow.

02:24:50.815 --> 02:24:51.749
I am literally just guessing.

02:24:51.749 --> 02:24:53.117
I'm going to go purple. I have no idea.

02:24:53.117 --> 02:24:54.919
All right, I need another one to compare.

02:24:56.487 --> 02:24:58.156
That is making it way, way harder.

02:24:58.156 --> 02:25:01.659
I think I got some more berry
vibes from the second one.

02:25:01.659 --> 02:25:03.227
I think the first one was a bit more sour,

02:25:03.227 --> 02:25:05.963
so it was either maybe an orange or lemon.

02:25:08.733 --> 02:25:09.801
I can't decide. Like, red?

02:25:13.071 --> 02:25:14.806
That one, I don't have a clue about.

02:25:16.908 --> 02:25:17.809
Lemon, yellow?

02:25:17.809 --> 02:25:18.943
Last one.

02:25:20.144 --> 02:25:21.045
Uh, red.

02:25:22.947 --> 02:25:24.582
This is way tougher than
I thought it would be.

02:25:24.582 --> 02:25:25.850
I'm going to go with, like,

02:25:25.850 --> 02:25:28.219
orange, strawberry,

02:25:28.219 --> 02:25:32.023
lemon, green, and black currant, maybe.

02:25:32.023 --> 02:25:32.890
Three out of five!

02:25:32.890 --> 02:25:33.725
How'd I do?

02:25:35.226 --> 02:25:36.394
[Joe laughing]

02:25:36.394 --> 02:25:38.029
I couldn't taste anything.
You guys wanna try?

02:25:38.029 --> 02:25:40.231
That was a lot harder than I
thought it was going to be.

02:25:40.231 --> 02:25:42.166
I honestly think if I just
saw it and still plugged,

02:25:42.166 --> 02:25:43.501
then I'd be like, "Yeah, I got orange."

02:25:43.501 --> 02:25:45.636
Sight, however, does not
always play a significant role

02:25:45.636 --> 02:25:46.904
in identifying flavors.

02:25:46.904 --> 02:25:49.407
In June 2016, Skittles
released a limited-edition

02:25:49.407 --> 02:25:50.742
all-white version of their product

02:25:50.742 --> 02:25:52.510
in order to celebrate London Pride.

02:25:52.510 --> 02:25:53.845
They've carried on doing this ever since,

02:25:53.845 --> 02:25:55.346
stating on the packaging
that during Pride,

02:25:55.346 --> 02:25:56.681
"only one rainbow matters."

02:25:56.681 --> 02:25:58.516
So they ditch the rainbow in
order to honor Pride month,

02:25:58.516 --> 02:26:00.451
which is a nice gesture on paper. However.

02:26:00.451 --> 02:26:03.988
Apparently, some people took
this to mean white pride

02:26:03.988 --> 02:26:06.858
and went on Twitter and
voiced said observation.

02:26:06.858 --> 02:26:08.092
The good news is that despite that,

02:26:08.092 --> 02:26:09.627
most people did still
appreciate the gesture,

02:26:09.627 --> 02:26:11.329
so they have carried on doing it.

02:26:11.329 --> 02:26:12.764
If they're all the same color,

02:26:12.764 --> 02:26:14.232
how do you know which
Skittle you're getting?

02:26:15.600 --> 02:26:18.002
It's actually way harder
to be, like, certain.

02:26:19.537 --> 02:26:20.905
They have, like, a fruit flavor to them,

02:26:20.905 --> 02:26:22.440
but they don't taste that fruity.

02:26:22.440 --> 02:26:25.643
I was almost like, wait, did
they also omit the flavor?

02:26:25.643 --> 02:26:27.078
So now, because I can't see the color,

02:26:27.078 --> 02:26:28.412
I'm not quite sure what flavor it is.

02:26:29.781 --> 02:26:31.816
Here are all the Skittles
products you'll find in the UK

02:26:31.816 --> 02:26:33.451
that you won't find in the US.

02:26:33.451 --> 02:26:36.521
Here are all the exclusive
US Skittle flavors

02:26:36.521 --> 02:26:38.089
you cannot get in the UK.

02:26:38.089 --> 02:26:39.791
We'll start from this end
and work our way along.

02:26:39.791 --> 02:26:41.859
So, in the UK, we can get Skittles Giants.

02:26:41.859 --> 02:26:43.094
Now, apparently these are Skittles

02:26:43.094 --> 02:26:45.797
which are three times the
size of regular Skittles.

02:26:45.797 --> 02:26:48.199
They do look pretty beefy, I will say.

02:26:48.199 --> 02:26:49.767
They are similar to
the regular UK Skittles

02:26:49.767 --> 02:26:51.469
in that they still have the hard shell

02:26:51.469 --> 02:26:53.037
and they are the same
flavors as the normal ones.

02:26:53.037 --> 02:26:55.573
However, apparently they
have a softer inside as well.

02:26:59.010 --> 02:27:01.345
My issue with this is they
do have a softer inside,

02:27:01.345 --> 02:27:03.848
however, it reminds me
very much of chewing gum.

02:27:03.848 --> 02:27:05.716
So everything in my brain
is now screaming at me

02:27:05.716 --> 02:27:08.786
to not swallow this. I
don't think I like that.

02:27:08.786 --> 02:27:09.921
If, for whatever reason, you don't like

02:27:09.921 --> 02:27:11.155
the crispy shell on your Skittles,

02:27:11.155 --> 02:27:13.157
then we've got you
covered, because in the UK,

02:27:13.157 --> 02:27:14.826
you can get Skittles Chewies,

02:27:14.826 --> 02:27:16.327
which are Skittles without a shell.

02:27:16.327 --> 02:27:17.728
Something about these
just looks a little bit

02:27:17.728 --> 02:27:18.629
off-putting to me.

02:27:18.629 --> 02:27:21.365
It just kind of looks
like little balls of wax.

02:27:21.365 --> 02:27:23.534
And again, something in your
brain kind of screams at you

02:27:23.534 --> 02:27:25.236
and says, "Please do not eat this candle."

02:27:26.871 --> 02:27:28.339
The flavor doesn't come
through as strongly

02:27:28.339 --> 02:27:30.541
as it does from a regular
Skittle for some reason.

02:27:30.541 --> 02:27:32.376
I feel like they must have
to coat them in something

02:27:32.376 --> 02:27:34.512
to prevent them from all just
clumping together in the bag.

02:27:34.512 --> 02:27:35.847
There's something kind of slimy going on

02:27:35.847 --> 02:27:37.715
on the tongue right now,
which I am not a fan of.

02:27:37.715 --> 02:27:38.816
I'm disappointed.

02:27:38.816 --> 02:27:39.717
I'm not impressed.

02:27:39.717 --> 02:27:41.552
This one, I mean, is this really a flavor?

02:27:41.552 --> 02:27:45.790
It is the wild berry and
tropical mix, flavor mash-ups.

02:27:45.790 --> 02:27:48.893
If there's ever been a lazier exclusive.

02:27:48.893 --> 02:27:52.129
Take the two things we already
have, stick them in a bag.

02:27:52.129 --> 02:27:54.832
Next up, we got Shriekers, Yuelei.

02:27:54.832 --> 02:27:56.367
"Beware! If you dare. Shriekers.

02:27:56.367 --> 02:27:58.569
Some are so sour they'll make you shriek."

02:27:58.569 --> 02:27:59.604
OK.

02:28:01.672 --> 02:28:02.840
[glass shattering]

02:28:02.840 --> 02:28:03.875
What? What?

02:28:03.875 --> 02:28:06.010
Are you shrieking? Like, is
it so sour you can't handle?

02:28:06.010 --> 02:28:08.045
It can't be you're not sure.

02:28:08.045 --> 02:28:09.513
No one has shrieked once.

02:28:09.513 --> 02:28:11.616
These expired in May.

02:28:11.616 --> 02:28:13.351
We have the America mix.

02:28:13.351 --> 02:28:15.186
No one is more patriotic than I.

02:28:15.186 --> 02:28:16.754
I bleed red, white, and blue.

02:28:16.754 --> 02:28:17.588
Mostly red.

02:28:17.588 --> 02:28:20.024
But this feels a little condescending.

02:28:20.024 --> 02:28:22.426
Because it looks like they
just put the flag on it.

02:28:22.426 --> 02:28:24.061
Like, what's the America mix?

02:28:24.061 --> 02:28:25.696
Five flavors, three colors.

02:28:27.498 --> 02:28:28.766
Oh! These are stale.

02:28:28.766 --> 02:28:31.235
Yeah, they might have done
this several years ago.

02:28:31.235 --> 02:28:34.238
Last exclusive flavor, we
have Brightside Skittles.

02:28:34.238 --> 02:28:36.007
Remember that song "Mr. Brightside"?

02:28:36.007 --> 02:28:39.810
♪ Wee-hoo I look just
like Mr. Brightside ♪

02:28:39.810 --> 02:28:41.245
I love The Black Keys.

02:28:41.245 --> 02:28:42.713
But it's kiwi banana, watermelon,

02:28:42.713 --> 02:28:44.782
paradise punch, tangerine,
and pink lemonade.

02:28:44.782 --> 02:28:47.118
That sounds a lot like the
tropical mix, doesn't it?

02:28:47.118 --> 02:28:48.719
These are dusty.

02:28:48.719 --> 02:28:50.855
Compared to

02:28:50.855 --> 02:28:53.658
a seemingly much fresher USA one.

02:28:55.393 --> 02:28:57.628
I should have googled how old these are.

02:28:59.463 --> 02:29:01.933
It does. These taste like shampoo.

02:29:01.933 --> 02:29:03.868
Our next UK exclusive is Squishy Cloudz.

02:29:03.868 --> 02:29:04.969
It's another product from Skittles

02:29:04.969 --> 02:29:06.470
in which they have kind
of ditched the shell

02:29:06.470 --> 02:29:08.439
and gone for a more soft, chewy approach.

02:29:08.439 --> 02:29:10.207
In the UK, these come in
both the standard flavors

02:29:10.207 --> 02:29:11.976
and also the Crazy Sours flavors.

02:29:11.976 --> 02:29:13.110
I don't know what I was expecting,

02:29:13.110 --> 02:29:16.013
but we have these kind
of very soft, pillowy,

02:29:16.013 --> 02:29:16.981
I guess Skittles.

02:29:16.981 --> 02:29:18.582
It feels weird to call
this a Skittle, but.

02:29:20.251 --> 02:29:22.787
I prefer these to the other
two that I've just tried.

02:29:22.787 --> 02:29:24.422
So, as you can see, the sour version

02:29:24.422 --> 02:29:27.425
has this kind of
granulated-sugar exterior,

02:29:27.425 --> 02:29:29.293
whereas the classic version does not.

02:29:29.293 --> 02:29:31.262
Skittles gummies. OK.

02:29:31.262 --> 02:29:32.496
First glance, it feels like

02:29:32.496 --> 02:29:34.398
they're taking away
the Skittleyness of it.

02:29:34.398 --> 02:29:36.267
I mean, they're making
just round gummies now.

02:29:36.267 --> 02:29:37.635
What's the difference
between Skittle gummies

02:29:37.635 --> 02:29:39.804
and other fruit-flavored gummies?

02:29:39.804 --> 02:29:42.139
And how much does the
shape affect the taste?

02:29:42.139 --> 02:29:45.109
Look how many greens I
pulled out in one pinch!

02:29:45.109 --> 02:29:46.610
Yep.

02:29:46.610 --> 02:29:47.812
I'm a sucker for gummy.

02:29:47.812 --> 02:29:49.246
I don't know why. I love gummy stuff.

02:29:49.246 --> 02:29:50.247
These aren't very good.

02:29:50.247 --> 02:29:51.515
I know gummy snacks

02:29:51.515 --> 02:29:55.186
are maybe the most unnatural
delivery system for flavors.

02:29:55.186 --> 02:29:58.155
But these taste woefully manufactured.

02:29:58.155 --> 02:29:59.890
Why are my gummies sweaty?

02:29:59.890 --> 02:30:01.726
Skittles, I think you got a
little more to learn about

02:30:01.726 --> 02:30:03.661
gummy manufacturing, because these just,

02:30:03.661 --> 02:30:04.662
these ain't it.

02:30:04.662 --> 02:30:06.230
My boys in Sweden

02:30:06.230 --> 02:30:08.666
are still kicking your butts
with their fish. Sorry.

02:30:08.666 --> 02:30:11.068
Then we're on to the final
UK exclusive, which is this,

02:30:11.068 --> 02:30:13.004
Skittles Stix ice cream bars.

02:30:13.004 --> 02:30:14.405
The back of the packet describes them as,

02:30:14.405 --> 02:30:15.573
"Fruity flavoured ice cream

02:30:15.573 --> 02:30:17.308
with a secret sugar pearl centre."

02:30:17.308 --> 02:30:18.743
Should I just bite into the whole thing?

02:30:18.743 --> 02:30:19.577
Charlie: Yeah.

02:30:22.880 --> 02:30:23.881
I feel like all of these

02:30:23.881 --> 02:30:25.683
are different flavors of ice cream.

02:30:25.683 --> 02:30:27.385
And one of them I am not enjoying,

02:30:27.385 --> 02:30:28.919
and I can't quite tell which one it is.

02:30:28.919 --> 02:30:30.121
Skittles freezer pops.

02:30:30.121 --> 02:30:31.856
These have been sitting
in my freezer for a month.

02:30:31.856 --> 02:30:33.524
I'm thrilled to get rid of them.

02:30:33.524 --> 02:30:35.259
Orange, because it's on the side here.

02:30:35.259 --> 02:30:37.361
Every summer at the pool just
trying to get this thing open,

02:30:37.361 --> 02:30:38.629
just sitting there [growls].

02:30:38.629 --> 02:30:40.264
Take me back, baby.

02:30:42.400 --> 02:30:44.402
Those can stay in the past.

02:30:44.402 --> 02:30:47.104
Last but not least, these guys.

02:30:47.104 --> 02:30:48.305
Singles to go.

02:30:48.305 --> 02:30:50.608
Little powder drinks. Make some juice.

02:30:50.608 --> 02:30:51.809
Well, it's not real juice, right?

02:30:51.809 --> 02:30:53.878
So, you got these little flavor pouches.

02:30:53.878 --> 02:30:56.814
That's really funny. They
didn't even bother with lemon.

02:30:56.814 --> 02:30:57.848
They're like, "who cares."

02:30:57.848 --> 02:30:58.983
Little green apple juice.

02:30:58.983 --> 02:31:01.318
Who doesn't love a
little green apple juice?

02:31:02.920 --> 02:31:04.355
[lightning crackles]

02:31:04.355 --> 02:31:05.523
Fun.

02:31:06.690 --> 02:31:08.859
Yeah, very natural looking.

02:31:09.994 --> 02:31:12.663
This is meant to be put in a water bottle.

02:31:12.663 --> 02:31:13.531
Uh-oh!

02:31:13.531 --> 02:31:15.299
It definitely has a strong odor.

02:31:15.299 --> 02:31:16.801
Finally, they did it.

02:31:16.801 --> 02:31:20.137
All the delicious flavor
of green-apple candy

02:31:20.137 --> 02:31:21.238
in your water.

02:31:23.541 --> 02:31:24.542
These are terrible.

02:31:24.542 --> 02:31:26.777
You have to be wondering the same thing

02:31:26.777 --> 02:31:27.878
that I'm wondering.

02:31:30.347 --> 02:31:32.216
I would 100% have done
this when I was a kid.

02:31:32.216 --> 02:31:33.217
The second I see these, I'm like,

02:31:33.217 --> 02:31:34.652
"I'm putting them all in one,
and I'm going to chug it."

02:31:34.652 --> 02:31:37.588
Oh, it's turning, like, a
brownie barf color, you see that?

02:31:40.391 --> 02:31:44.662
This right here is called
tasting the rainbow.

02:31:44.662 --> 02:31:46.497
[coughing]

02:31:52.169 --> 02:31:54.905
Drink the rainbow!

02:31:54.905 --> 02:31:57.274
I'll have one more sip.

02:31:57.274 --> 02:32:00.578
Like a fine wine. Why -- wah!

02:32:00.578 --> 02:32:03.280
You can also get kosher Skittles

02:32:03.280 --> 02:32:05.349
and Skittles gum.

02:32:06.684 --> 02:32:09.720
Original Skittles in the
UK contain the following.

02:32:09.720 --> 02:32:12.256
Original Skittles in the US contain:

02:32:13.190 --> 02:32:15.659
Artificial colors like Red 40, Yellow 5,

02:32:15.659 --> 02:32:18.229
Yellow 6, and Blue 1
are restricted in the UK

02:32:18.229 --> 02:32:20.131
because studies have linked
them to hyperactivity

02:32:20.131 --> 02:32:21.932
and attention deficit in children.

02:32:21.932 --> 02:32:23.734
Food and drink containing
these things in the UK

02:32:23.734 --> 02:32:24.735
have to bear a warning label

02:32:24.735 --> 02:32:27.037
about these potential adverse
affects, which means that

02:32:27.037 --> 02:32:29.340
most manufacturers will
simply avoid using them.

02:32:29.340 --> 02:32:30.174
But not here.

02:32:30.174 --> 02:32:32.910
Also, one chemical you
won't be eating in the EU

02:32:32.910 --> 02:32:36.013
anytime soon is titanium dioxide.

02:32:36.013 --> 02:32:37.815
The European Food Safety Authority

02:32:37.815 --> 02:32:40.885
found it could no longer
be considered safe

02:32:40.885 --> 02:32:42.753
when used as a food additive.

02:32:42.753 --> 02:32:44.688
Specifically, the panel concluded that

02:32:44.688 --> 02:32:47.758
titanium dioxide particles
"have the potential

02:32:47.758 --> 02:32:52.763
to induce DNA strand breaks
and chromosomal damage."

02:32:52.897 --> 02:32:53.731
What?

02:32:53.731 --> 02:32:56.834
Just feel my particles just pshh.

02:32:56.834 --> 02:32:58.002
Harry: Breaking news coming to you live

02:32:58.002 --> 02:32:59.336
from the "Food Wars" news desk.

02:32:59.336 --> 02:33:00.738
Shortly after we recorded this episode,

02:33:00.738 --> 02:33:02.740
Mars Inc. was actually hit
by a class-action lawsuit

02:33:02.740 --> 02:33:04.608
in the US. The suit alleges that Skittles

02:33:04.608 --> 02:33:06.277
are "unfit for human consumption"

02:33:06.277 --> 02:33:08.078
due to the presence of titanium dioxide.

02:33:08.078 --> 02:33:09.647
The suit points to the EFSA ruling

02:33:09.647 --> 02:33:11.015
as well as the fact Mars themselves

02:33:11.015 --> 02:33:12.783
had committed to removing
artificial colors

02:33:12.783 --> 02:33:14.585
from their products back in 2016,

02:33:14.585 --> 02:33:16.253
a promise they've since backtracked on.

02:33:16.253 --> 02:33:17.655
Mars isn't backing down just yet,

02:33:17.655 --> 02:33:18.956
telling The New York Times that its use

02:33:18.956 --> 02:33:20.758
of titanium dioxide is "in compliance

02:33:20.758 --> 02:33:22.860
with strict quality
and safety requirements

02:33:22.860 --> 02:33:25.729
established by food safety
regulators, including the F.D.A."

02:33:25.729 --> 02:33:27.231
We'll have to wait and
see how the trial unfolds

02:33:27.231 --> 02:33:29.133
to see if there's any
bearing on food-safety laws

02:33:29.133 --> 02:33:30.801
around titanium dioxide in the US

02:33:30.801 --> 02:33:32.469
and potentially even the UK.

02:33:32.469 --> 02:33:34.038
In slightly better news, standard Skittles

02:33:34.038 --> 02:33:35.973
do not contain any animal-derived gelatin,

02:33:35.973 --> 02:33:37.575
so they are suitable for vegans.

02:33:39.910 --> 02:33:44.248
In the UK, a 45-gram bag of
Skittles contains 179 calories.

02:33:44.248 --> 02:33:48.552
This entire bag is 250 calories, so.

02:33:48.552 --> 02:33:49.553
We're going to quickly count these out

02:33:49.553 --> 02:33:51.589
and figure out the
calorie count per Skittle.

02:33:53.023 --> 02:33:55.025
How many Skittles in a bag?

02:33:55.025 --> 02:33:56.994
Fifty ... two.

02:33:56.994 --> 02:33:58.829
By my count, a 45-gram bag in the UK

02:33:58.829 --> 02:34:00.564
contained around 52 Skittles.

02:34:00.564 --> 02:34:03.634
That means that the calorie
count per Skittle is 3.44.

02:34:03.634 --> 02:34:07.938
That's 4.24 calories per Skittle.

02:34:07.938 --> 02:34:09.807
As for sugar content,
this tiny bag contains

02:34:09.807 --> 02:34:12.743
66.4% of your recommended daily allowance.

02:34:12.743 --> 02:34:13.711
That seems like a lot.

02:34:13.711 --> 02:34:16.780
Gotta go bigger in the US. Ours is 90%.

02:34:17.014 --> 02:34:18.549
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

02:34:18.549 --> 02:34:19.917
we wanted to find out all the differences

02:34:19.917 --> 02:34:22.419
between Heinz products
in the UK and the US.

02:34:22.419 --> 02:34:23.921
This is Food Wars.

02:34:28.325 --> 02:34:29.727
Heinz is best known for its ketchup.

02:34:29.727 --> 02:34:32.663
In the UK, it comes in the
following bottle sizes,

02:34:32.663 --> 02:34:35.766
250 grams, 342 grams,

02:34:35.766 --> 02:34:39.103
460 grams, 570 grams,

02:34:39.103 --> 02:34:41.972
700 grams, 910 grams,

02:34:41.972 --> 02:34:45.309
or our largest size, a
1.35-kilogram bottle.

02:34:45.309 --> 02:34:47.378
I did also find a 1-kilogram bottle online

02:34:47.378 --> 02:34:48.212
and I ordered some,

02:34:48.212 --> 02:34:49.179
but unfortunately, it was stolen

02:34:49.179 --> 02:34:50.180
from the lobby of our building.

02:34:50.180 --> 02:34:51.448
So just imagine it there.

02:34:51.448 --> 02:34:55.552
Heinz ketchup in the US also
comes in a variety of sizes.

02:34:55.552 --> 02:34:58.022
Down at the end here, we
got the 14-ounce bottle.

02:34:58.022 --> 02:35:00.190
Then we go up to a 20-ounce bottle.

02:35:00.190 --> 02:35:03.861
We have a 32-ounce
bottle, 38-ounce bottle,

02:35:03.861 --> 02:35:07.731
and the big juggy guy, 114 ounces.

02:35:07.731 --> 02:35:08.966
What?

02:35:08.966 --> 02:35:11.702
This can't be for like a family.

02:35:11.702 --> 02:35:15.172
I guess if you just like dipping your food

02:35:15.172 --> 02:35:17.141
and your fingers directly in there.

02:35:18.175 --> 02:35:19.443
Nice.

02:35:21.779 --> 02:35:23.814
Apparently, one serving
size for ketchup in the UK

02:35:23.814 --> 02:35:24.815
is 15 grams,

02:35:24.815 --> 02:35:27.151
which means that this
bottle contains 90 servings.

02:35:27.151 --> 02:35:27.985
We're going to measure that out

02:35:27.985 --> 02:35:29.420
and see if it seems realistic.

02:35:31.055 --> 02:35:32.122
So according to Heinz in the UK,

02:35:32.122 --> 02:35:33.257
that's one serving of ketchup.

02:35:33.257 --> 02:35:34.291
That maybe looks like a bit less

02:35:34.291 --> 02:35:35.659
than what I might normally
have with my meal,

02:35:35.659 --> 02:35:37.294
if I was having say,
chips or something else,

02:35:37.294 --> 02:35:39.363
which required dipping but not too bad.

02:35:39.363 --> 02:35:41.965
The US serving size is 1 tablespoon,

02:35:41.965 --> 02:35:44.868
which Heinz says is 17 grams of ketchup.

02:35:44.868 --> 02:35:47.104
Let's take a look at that right now.

02:35:47.104 --> 02:35:47.938
Got to do this.

02:35:47.938 --> 02:35:48.772
You don't want to get that ketchup water

02:35:48.772 --> 02:35:49.940
that comes out first.

02:35:49.940 --> 02:35:51.241
That's the worst thing in the world.

02:35:51.241 --> 02:35:52.543
You don't do this; you
get that ketchup water.

02:35:52.543 --> 02:35:53.811
It's like, well, that's ruined.

02:35:53.811 --> 02:35:56.113
Oh, it's like, oh 10, 13.

02:35:57.247 --> 02:35:58.449
This is like when you're getting gas.

02:35:58.449 --> 02:36:00.918
You want to get the exact dollar amount.

02:36:00.918 --> 02:36:02.886
I've been training for this my whole life.

02:36:03.821 --> 02:36:05.289
[Gasping]

02:36:05.289 --> 02:36:06.824
Yay!

02:36:06.824 --> 02:36:08.058
17 grams of ketchup.

02:36:08.058 --> 02:36:10.627
That means that this 114-ounce jug

02:36:10.627 --> 02:36:13.564
contains 190 servings of this right here.

02:36:13.564 --> 02:36:15.999
That seems like a reasonable amount.

02:36:18.869 --> 02:36:20.137
Here are all the Heinz
products from the UK

02:36:20.137 --> 02:36:21.605
that you won't find in the US.

02:36:21.605 --> 02:36:23.741
We finally have way more than America.

02:36:23.741 --> 02:36:24.708
Come on.

02:36:24.708 --> 02:36:26.677
And here are all the
Heinz products in the US

02:36:26.677 --> 02:36:28.011
you won't find in the UK.

02:36:28.011 --> 02:36:29.079
Do they have more than us?

02:36:29.079 --> 02:36:30.247
I feel like we have more.

02:36:30.247 --> 02:36:31.415
Let's find out.

02:36:31.415 --> 02:36:34.017
In the UK, we can get two
exclusive varieties of ketchup.

02:36:34.017 --> 02:36:35.252
We can get ketchup up with 50%

02:36:35.252 --> 02:36:36.587
less added sugar and salt

02:36:36.587 --> 02:36:39.123
as well as ketchup with
no added sugar and salt.

02:36:39.123 --> 02:36:40.824
And these are exclusives here in the US,

02:36:40.824 --> 02:36:42.726
because we have them singularly.

02:36:42.726 --> 02:36:45.629
A no salt added and a no sugar added.

02:36:45.629 --> 02:36:48.165
Some of the exclusive ketchups we have are

02:36:48.165 --> 02:36:48.999
jalapenño ketchup.

02:36:49.900 --> 02:36:51.368
Oooh. Sriracha-flavored ketchup.

02:36:52.736 --> 02:36:53.570
Ooh.

02:36:54.772 --> 02:36:57.474
Not bad, but just taste more
garlicky than anything else.

02:36:57.474 --> 02:37:00.778
Hot and spicy Tabasco ketchup.

02:37:00.778 --> 02:37:03.814
Also, we have ketchup
with a blend of veggies,

02:37:03.814 --> 02:37:04.648
kids approved.

02:37:04.648 --> 02:37:06.517
I mean, I'm assuming this is to trick kids

02:37:06.517 --> 02:37:08.118
into eating more vegetables.

02:37:08.118 --> 02:37:09.787
I think it's better than none, right?

02:37:09.787 --> 02:37:10.721
I'm curious.

02:37:12.523 --> 02:37:14.825
That tastes exactly like
ketchup, which is good, right?

02:37:14.825 --> 02:37:17.394
Trying to trick kids into
eating vegetables. This is it.

02:37:17.394 --> 02:37:19.696
You can also get simple tomato ketchup

02:37:19.696 --> 02:37:21.265
with no artificial sweeteners

02:37:21.265 --> 02:37:23.734
and tomato ketchup
sweetened only with honey,

02:37:23.734 --> 02:37:27.137
not available at the time
of filming this segment.

02:37:27.137 --> 02:37:29.106
What the UK might lack in
exclusive ketchup options,

02:37:29.106 --> 02:37:30.641
we make up for with our mayonnaise.

02:37:30.641 --> 02:37:32.576
Over here, we can get light mayonnaise.

02:37:32.576 --> 02:37:33.644
The existence of light mayo

02:37:33.644 --> 02:37:36.380
implies the existence of
a forbidden dark mayo,

02:37:36.380 --> 02:37:37.514
just a heads-up.

02:37:37.514 --> 02:37:39.283
We have a vegan mayo option.

02:37:39.283 --> 02:37:41.752
Our other vegan options
include this vegan chili mayo

02:37:41.752 --> 02:37:44.555
as well as this vegan mayo/garlic aioli.

02:37:44.555 --> 02:37:46.657
Then we have some seriously
good mayo options,

02:37:46.657 --> 02:37:49.393
including this garlic
and caramelized onion one

02:37:49.393 --> 02:37:51.895
as well as this rich
and creamy truffle mayo.

02:37:51.895 --> 02:37:52.729
I don't like how they've had to

02:37:52.729 --> 02:37:54.364
create a fancy brand of mayo.

02:37:54.364 --> 02:37:56.200
I feel like nobody should
be too good for mayo.

02:37:56.200 --> 02:37:58.235
If you think you're too
good for mayo, you're wrong.

02:37:58.235 --> 02:37:59.503
The Heinz website also advertised

02:37:59.503 --> 02:38:00.704
a lemon and black pepper mayo,

02:38:00.704 --> 02:38:02.372
which unfortunately I
couldn't find anywhere.

02:38:02.372 --> 02:38:04.875
The mustard option you
get in the US exclusively

02:38:04.875 --> 02:38:08.512
are the 100% natural spicy brown mustard,

02:38:08.512 --> 02:38:11.248
a Heinz 100% natural honey mustard.

02:38:11.248 --> 02:38:14.084
And, of course, organic yellow mustard.

02:38:14.084 --> 02:38:15.719
Heinz sells two types
of mustard in the UK,

02:38:15.719 --> 02:38:17.254
a mild mustard and a honey mustard,

02:38:17.254 --> 02:38:18.856
but they were strangely
hard to track down.

02:38:18.856 --> 02:38:20.557
I did find the mild mustard online,

02:38:20.557 --> 02:38:21.992
but that was also one of the things stolen

02:38:21.992 --> 02:38:24.294
along with our ketchup.
So enjoy that as well.

02:38:24.294 --> 02:38:27.130
Barbecue sauces, USA,
we love our barbecue.

02:38:27.130 --> 02:38:28.932
We love our barbecue sauces.

02:38:28.932 --> 02:38:31.235
The first one is the
Carolina mustard-style,

02:38:31.235 --> 02:38:32.936
sweet mustard barbecue sauce.

02:38:32.936 --> 02:38:33.837
I'm like stoked for this one.

02:38:33.837 --> 02:38:35.472
When these arrived, I was like, hell yeah.

02:38:35.472 --> 02:38:36.874
I want to try these sauces.

02:38:36.874 --> 02:38:37.708
Ooh.

02:38:39.510 --> 02:38:40.511
Fantastic.

02:38:40.511 --> 02:38:41.345
Yep.

02:38:41.345 --> 02:38:43.780
We got the Carolina vinegar-style,

02:38:43.780 --> 02:38:45.616
sweet and tangy barbecue sauce.

02:38:47.050 --> 02:38:48.519
I like this one, too.

02:38:48.519 --> 02:38:51.255
There's a Kansas City-style
sweet and smoky barbecue sauce,

02:38:51.255 --> 02:38:52.956
I could not get it at
the time of filming this.

02:38:52.956 --> 02:38:53.790
But I was able to get

02:38:53.790 --> 02:38:54.625
the original sweet and thick barbecue.

02:38:57.461 --> 02:38:59.897
I mean, barbecue sauce is
like the best thing ever.

02:39:01.231 --> 02:39:02.766
Well, they weren't kidding. That is thick.

02:39:02.766 --> 02:39:06.870
The Texas barbecue sauce, bold and spicy.

02:39:06.870 --> 02:39:07.938
Just like the state.

02:39:09.973 --> 02:39:10.874
Also thick.

02:39:10.874 --> 02:39:11.708
Oh my goodness.

02:39:11.708 --> 02:39:14.011
I'd do this one or this one.
These are the two best ones.

02:39:14.011 --> 02:39:16.113
I can't decide: Carolina or Texas?

02:39:16.113 --> 02:39:18.215
We only have two barbecue
sauce options in the UK,

02:39:18.215 --> 02:39:21.451
a sweet barbecue sauce and
a classic barbecue sauce.

02:39:21.451 --> 02:39:22.319
I'm not sure how it compares

02:39:22.319 --> 02:39:24.021
to the original sweet option in the US

02:39:24.021 --> 02:39:26.223
or how they've managed to kind
of isolate those two flavors.

02:39:26.223 --> 02:39:27.858
I don't think like original/classic

02:39:27.858 --> 02:39:29.826
can be a flavor in itself.

02:39:29.826 --> 02:39:31.161
Vinegars!

02:39:31.161 --> 02:39:32.062
Let's go.

02:39:32.062 --> 02:39:34.565
Distilled white vinegar, 5% acidity.

02:39:34.565 --> 02:39:36.099
What does it mean by 5% acidity?

02:39:36.099 --> 02:39:36.967
Great question.

02:39:36.967 --> 02:39:39.903
Well, you see. I have no idea.

02:39:39.903 --> 02:39:43.006
Then we also got the Heinz
gourmet malt vinegar.

02:39:43.006 --> 02:39:47.244
We also have a salad vinegar,
gourmet salad vinegar.

02:39:47.244 --> 02:39:48.078
Yum.

02:39:48.078 --> 02:39:52.482
We have a gourmet tarragon vinegar. Ooh.

02:39:52.482 --> 02:39:54.851
All-natural apple cider vinegar,

02:39:54.851 --> 02:39:59.189
a organic unfiltered apple
cider vinegar with the mother,

02:39:59.189 --> 02:40:00.691
question mark, question
mark, question mark.

02:40:00.691 --> 02:40:02.159
Yeah, I don't know what that means either.

02:40:02.159 --> 02:40:04.695
We have a Heinz all-natural, multipurpose,

02:40:04.695 --> 02:40:07.731
extra-strength vinegar with a 6% acidity.

02:40:07.731 --> 02:40:10.133
And, of course, the Heinz imported

02:40:10.133 --> 02:40:13.437
balsamic vinegar from Modena.

02:40:13.437 --> 02:40:15.339
Well, in the UK, Heinz
just has malt vinegar,

02:40:15.339 --> 02:40:16.206
which I couldn't find today,

02:40:16.206 --> 02:40:17.975
but it does go great
on your fish and chips.

02:40:17.975 --> 02:40:21.979
We have got so many gravies.

02:40:21.979 --> 02:40:23.347
Gravy, as I know it,

02:40:23.347 --> 02:40:26.216
you pour it on mashed
potatoes and sometimes turkey.

02:40:26.216 --> 02:40:27.417
It's like a brown sauce,
sometimes a white sauce.

02:40:27.417 --> 02:40:29.720
You can also get biscuits
and gravy for breakfast,

02:40:29.720 --> 02:40:31.888
which is fantastic. That sauce is white.

02:40:31.888 --> 02:40:34.458
We got a lot of different
variations of gravy.

02:40:34.458 --> 02:40:37.327
I can't be the US gravy ambassador.

02:40:37.327 --> 02:40:39.162
All right, I didn't ask for this job.

02:40:39.162 --> 02:40:40.263
At no point in the interview -

02:40:40.263 --> 02:40:41.098
and they was like, well, you know

02:40:41.098 --> 02:40:42.666
a lot about gravies, right?

02:40:42.666 --> 02:40:45.369
And if I said I did, I lied, all right?

02:40:45.369 --> 02:40:47.671
Please revoke my gravy ambassador status,

02:40:47.671 --> 02:40:51.742
and I'm sorry for deceiving
the people of Food Insider.

02:40:51.742 --> 02:40:53.543
Heinz homestyle gravy with mushroom,

02:40:53.543 --> 02:40:54.411
there's mushrooms in it.

02:40:54.411 --> 02:40:56.913
I see gravy twice a year,
once at Thanksgiving

02:40:56.913 --> 02:40:58.849
and once when I go to Jollibee.

02:40:58.849 --> 02:41:03.854
And then you got the savory
beef gravy, bistro au jus,

02:41:03.954 --> 02:41:05.722
signature gravy, rich and savory,

02:41:05.722 --> 02:41:08.759
made with roasted turkey. Let's go.

02:41:08.759 --> 02:41:11.028
We got the country-style sausage gravy.

02:41:11.028 --> 02:41:13.897
This goes, yes, see he's
pouring it on biscuits.

02:41:13.897 --> 02:41:15.599
It's a breakfast. It's a breakfast gravy.

02:41:15.599 --> 02:41:18.335
It comes with a country
sausage and hashbrowns recipe.

02:41:18.335 --> 02:41:20.270
Joe, I don't know what that
is, but that is not gravy.

02:41:20.270 --> 02:41:21.204
That looks horrific.

02:41:21.204 --> 02:41:23.573
Especially that creamy
stuff, like why is it white?

02:41:23.573 --> 02:41:24.675
Gravy should never be white.

02:41:24.675 --> 02:41:25.876
Classic chicken grav...

02:41:25.876 --> 02:41:26.943
this is like not white.

02:41:26.943 --> 02:41:29.846
It's more of like a khaki-colored gravy.

02:41:29.846 --> 02:41:32.549
Now, that isn't even close
to the amount of gravies

02:41:32.549 --> 02:41:34.685
you can get in the US.

02:41:34.685 --> 02:41:37.320
I went to several grocery stores, several,

02:41:37.320 --> 02:41:39.656
looking for these gravies,
as many as I could find.

02:41:39.656 --> 02:41:41.425
So, I'm going to rattle off the list.

02:41:41.425 --> 02:41:44.494
Signature rich and savory gravy
with real roasted chicken.

02:41:44.494 --> 02:41:48.331
The homestyle brown with onion gravy.

02:41:48.331 --> 02:41:51.168
Heinz homestyle classic
chicken fat-free gravy.

02:41:51.168 --> 02:41:52.836
Homestyle pork gravy.

02:41:52.836 --> 02:41:54.638
And, last but not least,

02:41:54.638 --> 02:41:58.842
homestyle savory beef fat-free gravy.

02:41:58.842 --> 02:41:59.876
British gravy is either made from

02:41:59.876 --> 02:42:01.645
the juices and drippings of a roast dinner

02:42:01.645 --> 02:42:04.381
or made by adding water
to some gravy granules.

02:42:04.381 --> 02:42:05.949
Not a Heinz brand, just a good brand.

02:42:05.949 --> 02:42:06.783
I like Bisto.

02:42:06.783 --> 02:42:07.751
Thankfully, we don't have any of that

02:42:07.751 --> 02:42:08.885
American gravy in the UK.

02:42:08.885 --> 02:42:11.188
But what we do have is salad cream

02:42:11.188 --> 02:42:13.123
or "salad queen" as it's currently called

02:42:13.123 --> 02:42:15.425
in honor of the Queen's Platinum Jubilee.

02:42:15.425 --> 02:42:17.227
It's just what Liz
would've wanted, I'm sure.

02:42:17.227 --> 02:42:19.029
Now Americans, don't be
alarmed by salad cream.

02:42:19.029 --> 02:42:20.464
I know the name sounds
a little off-putting,

02:42:20.464 --> 02:42:21.364
but it's actually not that bad.

02:42:21.364 --> 02:42:22.365
It's basically just mayo

02:42:22.365 --> 02:42:23.900
that they've added some kind of vinegar

02:42:23.900 --> 02:42:25.435
and also a little bit of sugar to.

02:42:25.435 --> 02:42:26.536
So it's kinda like a tangy mayo,

02:42:26.536 --> 02:42:28.705
which you can add to your
salads or to your sandwiches.

02:42:28.705 --> 02:42:29.906
I feel like with Americans,

02:42:29.906 --> 02:42:31.675
some of the things that
they pass off as salads,

02:42:31.675 --> 02:42:33.944
they have no right to
criticize a salad cream.

02:42:33.944 --> 02:42:36.446
You ever seen, I think it's
ambrosia salad in America,

02:42:36.446 --> 02:42:38.315
which is basically just
loads of mayo anyway.

02:42:38.315 --> 02:42:39.883
Pickles and relish.

02:42:39.883 --> 02:42:42.986
I think relish is just
okay. Not a fan of pickles.

02:42:42.986 --> 02:42:46.323
The hot dog relish. Why is it this color?

02:42:46.323 --> 02:42:47.491
Why is it this color?

02:42:47.491 --> 02:42:49.126
If I got a hot dog and
they put this relish on it,

02:42:49.126 --> 02:42:51.595
I'd be like, yeah, you're
making it again without that.

02:42:51.595 --> 02:42:52.596
Dill relish, right?

02:42:52.596 --> 02:42:54.498
Just straightforward from dill pickles,

02:42:54.498 --> 02:42:55.332
or they add a dill to it.

02:42:55.332 --> 02:42:56.166
What do you think?

02:42:56.166 --> 02:43:00.237
Burger fans, get yourself
a Hamburger Dill Chips.

02:43:00.237 --> 02:43:03.006
Other pickle stroke relish options

02:43:03.006 --> 02:43:04.074
that we have here in the US are,

02:43:04.074 --> 02:43:06.843
genuine whole original sour dill pickles,

02:43:06.843 --> 02:43:09.613
spicy garlic chips with
garlic and red pepper,

02:43:09.613 --> 02:43:12.015
sweet and spicy chips
with crushed red pepper,

02:43:12.015 --> 02:43:13.750
sweet gherkins.

02:43:13.750 --> 02:43:15.418
We don't have Heinz pickles in the UK,

02:43:15.418 --> 02:43:16.953
but we do have Heinz pickle.

02:43:16.953 --> 02:43:18.054
This is an important
distinction to make in the UK,

02:43:18.054 --> 02:43:21.424
'cause if you ask for just
pickle singular on your sandwich,

02:43:21.424 --> 02:43:22.793
you'll end up with this.

02:43:22.793 --> 02:43:24.127
This is an iconic British condiment,

02:43:24.127 --> 02:43:26.129
which is either served as
part of your ploughman's lunch

02:43:26.129 --> 02:43:28.098
or on a sandwich, like
a cheese and pickle.

02:43:28.098 --> 02:43:30.367
It's a thick condiment made
with some cooked vegetables

02:43:30.367 --> 02:43:33.136
like carrots, swedes,
cauliflower, and onions,

02:43:33.136 --> 02:43:35.405
all served in this kind
of thick, tangy sauce.

02:43:35.405 --> 02:43:37.674
We also have Heinz Piccalilli in the UK.

02:43:37.674 --> 02:43:39.242
It's sort of similar
to ploughman's pickle,

02:43:39.242 --> 02:43:40.477
in that it's a spreadable condiment

02:43:40.477 --> 02:43:42.612
with some crunchy cooked
vegetables inside,

02:43:42.612 --> 02:43:43.446
but it's also different because

02:43:43.446 --> 02:43:44.848
it's got the mustard base,

02:43:44.848 --> 02:43:46.750
which gives it this
distinctive yellow color.

02:43:46.750 --> 02:43:48.618
I will say, it's kind of
an old person's condiment.

02:43:48.618 --> 02:43:49.686
I don't think you'll really see anyone

02:43:49.686 --> 02:43:51.655
under the age of 50 or so in the UK

02:43:51.655 --> 02:43:53.490
rushing for the Piccalilli,
but it is tasty.

02:43:53.490 --> 02:43:56.893
Next up, Heinz US offers a
bunch of specialty sauces,

02:43:56.893 --> 02:43:58.862
Heinz 57 sauce, real talk,

02:43:58.862 --> 02:44:00.730
I've never heard of
this before this video.

02:44:00.730 --> 02:44:02.098
It says that it adds zest to

02:44:02.098 --> 02:44:03.400
chicken, steak, or pork.

02:44:03.400 --> 02:44:04.868
Doesn't this sound like
a weird thing to say,

02:44:04.868 --> 02:44:06.469
but doesn't this look really good.

02:44:06.469 --> 02:44:09.105
So, let's find out.

02:44:09.105 --> 02:44:11.208
First, the sniff test.

02:44:11.208 --> 02:44:14.578
I like how it smells.
I like how it smells.

02:44:14.578 --> 02:44:15.912
Oh, oh, oh, oh.

02:44:15.912 --> 02:44:16.746
There we go.

02:44:18.982 --> 02:44:20.750
Yeah, that's really good.

02:44:20.750 --> 02:44:22.786
I can't place my finger
on exactly what it is.

02:44:22.786 --> 02:44:25.055
Ah, secret blend of
premium herbs and spices.

02:44:25.055 --> 02:44:27.157
Oh man, it's like liquid KFC apparently.

02:44:29.793 --> 02:44:31.895
It's like, like a mustard base,

02:44:31.895 --> 02:44:34.130
but like a sweeter and zestier

02:44:34.130 --> 02:44:36.166
like punctuation to it.

02:44:36.166 --> 02:44:39.035
Ooh. Heinz 57 chili sauce.

02:44:39.035 --> 02:44:41.338
I got to say Heinz, don't be putting this,

02:44:41.338 --> 02:44:42.272
"great on burgers and fries."

02:44:42.272 --> 02:44:43.740
Don't tell me what to put my sauce on.

02:44:43.740 --> 02:44:45.375
I'll brush my teeth
with this if I want to.

02:44:45.375 --> 02:44:46.776
You're not going to stop me.

02:44:47.644 --> 02:44:50.614
Yeah. That would be great

02:44:50.614 --> 02:44:52.549
on my burgers and/or fries.

02:44:52.549 --> 02:44:54.184
The Heinz original cocktail sauce.

02:44:54.184 --> 02:44:55.619
I'm not opening this, you know,

02:44:55.619 --> 02:44:56.553
shrimp, you get it, cocktails.

02:44:56.553 --> 02:44:58.054
I have no opinion on cocktail sauce.

02:44:58.054 --> 02:45:00.023
Worcestershire sauce.
Couldn't get my hands on it.

02:45:00.023 --> 02:45:01.224
But I do think that it's weird

02:45:01.224 --> 02:45:03.260
that Heinz also makes its own Wor-

02:45:03.260 --> 02:45:04.694
Worcestershire sauce.

02:45:04.694 --> 02:45:05.795
Worcestershire, Worcestershire?

02:45:05.795 --> 02:45:07.264
Is that right?

02:45:07.264 --> 02:45:10.100
Now, they also own Lea and Perrins,

02:45:10.100 --> 02:45:11.768
so are they competing against themselves?

02:45:11.768 --> 02:45:13.770
I don't think so. I
think they just bought it

02:45:13.770 --> 02:45:14.704
'cause they're Heinz.

02:45:14.704 --> 02:45:15.705
You think like,

02:45:15.705 --> 02:45:17.774
I'm not getting the Heinz
Worcestershire sauce,

02:45:17.774 --> 02:45:19.910
I'm getting the Lea and Perrins.

02:45:19.910 --> 02:45:22.979
That joke's on you. Heinz
gets paid on all that.

02:45:22.979 --> 02:45:24.781
Firstly, that should
be speciality sources,

02:45:24.781 --> 02:45:25.615
with the extra "i."

02:45:25.615 --> 02:45:26.449
And, secondly, in the UK,

02:45:26.449 --> 02:45:28.385
we also have a range of
specialty condiments.

02:45:28.385 --> 02:45:32.188
We have an American-style,
smoky baconnaise sauce

02:45:32.188 --> 02:45:34.457
and also an American-style burger sauce.

02:45:34.457 --> 02:45:36.559
Do these actually look or
feel American to you, Joe?

02:45:36.559 --> 02:45:38.862
Yes. That looks like sauce
that we potentially have.

02:45:38.862 --> 02:45:41.164
Then we have a Thai-style,
sweet chili sauce.

02:45:41.164 --> 02:45:43.400
British people love
Thai sweet chili sauce.

02:45:43.400 --> 02:45:44.968
I don't know where that
association came from,

02:45:44.968 --> 02:45:46.536
but I'm not losing pain,
'cause it's delicious.

02:45:46.536 --> 02:45:48.672
And we have a Turkish-style garlic sauce.

02:45:48.672 --> 02:45:49.940
I think this is kind of a nod to the

02:45:49.940 --> 02:45:52.275
Turkish-style kebab shops,
which are so popular in the UK.

02:45:52.275 --> 02:45:54.844
Drunk kebab is one of the
best pleasures in life.

02:45:54.844 --> 02:45:55.679
Speaking of drunk food,

02:45:55.679 --> 02:45:57.914
we also have Heinz curry sauce in the UK.

02:45:57.914 --> 02:45:58.915
You don't have to be drunk to enjoy it,

02:45:58.915 --> 02:45:59.749
but it does help.

02:45:59.749 --> 02:46:01.251
Cheesy chips and curry sauce

02:46:01.251 --> 02:46:03.186
is a big British hit.

02:46:03.186 --> 02:46:05.255
Next up, we have a
spiced pineapple chutney

02:46:05.255 --> 02:46:08.124
as well as a North
African-style spicy kebab sauce

02:46:08.124 --> 02:46:09.592
made with chili and onion.

02:46:09.592 --> 02:46:11.861
Why doesn't ketchup and mustard
come in the same bottle?

02:46:11.861 --> 02:46:13.897
We all scream to the heavens.

02:46:13.897 --> 02:46:16.032
Apparently, Heinz has a whole thing called

02:46:16.032 --> 02:46:18.034
sauces mixed by Heinz.

02:46:18.034 --> 02:46:20.036
'Cause God forbid you just do it yourself.

02:46:20.036 --> 02:46:21.204
Some of these, I mean.

02:46:21.204 --> 02:46:22.038
I didn't even know

02:46:22.038 --> 02:46:23.840
you wanted them together to begin with.

02:46:23.840 --> 02:46:25.375
Who knows how they figured this out?

02:46:25.375 --> 02:46:27.143
Honeyracha, what do you think that is?

02:46:27.143 --> 02:46:28.545
Surely, you watching at home

02:46:28.545 --> 02:46:30.613
understand the value of
having a bottle of Sriracha

02:46:30.613 --> 02:46:31.681
and a bottle of honey.

02:46:31.681 --> 02:46:33.183
The amount of times you mix them

02:46:33.183 --> 02:46:35.118
must pale in comparison,

02:46:35.118 --> 02:46:37.454
amount of times you
don't need to mix them.

02:46:37.454 --> 02:46:39.155
Actually, that's pretty good though.

02:46:39.155 --> 02:46:41.224
Then we got Mayochup.

02:46:41.224 --> 02:46:43.026
[Laughing]

02:46:43.026 --> 02:46:46.329
Yeah. Mayo and ketchup together at last.

02:46:49.165 --> 02:46:50.834
Oh God. Nope.

02:46:50.834 --> 02:46:52.369
Even by these standards, it's really bad.

02:46:52.369 --> 02:46:53.236
Up next,

02:46:54.537 --> 02:46:55.605
Kranch.

02:46:55.605 --> 02:46:56.439
Kranch.

02:46:56.439 --> 02:46:57.874
Just call it ranch and ketchup.

02:46:57.874 --> 02:46:59.943
Two things I never combine anyway.

02:46:59.943 --> 02:47:02.612
Oh, there's like chunks
of (censored) in it, too.

02:47:02.612 --> 02:47:04.714
Oh, this one. Okay.

02:47:04.714 --> 02:47:06.416
Both the ketchup connection ones.

02:47:06.416 --> 02:47:08.251
This tastes almost exactly like ranch

02:47:08.251 --> 02:47:10.086
and the ketchup mayo one, was it Mayochup?

02:47:10.086 --> 02:47:11.588
Taste just like mayo.

02:47:11.588 --> 02:47:13.390
Buffaranch.

02:47:13.390 --> 02:47:16.192
I'll concede, Buffalo and
ranch in the same thing,

02:47:16.192 --> 02:47:18.828
I understand this one.

02:47:18.828 --> 02:47:19.662
[Liquid squirting]

02:47:19.662 --> 02:47:21.164
Oh, that was the bottle, not me.

02:47:21.164 --> 02:47:21.998
[Liquid squirting]

02:47:21.998 --> 02:47:22.832
That was me.

02:47:25.568 --> 02:47:27.003
I went from hating this to loving it

02:47:27.003 --> 02:47:28.371
in like two seconds.

02:47:28.371 --> 02:47:30.807
This might be genius. I'm taking this one.

02:47:30.807 --> 02:47:31.841
This is actually pretty good.

02:47:31.841 --> 02:47:32.842
And this would go really good on

02:47:32.842 --> 02:47:35.612
any potatoes or fries.
Of course, on chicken.

02:47:35.612 --> 02:47:38.314
Oh my God, with popcorn
chicken, this would be ideal.

02:47:38.314 --> 02:47:40.316
Oh my God. Mayomust.

02:47:41.818 --> 02:47:42.652
This one works just fine.

02:47:42.652 --> 02:47:43.486
You know what it's like.

02:47:43.486 --> 02:47:45.188
It tastes like a creamy mustard.

02:47:45.188 --> 02:47:48.358
You can also get yourself
a Mayoracha sauce

02:47:48.358 --> 02:47:49.659
and, ugh, God,

02:47:49.659 --> 02:47:51.127
and this last one. Come on, guys.

02:47:51.127 --> 02:47:53.329
Sweet Ketchili sauce,

02:47:53.329 --> 02:47:56.766
which I'm assuming is sweet
ketchup and chili sauce.

02:47:56.766 --> 02:47:58.401
Ugh.

02:47:58.401 --> 02:48:00.437
We only have one premixed
sauce option in the UK

02:48:00.437 --> 02:48:03.006
and it's this, Saucy Sauce.

02:48:03.006 --> 02:48:04.207
I'm not sure why they've
gone with this name

02:48:04.207 --> 02:48:06.109
instead of Mayochup.
Because, as far as I can see,

02:48:06.109 --> 02:48:08.044
it is just mayo and ketchup combined.

02:48:08.044 --> 02:48:10.380
Another thing worth touching upon is

02:48:10.380 --> 02:48:12.549
HP versus A.1. sauce.

02:48:12.549 --> 02:48:15.785
The great HP, A.1. divide.

02:48:15.785 --> 02:48:16.986
I mean, there's literally like

02:48:16.986 --> 02:48:19.355
a rip through the Atlantic Ocean.

02:48:19.355 --> 02:48:21.291
It's a sauce that's meant to be on meat,

02:48:21.291 --> 02:48:23.460
mostly steak and hamburgers.

02:48:23.460 --> 02:48:25.728
In the UK, Heinz produces
another iconic condiment,

02:48:25.728 --> 02:48:26.863
HP sauce.

02:48:26.863 --> 02:48:28.164
It's a sauce, which is also designed

02:48:28.164 --> 02:48:29.766
to be enjoyed with meats like sausages

02:48:29.766 --> 02:48:31.101
or on your bacon sandwich.

02:48:31.101 --> 02:48:32.635
I'm more of a ketchup on
the bacon sandwich guy,

02:48:32.635 --> 02:48:33.603
but you do you.

02:48:33.603 --> 02:48:34.804
You have to understand,

02:48:34.804 --> 02:48:36.639
that when A.1. sauce dropped,

02:48:36.639 --> 02:48:39.375
it was that time in the '80s

02:48:39.375 --> 02:48:43.313
where marketing was so crazy effective.

02:48:43.313 --> 02:48:44.714
Obviously, there's no internet,

02:48:44.714 --> 02:48:47.016
so we all watched the
same five to 10 channels

02:48:47.016 --> 02:48:48.418
and you couldn't look at a TV

02:48:48.418 --> 02:48:50.386
without seeing an A1
steak sauce commercial,

02:48:50.386 --> 02:48:52.889
from like 1985 to 1989.

02:48:52.889 --> 02:48:54.724
Ta-da! A.1. steak sauce.

02:48:54.724 --> 02:48:56.493
And it was on the table,
and we just put it on

02:48:56.493 --> 02:48:58.528
and like licking the knife, like, oh yeah,

02:48:58.528 --> 02:48:59.996
A.1., look at us, we got A.1.

02:48:59.996 --> 02:49:01.831
This [censored] is nasty.

02:49:01.831 --> 02:49:04.134
I'm not nostalgic for it. I don't like it.

02:49:04.134 --> 02:49:06.669
I don't put sauces on meats like steak.

02:49:08.805 --> 02:49:10.473
Oh God.

02:49:10.473 --> 02:49:12.509
It's so tangy.

02:49:12.509 --> 02:49:13.776
HP sauce is better than this.

02:49:13.776 --> 02:49:15.044
I forgot to bring my bottle.

02:49:15.044 --> 02:49:15.879
To give you an idea of just how

02:49:15.879 --> 02:49:17.080
British this sauce is,

02:49:17.080 --> 02:49:19.082
HP actually stands for
Houses of Parliament,

02:49:19.082 --> 02:49:20.617
as you can maybe infer from the fact

02:49:20.617 --> 02:49:22.085
that they are right there on the label.

02:49:22.085 --> 02:49:24.487
HP sauce is part of a
subcategory of sauces in the UK,

02:49:24.487 --> 02:49:25.922
which are called brown sauces.

02:49:25.922 --> 02:49:27.157
While it might not sound very appetizing,

02:49:27.157 --> 02:49:28.558
it's really popular in the UK,

02:49:28.558 --> 02:49:29.459
as evidenced by the fact that

02:49:29.459 --> 02:49:30.727
it's not even the only brown sauce

02:49:30.727 --> 02:49:31.928
Kraft Heinz produces.

02:49:31.928 --> 02:49:34.764
We also have Daddy's brown sauce.

02:49:34.764 --> 02:49:36.866
Yes, this is a real thing.

02:49:36.866 --> 02:49:37.934
It wouldn't be a Heinz episode

02:49:37.934 --> 02:49:39.269
unless I talked about baked beans.

02:49:39.269 --> 02:49:40.403
In the UK, Heinz doesn't just

02:49:40.403 --> 02:49:41.471
manufacture condiments but also

02:49:41.471 --> 02:49:43.606
an enormous range of canned goods.

02:49:43.606 --> 02:49:45.875
The most iconic of these
is probably Heinz beans.

02:49:45.875 --> 02:49:47.544
I'd say Heinz is probably
the gold standard

02:49:47.544 --> 02:49:48.878
for baked beans in the UK.

02:49:48.878 --> 02:49:50.647
As you may know, British
people love to eat baked beans,

02:49:50.647 --> 02:49:52.949
with a range of dishes, including
a full English breakfast,

02:49:52.949 --> 02:49:55.318
sausage mash and beans, fish
fingers, chips, and beans.

02:49:55.318 --> 02:49:56.686
And, of course, beans on toast.

02:49:56.686 --> 02:49:57.587
Baked beans.

02:49:57.587 --> 02:49:58.988
So I'm going to say right off the bat,

02:49:58.988 --> 02:50:01.057
going to show my Americanness right now.

02:50:01.057 --> 02:50:03.560
Beans for breakfast sounds absurd.

02:50:03.560 --> 02:50:08.531
Now granted, we have
sausage, gravy, and potatoes

02:50:08.531 --> 02:50:10.066
and chicken-fried steak.

02:50:10.066 --> 02:50:12.468
So I know that our breakfasts
are very questionable.

02:50:12.468 --> 02:50:13.303
Beans for breakfas-

02:50:13.303 --> 02:50:14.637
Like beans, beans on toast?

02:50:14.637 --> 02:50:15.605
No, no, no, no.

02:50:15.605 --> 02:50:16.439
No, thank you.

02:50:16.439 --> 02:50:17.674
My only issue with Heinz beans

02:50:17.674 --> 02:50:19.876
is the stupid beans with the "z" spelling.

02:50:19.876 --> 02:50:21.511
I think it was part of
some marketing campaign

02:50:21.511 --> 02:50:23.313
from back in the '80s or '70s,

02:50:23.313 --> 02:50:24.547
and they just never let it go.

02:50:24.547 --> 02:50:26.349
But I will say, British
beans are generally

02:50:26.349 --> 02:50:28.051
served in quite a sweet tomato sauce,

02:50:28.051 --> 02:50:29.686
which does go with quite a few dishes.

02:50:29.686 --> 02:50:31.087
I feel like American beans are different.

02:50:31.087 --> 02:50:33.289
Yeah. Beans like here, like they're baked.

02:50:33.289 --> 02:50:35.858
They usually come in like a barbecue sauce

02:50:35.858 --> 02:50:37.360
or a barbecue-like sauce.

02:50:37.360 --> 02:50:39.095
You get them at barbecue places, right?

02:50:39.095 --> 02:50:40.597
Like picnic food, right?

02:50:40.597 --> 02:50:41.731
Beans aren't even the only canned goods

02:50:41.731 --> 02:50:43.132
that Heinz sells in the UK.

02:50:43.132 --> 02:50:46.569
They have a huge range of
soups, canned pastas, and more.

02:50:46.569 --> 02:50:48.538
Heinz soups are pretty
highly regarded in the UK.

02:50:48.538 --> 02:50:49.939
I like the big soups 'cause I feel like

02:50:49.939 --> 02:50:50.773
a standard can of soup

02:50:50.773 --> 02:50:52.041
isn't enough for an actual meal.

02:50:52.041 --> 02:50:53.710
But some of the other
flavors are really good.

02:50:53.710 --> 02:50:55.378
The classic tomato can't really be beaten.

02:50:55.378 --> 02:50:57.380
Heinz canned pastas don't have
quite the same reputation,

02:50:57.380 --> 02:51:00.516
but I will fight to the death
to defend the beef ravioli.

02:51:00.516 --> 02:51:01.985
I ate so much of that growing up,

02:51:01.985 --> 02:51:03.553
and I still have such a strong nostalgic

02:51:03.553 --> 02:51:05.455
attachment to it. That
if anyone criticizes it,

02:51:05.455 --> 02:51:06.623
you know where to find me.

02:51:06.623 --> 02:51:07.724
And there are even more food options

02:51:07.724 --> 02:51:09.592
from Heinz in the UK, including jars of

02:51:09.592 --> 02:51:11.127
pasta sauce, salad dressings, and

02:51:11.127 --> 02:51:12.528
veggie burger options.

02:51:12.528 --> 02:51:14.330
Heinz covers so many
product areas in the UK

02:51:14.330 --> 02:51:16.733
that they even have a
huge range of baby foods.

02:51:16.733 --> 02:51:18.935
I picked up this peach,
mango, banana, and apple one,

02:51:18.935 --> 02:51:19.969
because it sounds delicious,

02:51:19.969 --> 02:51:21.437
and I am technically older than 4 months,

02:51:21.437 --> 02:51:23.606
so I think I can eat this.

02:51:27.944 --> 02:51:30.947
Here's everything in
Heinz ketchup in the US.

02:51:30.947 --> 02:51:33.182
Tomato concentrate from red, red tomatoes;

02:51:33.182 --> 02:51:35.485
distilled vinegar;
high-fructose corn syrup;

02:51:35.485 --> 02:51:40.490
corn syrup; salt; spice;
onion powder; natural flavor.

02:51:40.490 --> 02:51:41.691
Here's everything on the ingredients list

02:51:41.691 --> 02:51:42.892
for ketchup in the UK.

02:51:42.892 --> 02:51:45.928
Tomatoes, spirit vinegar, sugar, salt,

02:51:45.928 --> 02:51:49.032
spice and herb extracts,
contains celery and spice.

02:51:49.032 --> 02:51:50.066
There are a few obvious differences

02:51:50.066 --> 02:51:51.367
between the two lists.

02:51:51.367 --> 02:51:53.169
The spices listed are slightly different.

02:51:53.169 --> 02:51:56.272
With ours explicitly
containing onion powder.

02:51:56.272 --> 02:51:57.273
That's not to say the UK's

02:51:57.273 --> 02:51:58.574
doesn't also contain onion powder,

02:51:58.574 --> 02:51:59.876
as it may just fall under the

02:51:59.876 --> 02:52:02.312
spice and herb extract
subcategory in the UK.

02:52:02.312 --> 02:52:03.980
Our label also explicitly lists celery

02:52:03.980 --> 02:52:04.814
as an ingredient,

02:52:04.814 --> 02:52:06.449
largely for allergen purposes.

02:52:06.449 --> 02:52:08.217
In the US, our ketchup is sweetened

02:52:08.217 --> 02:52:10.720
with both corn syrup and
high-fructose corn syrup,

02:52:10.720 --> 02:52:13.356
while the UK, they just
sweeten theirs with sugar.

02:52:13.356 --> 02:52:15.525
Americans put corn syrup
in absolutely everything

02:52:15.525 --> 02:52:17.226
because it's cheaper for
food producers to use

02:52:17.226 --> 02:52:18.461
when compared to real sugar.

02:52:18.461 --> 02:52:19.929
That's because corn farmers in the US

02:52:19.929 --> 02:52:22.031
receive huge subsidies
from the government,

02:52:22.031 --> 02:52:25.268
more than 116 billion since 1995,

02:52:25.268 --> 02:52:27.337
leading to massive corn production.

02:52:27.337 --> 02:52:28.171
We also have tariffs,

02:52:28.171 --> 02:52:30.340
which make it more expensive
to import real sugar.

02:52:30.340 --> 02:52:31.474
There isn't really a
health difference when

02:52:31.474 --> 02:52:33.676
comparing the consumption
of sugar and corn syrup,

02:52:33.676 --> 02:52:35.478
but there can be a
discernible taste difference.

02:52:35.478 --> 02:52:37.146
For example, American Coca-Cola

02:52:37.146 --> 02:52:38.715
is flavored with high-fructose corn syrup

02:52:38.715 --> 02:52:40.616
while Mexican Coke is flavored with sugar.

02:52:40.616 --> 02:52:41.884
When comparing the taste of the two,

02:52:41.884 --> 02:52:43.553
some people said that the American version

02:52:43.553 --> 02:52:45.088
was an even sweeter taste

02:52:45.088 --> 02:52:46.889
but also had a slightly chemical flavor.

02:52:46.889 --> 02:52:48.224
Are there any other ingredients

02:52:48.224 --> 02:52:51.027
to look out for across
Heinz product range?

02:52:51.027 --> 02:52:53.529
In the US, we have pickles that contain

02:52:53.529 --> 02:52:55.932
polysorbate 80, a chemical used in foods

02:52:55.932 --> 02:52:56.999
like pickles and ice cream

02:52:56.999 --> 02:52:59.335
as an emulsifier and a solubilizer.

02:52:59.335 --> 02:53:03.172
A solubilizer, solubilizer?

02:53:03.172 --> 02:53:04.507
Solubilizer.

02:53:04.507 --> 02:53:07.143
The bad news is that
studies have suggested

02:53:07.143 --> 02:53:09.879
it can cause colon cancer in mice.

02:53:09.879 --> 02:53:14.217
You know what that means.
Give me them dancing rats.

02:53:16.285 --> 02:53:18.054
Also in the US, our Heinz colors,

02:53:18.054 --> 02:53:20.590
it's sausage gravy with titanium dioxide.

02:53:20.590 --> 02:53:22.692
This is a white dye most commonly used

02:53:22.692 --> 02:53:24.460
in paints and varnishes.

02:53:24.460 --> 02:53:25.328
Delicious.

02:53:25.328 --> 02:53:27.330
Its use in food is controversial.

02:53:27.330 --> 02:53:29.499
Studies show it has
harmful effects on animals

02:53:29.499 --> 02:53:30.666
during scientific tests, leading the

02:53:30.666 --> 02:53:34.404
European Union to ban
it from use in foods,

02:53:34.404 --> 02:53:36.939
starting in August of 2022.

02:53:36.939 --> 02:53:37.807
Due to Brexit however,

02:53:37.807 --> 02:53:40.376
the UK is no longer bound
by EU rules about food.

02:53:40.376 --> 02:53:41.711
Unfortunately, for Brits and Americans,

02:53:41.711 --> 02:53:43.312
our respective food-safety authorities

02:53:43.312 --> 02:53:45.348
have actually disagreed
with the EU ruling.

02:53:45.348 --> 02:53:46.949
So we may still see titanium dioxide

02:53:46.949 --> 02:53:48.017
popping up in our food.

02:53:50.453 --> 02:53:52.555
Here's everything in 100
grams of original ketchup

02:53:52.555 --> 02:53:53.656
in the UK.

02:53:53.656 --> 02:53:56.526
102 calories, 0.1 grams of total fat,

02:53:56.526 --> 02:53:58.261
of which 0 grams are saturates,

02:53:58.261 --> 02:54:01.731
23.2 grams of carbs, of
which 22.8 grams are sugars,

02:54:01.731 --> 02:54:04.100
and 720 milligrams of sodium.

02:54:04.100 --> 02:54:04.934
Here's everything in 100 grams

02:54:04.934 --> 02:54:07.069
of ketchup in the US.

02:54:07.069 --> 02:54:11.207
117 calories, no fat, 29.4 carbs.

02:54:11.207 --> 02:54:14.477
And, of that, 23.5 grams of that is sugar.

02:54:14.477 --> 02:54:17.847
And the sodium is 941 milligrams.

02:54:17.847 --> 02:54:19.916
Other than the trace amounts
of fat in the UK ketchup,

02:54:19.916 --> 02:54:21.884
the US stats are higher in every category.

02:54:21.884 --> 02:54:23.820
Now these stats are for
100 grams of ketchup,

02:54:23.820 --> 02:54:26.055
which is roughly five times a recommended

02:54:26.055 --> 02:54:27.890
serving size, which is 1 tablespoon.

02:54:27.890 --> 02:54:30.827
That's still a surprising
amount of sugar and sodium.

02:54:30.827 --> 02:54:33.429
Just 100 grams of ketchup in the US

02:54:33.429 --> 02:54:35.631
contain almost half your
daily sugar allowance

02:54:35.631 --> 02:54:38.167
as well as 40% of your daily sodium.

02:54:38.167 --> 02:54:39.702
It's also worth checking
out the serving size,

02:54:39.702 --> 02:54:41.838
because companies have a habit
of underestimating these.

02:54:41.838 --> 02:54:43.906
Let's bring out the serving
size we measured earlier.

02:54:43.906 --> 02:54:45.141
I still think that's a little bit smaller

02:54:45.141 --> 02:54:46.309
than what I'd normally add to my plate.

02:54:46.309 --> 02:54:47.343
So I'm going to add some more to it

02:54:47.343 --> 02:54:48.311
and then figure out the stats for

02:54:48.311 --> 02:54:50.346
what I think is a realistic serving size.

02:54:50.346 --> 02:54:51.180
I don't think that's unrealistic.

02:54:51.180 --> 02:54:52.548
Judge me if you want.

02:54:52.548 --> 02:54:53.649
That's actually more than double

02:54:53.649 --> 02:54:54.717
the recommended serving size, coming in

02:54:54.717 --> 02:54:57.420
at around 31 grams. Let's figure it out.

02:54:57.420 --> 02:55:00.223
Okay. So if I had fries, I'd probably go.

02:55:00.223 --> 02:55:02.124
Not that long, right?

02:55:02.124 --> 02:55:03.593
This is 20 grams.

02:55:03.593 --> 02:55:04.827
Ahh, that doesn't seem so bad.

02:55:04.827 --> 02:55:08.064
And also, I don't think...I
don't think I'm eating all that.

02:55:08.064 --> 02:55:09.932
I don't think all of this
is getting in my body.

02:55:09.932 --> 02:55:11.300
I think this would be smeared around.

02:55:11.300 --> 02:55:13.569
And so here in America, not concerned.

02:55:14.670 --> 02:55:16.072
This one's for you, Papa.

02:55:16.072 --> 02:55:19.141
I like pickled onion, I like Piccalilli.

02:55:19.141 --> 02:55:20.443
Pickled cabbage is all right

02:55:20.443 --> 02:55:21.277
with bubble and squeak on a Friday night.

02:55:22.111 --> 02:55:24.213
I like tomatoes, but what I do prefer

02:55:24.213 --> 02:55:25.748
is a little bit of I
come, you come, we come,

02:55:25.748 --> 02:55:27.216
a little bit of cucumber. Oy!

02:55:28.084 --> 02:55:29.218
Ta-da.

02:55:29.218 --> 02:55:31.120
Imagine when that dropped in like 1920.

02:55:31.120 --> 02:55:32.455
People would've lost their minds.

